-- Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab) textbook MCQs, chapter 1 (Measurements) PHY-0447 to PHY-0503, chapter 2 (Force and Motion) PHY-0504 to PHY-0567, chapter 3 (Circular and Rotational Motion) PHY-0568 to PHY-0623, chapter 4 (Work, Energy and Power) PHY-0624 to PHY-0667, chapter 5 (Solids and Fluid Dynamics) PHY-0668 to PHY-0723, chapter 6 (Heat and Thermodynamics) PHY-0724 to PHY-0776, chapter 7 (Waves and Vibrations) PHY-0777 to PHY-0834, chapter 8 (Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves) PHY-0835 to PHY-0880, chapter 9 (Electrostatics and Current Electricity) PHY-0881 to PHY-0945, chapter 10 (Electromagnetism) PHY-0946 to PHY-0988, chapter 11 (Special Theory of Relativity) PHY-0989 to PHY-1016, chapter 12 (Nuclear and Particle Physics) PHY-1017 to PHY-1063.
-- Original questions in the bank's template (stem, four options, key, why the key is right, why each other option is
-- wrong) with the source page in the textbook. questions.chapter is the MDCAT chapter (or, for questions off the MDCAT
-- syllabus, the AKU chapter); FSc Part 1 lists every question in bank_questions under its textbook chapter. Not in the
-- free set. INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the file safe to run twice; if the site owner's admin account doesn't exist (a fresh
-- local database) nothing is inserted.

-- Not every topic is on every test. Checked against the PMDC MDCAT 2025 uniform curriculum (26-05-2025) and the AKU
-- admission test Physics syllabus. PMDC has no measurements, elasticity, physical optics, gravitational waves,
-- relativity or particle physics unit, so those questions get questions.mdcat = 0 (out of the MDCAT and NUMS banks and
-- the mocks). AKU's syllabus has all of them except gravitational waves, which stay FSc Part 1 only.
-- The NUMS bank follows the NUMS MDCAT 2026 syllabus: it has no vectors and equilibrium, fluid dynamics or alternating
-- current unit, and within its units it leaves out subtopics its list does not name (for example moment of inertia,
-- satellites, kinetic theory, the second law, beats, diffraction, Gauss's law, Kirchhoff's rules, bridges, the velocity
-- selector). Those questions are simply not added to the NUMS bank.

-- Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0447', 'Measurements', 'Base and derived quantities', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'Which of the following is a base quantity in the SI?', '["Electric charge", "Force", "Pressure", "Electric current"]', 3, 'The book lists seven base quantities: length, mass, time, temperature, electric current, luminous intensity and amount of substance. Electric current (ampere) is one of them.', '{"0": "Charge is derived: 1 C = 1 A s, so it depends on current and time.", "1": "Force is a derived quantity (kg m s⁻²).", "2": "Pressure is derived from force and area (N m⁻²)."}'),
  ('PHY-0448', 'Measurements', 'Base and derived quantities', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'What are the two steps involved in measuring a base quantity, according to the book?', '["Choosing a unit, then converting it into derived units", "Defining the quantity in terms of other quantities, then calculating it", "Estimating the value, then rounding it to significant figures", "Choosing a standard, then setting up a procedure to compare the quantity with that standard"]', 3, 'The book says measuring a base quantity involves first choosing a standard and second establishing a procedure for comparing the quantity with the standard, so that a number and a unit are obtained.', '{"0": "Conversion into derived units is not part of measuring a base quantity.", "1": "Base quantities are not defined in terms of other quantities; that describes derived quantities.", "2": "Rounding is about recording a result, not the two steps of measurement the book names."}'),
  ('PHY-0449', 'Measurements', 'Base and derived quantities', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'Which statement about base quantities is correct?', '["They are defined in terms of length, mass and time only.", "They always have dimensionless units.", "They include velocity and acceleration.", "They are independent quantities in terms of which other quantities are defined."]', 3, 'Base quantities are not defined in terms of other physical quantities; they are the independent quantities from which the others (velocity, force, etc.) are built.', '{"0": "Length, mass and time are themselves base quantities; base quantities are not defined through other quantities.", "1": "Base quantities such as mass (kg) and length (m) have units with dimensions.", "2": "Velocity and acceleration are the book''s examples of derived quantities."}'),
  ('PHY-0450', 'Measurements', 'SI base units', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 2 (§1.2)', 'How many base units does the System International (SI) have?', '["Five", "Seven", "Six", "Nine"]', 1, 'The SI has seven base units: metre, kilogram, second, kelvin, ampere, candela and mole.', '{"0": "Five is the number of dimensions the book uses ([M], [L], [T], [A], [θ]), not the number of SI base units.", "2": "Six leaves out one of length, mass, time, temperature, current, luminous intensity or amount of substance.", "3": "Nine would wrongly include supplementary or derived units such as the radian and steradian."}'),
  ('PHY-0451', 'Measurements', 'SI base units', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 2 (§1.2)', 'In which year did an international committee agree on the set of definitions and standards called the System International (SI)?', '["1875", "1905", "1960", "1995"]', 2, 'The book states that in 1960 an international committee agreed on the definitions and standards, and the system set up was called the System International (SI).', '{"0": "1875 is not the year the book gives for the SI.", "1": "1905 is the year of Einstein''s special relativity papers, not the SI.", "3": "1995 is when the radian and steradian were put in the list of derived units."}'),
  ('PHY-0452', 'Measurements', 'SI base units', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 2 (Table 1.1)', 'Which pair correctly matches a base quantity with its SI unit?', '["Thermodynamic temperature – degree Celsius", "Luminous intensity – candela", "Amount of substance – kilogram", "Electric current – coulomb"]', 1, 'Table 1.1 gives the candela (cd) as the SI unit of luminous intensity.', '{"0": "The SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature is the kelvin.", "2": "Amount of substance is measured in moles; the kilogram is the unit of mass.", "3": "The base unit of current is the ampere; the coulomb is a derived unit of charge."}'),
  ('PHY-0453', 'Measurements', 'Derived units', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 3 (Table 1.2)', 'Which of these is the correct expression of the pascal in SI base units?', '["kg m s⁻²", "kg m² s⁻²", "kg m⁻¹ s⁻²", "kg m⁻² s⁻²"]', 2, 'Pressure = force ÷ area, so 1 Pa = 1 N m⁻² = (kg m s⁻²)(m⁻²) = kg m⁻¹ s⁻².', '{"0": "kg m s⁻² is the newton, the unit of force.", "1": "kg m² s⁻² is the joule, the unit of work or energy.", "3": "Pressure is force/area: (kg m s⁻²)/m² = kg m⁻¹ s⁻², not kg m⁻² s⁻²."}'),
  ('PHY-0454', 'Measurements', 'Derived units', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 3 (Table 1.2)', 'The watt expressed in SI base units is:', '["kg m² s⁻²", "kg m s⁻³", "kg m² s⁻¹", "kg m² s⁻³"]', 3, '1 W = 1 J s⁻¹ = (kg m² s⁻²)(s⁻¹) = kg m² s⁻³.', '{"0": "kg m² s⁻² is the joule; power is energy per second, which adds another s⁻¹.", "1": "The metre must be squared because energy contains m².", "2": "This is the unit of angular momentum (J s), not power."}'),
  ('PHY-0455', 'Measurements', 'Derived units', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 3 (§1.2)', 'How are the radian and steradian treated in the SI, according to the book?', '["As base units of angle", "As dimensionless derived units, listed since 1995", "As non-SI units that must not be used", "As supplementary units with the dimension of length"]', 1, 'Table 1.2 lists the radian (plane angle) and steradian (solid angle) as derived units that are dimensionless, included in the derived units since 1995.', '{"0": "Angle is not one of the seven base quantities.", "2": "They are SI derived units.", "3": "A ratio of lengths (or of area to area) has no dimension."}'),
  ('PHY-0456', 'Measurements', 'Derived units', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 3 (Table 1.2)', 'The coulomb, unit of electric charge, can be written in base units as:', '["A s⁻¹", "J V", "A s", "kg A s⁻²"]', 2, 'Since Q = It, 1 C = 1 A × 1 s = 1 A s.', '{"0": "Charge = current × time, so the second multiplies, it does not divide.", "1": "A coulomb is J/V, not J × V, and those are not base units.", "3": "No mass appears in the unit of charge."}'),
  ('PHY-0457', 'Measurements', 'Units allowed with SI', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 3 (§1.2)', 'The book lists units that the SI permits alongside base and derived units. Which pair is described as the two non-metric scientific units?', '["The litre and the tonne", "The nautical mile and the knot", "The angstrom and the barn", "The atomic mass unit and the electron volt"]', 3, 'The book says the two non-metric scientific units permitted with the SI are the atomic mass unit (u) and the electron volt (eV).', '{"0": "The book calls these the two metric units common in ordinary life.", "1": "These are units traditionally used at sea and in meteorology.", "2": "These are named as units used in physics and astronomy, not the two non-metric scientific units."}'),
  ('PHY-0458', 'Measurements', 'Units allowed with SI', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 3 (§1.2)', 'Which unit does the book describe as a logarithmic unit permitted for use with the SI?', '["The bar", "The hectare", "The arcsecond", "The bel (and the decibel)"]', 3, 'Among the additional units, the book lists the logarithmic unit bel and its multiples such as the decibel.', '{"0": "The bar is a unit of pressure.", "1": "The hectare is a unit of land area.", "2": "The arcsecond is a traditional angle unit."}'),
  ('PHY-0459', 'Measurements', 'Scientific notation', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 3 (§1.2)', 'How should 0.000508 be written in scientific notation?', '["0.508 × 10⁻³", "5.08 × 10⁻⁴", "5.08 × 10⁻³", "50.8 × 10⁻⁵"]', 1, 'Scientific notation has exactly one non-zero digit before the decimal point: 0.000508 = 5.08 × 10⁻⁴.', '{"0": "The digit left of the decimal must be non-zero.", "2": "5.08 × 10⁻³ = 0.00508, ten times too large.", "3": "Scientific notation keeps only one non-zero digit to the left of the decimal point."}'),
  ('PHY-0460', 'Measurements', 'Prefixes', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 4 (Table 1.3)', 'Which prefix stands for the factor 10⁻¹⁵?', '["femto (f)", "pico (p)", "atto (a)", "nano (n)"]', 0, 'Table 1.3 gives femto, symbol f, as 10⁻¹⁵.', '{"1": "pico is 10⁻¹².", "2": "atto is 10⁻¹⁸.", "3": "nano is 10⁻⁹."}'),
  ('PHY-0461', 'Measurements', 'Prefixes', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 4 (Table 1.3)', 'The prefix "peta" (P) represents:', '["10¹⁵", "10¹²", "10¹⁸", "10⁹"]', 0, 'Table 1.3 lists peta, symbol P, as 10¹⁵.', '{"1": "10¹² is tera (T).", "2": "10¹⁸ is exa (E).", "3": "10⁹ is giga (G)."}'),
  ('PHY-0462', 'Measurements', 'Prefixes', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 4 (§1.2 Prefixes)', 'Most SI prefixes change the order of magnitude in steps of 1000. Which group lists the four prefixes the book says SI also includes to keep older usage?', '["milli, micro, nano and pico", "centi, deci, deca and hecto", "kilo, mega, giga and tera", "femto, atto, peta and exa"]', 1, 'The book says SI also includes centi (10⁻²), deci (10⁻¹), deca (10¹) and hecto (10²) to accommodate usage established before SI.', '{"0": "These are all steps-of-1000 prefixes.", "2": "These are all steps-of-1000 prefixes.", "3": "These are also steps-of-1000 prefixes."}'),
  ('PHY-0463', 'Measurements', 'SI conventions', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 4 (§1.2 Conventions)', 'Which of these is written correctly according to the SI conventions in the book?', '["25 kg", "25 Kg.", "25 kgs", "25kg"]', 0, 'SI symbols are lower case (except those named after scientists), take no plural, have no full stop, and are separated from the number by a space: 25 kg.', '{"1": "The symbol for kilo is lower-case k, and no full stop goes after a symbol except at the end of a sentence.", "2": "Symbols do not take a plural form.", "3": "A space must be left between the number and the symbol."}'),
  ('PHY-0464', 'Measurements', 'SI conventions', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 4 (§1.2 Conventions)', 'Which unit name breaks the rule "the full name of a unit does not begin with a capital letter"?', '["newton", "pascal", "Celsius", "metre"]', 2, 'The book''s rule is that full unit names are written in lower case, with Celsius as the exception.', '{"0": "newton is written in lower case even though it is named after a scientist.", "1": "pascal is written in lower case.", "3": "metre is written in lower case."}'),
  ('PHY-0465', 'Measurements', 'SI conventions', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 4 (§1.2 Conventions)', 'Which unit symbol is the book''s stated exception to the rule that unit symbols are written in lower case (apart from those named after scientists)?', '["m for metre", "s for second", "kg for kilogram", "L for litre"]', 3, 'Symbols appear in lower case, e.g. m and s; the exception named is L for litre. Symbols named after scientists (N, Pa, W) are capitalised by a separate rule.', '{"0": "m is lower case, following the rule.", "1": "s is lower case, following the rule.", "2": "kg is lower case, following the rule."}'),
  ('PHY-0466', 'Measurements', 'SI conventions', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 5 (§1.2 Conventions)', 'Following the SI rule against compound prefixes, how should 1 μμF be written?', '["1 pF", "1 nF", "1 mμF", "1 fF"]', 0, 'Compound prefixes are not allowed; μμ = 10⁻¹² = pico, so 1 μμF is written 1 pF.', '{"1": "μμ = 10⁻⁶ × 10⁻⁶ = 10⁻¹², which is pico, not nano (10⁻⁹).", "2": "That is still a compound prefix, which is not allowed.", "3": "femto is 10⁻¹⁵, not 10⁻¹²."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0467', 'Measurements', 'SI conventions', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 5 (§1.2 Conventions)', 'When a prefixed unit is raised to a power, the power applies to the whole multiple. What is 1 cm³ in m³?', '["1 × 10⁻⁶ m³", "1 × 10⁻² m³", "1 × 10⁻³ m³", "1 × 10⁻⁹ m³"]', 0, '1 cm³ = (10⁻² m)³ = 10⁻⁶ m³, because the cube applies to the prefix as well as the metre.', '{"1": "This applies the power to the base unit only, not to the prefix.", "2": "(10⁻² m)³ = 10⁻⁶ m³, not 10⁻³ m³.", "3": "10⁻⁹ m³ would be 1 mm³."}'),
  ('PHY-0468', 'Measurements', 'SI conventions', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 5 (§1.2 Conventions)', 'Which of these units is NOT allowed in the SI, according to the book?', '["dyne", "electron volt", "litre", "hectare"]', 0, 'The book says the SI does not allow former CGS units such as the dyne, erg, gauss, poise and torr.', '{"1": "The electron volt is one of the additional units the SI permits.", "2": "The litre is permitted as a common metric unit.", "3": "The hectare is permitted as a unit of land area."}'),
  ('PHY-0469', 'Measurements', 'SI conventions', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 5 (§1.2 Conventions)', 'How does the book recommend writing the unit of speed?', '["m/s", "m s⁻¹", "metre/s", "m/sec"]', 1, 'The conventions say: use negative index notation (m s⁻¹) instead of the solidus (m/s), and don''t mix names with symbols.', '{"0": "The book asks for negative index notation instead of the solidus.", "2": "Symbols and names must not be mixed in one expression.", "3": "\"sec\" is not the SI symbol, and the solidus should be avoided."}'),
  ('PHY-0470', 'Measurements', 'Uncertainty', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 5 (§1.3)', 'Why does every measured quantity have some uncertainty?', '["Physical quantities keep changing while they are measured.", "Only digital instruments give exact readings.", "Every instrument is calibrated only to a certain smallest division, which limits how finely it can read.", "Measurements are always recorded in the wrong units."]', 2, 'The book explains that each instrument has a smallest division (graduation), so a reading can only be taken to the nearest graduation; this puts a limit on accuracy and gives every measurement an uncertainty.', '{"0": "Most quantities measured (e.g. a book''s length) are steady; the limit comes from the instrument.", "1": "Digital instruments also have uncertainty, shown by the fluctuating last digit.", "3": "Units do not cause uncertainty."}'),
  ('PHY-0471', 'Measurements', 'Uncertainty', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 5 (§1.3)', 'One end of a rod lines up with the 20.0 cm mark of a millimetre scale and the other with 46.5 cm. Using ±0.05 cm for each reading, the length should be recorded as:', '["(26.5 ± 0.05) cm", "(26.5 ± 0.0) cm", "(66.5 ± 0.1) cm", "(26.5 ± 0.1) cm"]', 3, 'Length = 46.5 − 20.0 = 26.5 cm. For a difference, absolute uncertainties add: ±(0.05 + 0.05) = ±0.1 cm, which equals the least count of the metre rule.', '{"0": "Two readings are subtracted, so their uncertainties add: 0.05 + 0.05 = 0.1 cm.", "1": "Uncertainties do not cancel when readings are subtracted.", "2": "The length is the difference of the readings, not their sum."}'),
  ('PHY-0472', 'Measurements', 'Uncertainty', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 5 (§1.3)', 'A length is measured as 40.0 cm with an absolute uncertainty of ±0.2 cm. Its percentage uncertainty is:', '["0.2%", "0.5%", "2%", "5%"]', 1, 'Percentage uncertainty = (absolute uncertainty ÷ measured value) × 100% = (0.2/40.0) × 100% = 0.5%.', '{"0": "0.2 is the absolute uncertainty in cm, not a percentage.", "2": "This would need an uncertainty of 0.8 cm.", "3": "0.2/40 = 0.005, which is 0.5%, not 5%."}'),
  ('PHY-0473', 'Measurements', 'Uncertainty in digital instruments', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 6 (§1.3)', 'While reading a digital balance, the last digit keeps fluctuating by about 5. According to the book, what does this most likely mean?', '["Some outside factor, such as air currents, is affecting the reading, and that last digit may not really be significant.", "The last digit should be recorded because it is the most precise one.", "The balance has no uncertainty at all.", "The reading should be multiplied by the fluctuation."]', 0, 'If the last digit fluctuates by 1 or 2 you write it down; if it fluctuates by more than about 2, the reading may be influenced by factors such as air currents and the displayed digit may not be significant.', '{"1": "The book says only fluctuations of 1 or 2 are recorded; a large fluctuation means the digit may not be significant.", "2": "Fluctuations show there is uncertainty.", "3": "There is no such rule."}'),
  ('PHY-0474', 'Measurements', 'Significant figures', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 6 (§1.4)', 'In the book''s definition, significant figures in a measurement are:', '["all the digits shown on a calculator display", "the accurately known digits plus the first doubtful (estimated) digit", "only the digits that are not zero", "the digits after the decimal point only"]', 1, 'The book defines significant figures as the accurately known digits and the first estimated or doubtful digit of a measurement.', '{"0": "Calculator digits beyond the instrument''s limit are not significant and must be rounded off.", "2": "Some zeros are significant (e.g. in 3.570).", "3": "Digits before the decimal point can also be significant."}'),
  ('PHY-0475', 'Measurements', 'Significant figures', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 6 (§1.4)', 'The same object is weighed as 3.145 g on an electronic balance and as 3.1 g on a lever balance. What does the book conclude?', '["3.1 g is more accurate because it has fewer digits.", "Both are equally accurate because they are the same object.", "3.145 g is more accurate, showing a more precise instrument was used.", "The two readings cannot both be correct."]', 2, 'The book says a reported mass of 3.145 g is more accurate than 3.1 g, reflecting a better, more precise instrument; more significant figures mean better accuracy.', '{"0": "Fewer significant figures means a coarser instrument and a less accurate value.", "1": "The number of significant figures shows the quality of the instrument.", "3": "Both are correct to the precision of their instruments."}'),
  ('PHY-0476', 'Measurements', 'Counting significant figures', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 7 (§1.4 (i))', 'How many significant figures are there in 0.04060 m?', '["3", "4", "5", "6"]', 1, 'Leading zeros (0.0) are not significant; the zero between 4 and 6 is significant; the trailing zero in a decimal fraction is significant. So 4, 0, 6, 0 → 4 significant figures.', '{"0": "The final zero after the 6, in a decimal fraction, is significant.", "2": "The zeros before the 4 are not significant.", "3": "Leading zeros are never significant."}'),
  ('PHY-0477', 'Measurements', 'Counting significant figures', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 7 (§1.4 (i))', 'A mass is recorded as 6000 kg using a scale whose least count is 10 kg. Written in scientific notation with the correct number of significant figures, it is:', '["6.00 × 10³ kg", "6 × 10³ kg", "6.000 × 10³ kg", "6.0 × 10³ kg"]', 0, 'For integers like 6000, the number of significant zeros depends on the instrument. A least count of 10 kg makes the hundreds and tens digits reliable: 6.00 × 10³ kg (3 s.f.).', '{"1": "With a 10 kg least count the tens digit is known, so there are three significant figures, not one.", "2": "Four significant figures would need a least count of 1 kg.", "3": "Two significant figures would match a least count of 100 kg."}'),
  ('PHY-0478', 'Measurements', 'Counting significant figures', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 7 (§1.4 (i))', 'How many significant figures does 4.050 × 10⁻³ kg have?', '["2", "3", "4", "7"]', 2, 'In scientific notation, all figures other than the power of ten are significant: 4, 0, 5, 0 → 4 significant figures.', '{"0": "The zero between 4 and 5 is significant.", "1": "The trailing zero after 5 in 4.050 is significant.", "3": "The power of ten does not add significant figures."}'),
  ('PHY-0479', 'Measurements', 'Rounding off', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 7 (§1.4 (ii))', 'Using the book''s rounding rule, what is 27.65 rounded to three significant figures?', '["27.5", "27.6", "27.7", "28.0"]', 1, 'The book''s rule: if the digit dropped is 5, the previous digit is increased by one if it is odd and kept as it is if it is even. Here the retained digit 6 is even, so 27.65 → 27.6.', '{"0": "Rounding never lowers the retained digit.", "2": "When the dropped digit is exactly 5, the retained digit is increased only if it is odd; 6 is even.", "3": "That is two significant figures shown as three, and it is the wrong value."}'),
  ('PHY-0480', 'Measurements', 'Rounding off', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 7 (§1.4 (ii))', 'Using the book''s rounding rule, what is 18.35 rounded to three significant figures?', '["18.0", "18.3", "18.4", "18.5"]', 2, 'The digit dropped is 5 and the retained digit 3 is odd, so it is increased by one: 18.35 → 18.4.', '{"0": "Rounding to three significant figures keeps the 3 or raises it.", "1": "The retained digit 3 is odd, so it must be increased by one when a 5 is dropped.", "3": "Only the last retained digit changes, by one."}'),
  ('PHY-0481', 'Measurements', 'Multiplying and dividing', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 7 (§1.4 (ii))', 'A calculator gives 3.6 × 2.417 = 8.7012. Written to the correct number of significant figures, the answer is:', '["8.7", "8.70", "8.701", "9"]', 0, 'In multiplication or division, keep as many significant figures as the least accurate factor. 3.6 has two, so 8.7012 → 8.7.', '{"1": "The least accurate factor (3.6) has only two significant figures.", "2": "The answer cannot have more significant figures than the least accurate factor.", "3": "This rounds to one significant figure; two are allowed."}'),
  ('PHY-0482', 'Measurements', 'Adding and subtracting', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 8 (§1.4 (iii))', 'What is 15.2 m + 3.481 m + 0.06 m to the appropriate precision?', '["18.7 m", "18.74 m", "18.741 m", "19 m"]', 0, 'Sum = 18.741 m. In addition, the number of decimal places in the answer equals the smallest number of decimal places in the terms: 15.2 has one, so the answer is 18.7 m.', '{"1": "The answer must have as many decimal places as the least precise term (15.2, one decimal place).", "2": "This keeps all the calculator digits.", "3": "This rounds too far; one decimal place is allowed."}'),
  ('PHY-0483', 'Measurements', 'Adding and subtracting', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 11 (Example 1.2)', 'A box of mass 2.46 kg (lever balance) has a 25.12 g weight added (beam balance). Its total mass to appropriate precision is:', '["2.485 kg", "2.48512 kg", "2.49 kg", "27.58 kg"]', 2, '2.46 + 0.02512 = 2.48512 kg. The least precise value, 2.46 kg, has two decimal places, so the total is 2.49 kg.', '{"0": "2.46 kg has only two decimal places, so the sum must be rounded to two.", "1": "This keeps more decimal places than the least precise mass allows.", "3": "The grams must first be converted to kilograms (25.12 g = 0.02512 kg)."}'),
  ('PHY-0484', 'Measurements', 'Limitations of significant figures', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 8 (§1.4)', 'What is the main limitation of significant figures stated in the book?', '["They cannot be used with numbers written in scientific notation.", "They always overestimate the total uncertainty.", "They apply only to digital instruments.", "They deal only with the uncertainty of reading the scale, while real uncertainty also includes personal and systematic errors."]', 3, 'Significant figures deal with only one source of uncertainty, the scale reading. Real experiments also have personal and hidden systematic errors, so the total uncertainty may be larger than the significant figures suggest.', '{"0": "Scientific notation is the best way to show significant figures.", "1": "The total uncertainty may be more, not less, than significant figures suggest.", "2": "They apply to any measurement."}'),
  ('PHY-0485', 'Measurements', 'Precision and accuracy', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 8 (§1.5)', 'According to the book, the precision of a measurement is determined by:', '["the least count (absolute uncertainty) of the instrument", "the percentage uncertainty of the measurement", "the size of the quantity being measured", "the number of readings averaged"]', 0, 'Precision is set by the instrument: the smaller the least count (absolute uncertainty), the more precise the measurement.', '{"1": "Percentage (fractional) uncertainty expresses accuracy.", "2": "The quantity''s size affects accuracy, not the instrument''s precision.", "3": "The book ties precision to the instrument used."}'),
  ('PHY-0486', 'Measurements', 'Precision and accuracy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 9 (§1.5)', 'Measurement P is 50.0 cm taken with a metre rule (least count 0.1 cm). Measurement Q is 0.20 cm taken with Vernier callipers (least count 0.01 cm). Which statement is correct?', '["P is both more precise and more accurate.", "Q is more precise, but P is more accurate.", "Q is both more precise and more accurate.", "Both are equally accurate because each is recorded to its least count."]', 1, 'Precision follows the absolute uncertainty: 0.01 cm (Q) < 0.1 cm (P), so Q is more precise. Accuracy follows percentage uncertainty: P = 0.1/50.0 = 0.2%, Q = 0.01/0.20 = 5%, so P is more accurate.', '{"0": "Q''s least count is smaller, so Q is more precise.", "2": "Q''s percentage uncertainty is 5% against P''s 0.2%, so P is more accurate.", "3": "Accuracy depends on percentage uncertainty, which differs a lot here."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0487', 'Measurements', 'Precision and accuracy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 9 (§1.5)', 'A thin wire of diameter about 0.5 mm must be measured as accurately as possible. Following the book''s reasoning, which instrument is best?', '["A metre rule", "Vernier callipers", "A micrometer screw gauge", "A measuring tape"]', 2, 'The smaller the quantity, the more precise the instrument should be. The micrometer screw gauge, with the smallest least count (0.01 mm, i.e. 0.001 cm), gives the smallest percentage uncertainty.', '{"0": "A 1 mm least count would give a percentage uncertainty of about 200%.", "1": "Vernier callipers (0.1 mm) give a large percentage uncertainty for such a small value.", "3": "A tape is the least precise of these."}'),
  ('PHY-0488', 'Measurements', 'Combining uncertainties', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 10 (§1.6)', 'Two masses (5.4 ± 0.1) g and (2.1 ± 0.1) g are subtracted. The result is:', '["(3.3 ± 0.1) g", "(3.3 ± 0.0) g", "(7.5 ± 0.2) g", "(3.3 ± 0.2) g"]', 3, 'For addition and subtraction, absolute uncertainties are added: 5.4 − 2.1 = 3.3 g, uncertainty 0.1 + 0.1 = 0.2 g.', '{"0": "For subtraction, the absolute uncertainties add; they do not stay the same.", "1": "Uncertainties never cancel.", "2": "7.5 g is the sum, not the difference."}'),
  ('PHY-0489', 'Measurements', 'Combining uncertainties', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 10 (§1.6)', 'A potential difference is (6.0 ± 0.3) V and the current is (1.50 ± 0.03) A. What is the percentage uncertainty in R = V/I?', '["3%", "5%", "7%", "10%"]', 2, 'For multiplication and division, percentage uncertainties add. V: 0.3/6.0 = 5%; I: 0.03/1.50 = 2%. Total = 7%.', '{"0": "This subtracts the percentage uncertainties; for division they are added.", "1": "5% is only the uncertainty in V.", "3": "This doubles the 5% instead of adding 2%."}'),
  ('PHY-0490', 'Measurements', 'Combining uncertainties', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 10 (§1.6)', 'The side of a cube is measured with a percentage uncertainty of 0.5%. What is the percentage uncertainty in its volume?', '["0.17%", "0.5%", "1.0%", "1.5%"]', 3, 'For a power factor, the percentage uncertainty is multiplied by the power: V = a³, so 3 × 0.5% = 1.5%.', '{"0": "The uncertainty is multiplied by the power, not divided by it.", "1": "The power 3 in V = a³ multiplies the percentage uncertainty.", "2": "Multiplying by 2 is for an area (a²), not a volume."}'),
  ('PHY-0491', 'Measurements', 'Combining uncertainties', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 11 (Example 1.3)', 'A cylinder has diameter (2.00 ± 0.01) cm and length (5.00 ± 0.01) cm. Using V = πd²L/4, the percentage uncertainty in V is:', '["0.5%", "0.7%", "1.0%", "1.2%"]', 3, '%Δd = 0.01/2.00 = 0.5%, doubled for d² = 1.0%. %ΔL = 0.01/5.00 = 0.2%. Total = 1.0% + 0.2% = 1.2%.', '{"0": "This ignores both the power of 2 and the length''s uncertainty.", "1": "The diameter''s 0.5% must be doubled because d is squared.", "2": "This is only the diameter''s contribution (2 × 0.5%); the length''s 0.2% must be added."}'),
  ('PHY-0492', 'Measurements', 'Combining uncertainties', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 10 (§1.6)', 'A resistance is calculated as 4.27 Ω with a total uncertainty of about ±0.3 Ω. How should it be recorded according to the book?', '["(4.3 ± 0.3) Ω", "(4.27 ± 0.3) Ω", "(4.27 ± 0.30) Ω", "(4 ± 0.3) Ω"]', 0, 'The uncertainty, being only an estimate, is given to one significant figure (±0.3 Ω), and the value is rounded to the same decimal place: (4.3 ± 0.3) Ω, as in the book''s R = 5.0 ± 0.5 Ω example.', '{"1": "The value should not carry digits beyond the place where the uncertainty lies.", "2": "The uncertainty is only an estimate and is recorded to one significant figure.", "3": "The value should be kept to the same decimal place as the uncertainty (one decimal place)."}'),
  ('PHY-0493', 'Measurements', 'Dimensions', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 12 (§1.7)', 'Which five quantities does the book take as the basic dimensions?', '["Mass, length, time, force and energy", "Mass, length, time, electric current and temperature", "Mass, length, time, luminous intensity and amount of substance", "Length, velocity, acceleration, force and mass"]', 1, 'The book uses [M], [L], [T], [A] and [θ] for mass, length, time, electric current and temperature, chosen because they are easy to measure in experiments.', '{"0": "Force and energy are derived; their dimensions come from M, L and T.", "2": "The book chooses current and temperature, not these, as the other two dimensions.", "3": "Velocity, acceleration and force are derived quantities."}'),
  ('PHY-0494', 'Measurements', 'Dimensions', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 12 (§1.7)', 'Which quantity has the dimensions [MLT⁻²]?', '["Momentum", "Energy", "Force", "Power"]', 2, 'Force = mass × acceleration = [M][LT⁻²] = [MLT⁻²].', '{"0": "Momentum = mv has dimensions [MLT⁻¹].", "1": "Energy has dimensions [ML²T⁻²].", "3": "Power has dimensions [ML²T⁻³]."}'),
  ('PHY-0495', 'Measurements', 'Dimensions', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 12 (§1.7)', 'What are the dimensions of pressure?', '["[MLT⁻²]", "[ML⁻²T⁻²]", "[ML²T⁻²]", "[ML⁻¹T⁻²]"]', 3, 'Pressure = force/area = [MLT⁻²]/[L²] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²].', '{"0": "These are the dimensions of force, not force per area.", "1": "Dividing [MLT⁻²] by [L²] gives L⁻¹, not L⁻².", "2": "These are the dimensions of work or energy."}'),
  ('PHY-0496', 'Measurements', 'Dimensions', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 12 (§1.7)', 'Which pair of quantities have the SAME dimensions?', '["Impulse and linear momentum", "Force and power", "Work and momentum", "Pressure and energy"]', 0, 'Impulse = FΔt = [MLT⁻²][T] = [MLT⁻¹], the same as momentum mv.', '{"1": "Force is [MLT⁻²]; power is [ML²T⁻³].", "2": "Work is [ML²T⁻²]; momentum is [MLT⁻¹].", "3": "Pressure is [ML⁻¹T⁻²]; energy is [ML²T⁻²]."}'),
  ('PHY-0497', 'Measurements', 'Dimensions', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 12 (§1.7)', 'Which of the following quantities is dimensionless?', '["Angular velocity", "Strain (change in length ÷ original length)", "Frequency", "Gravitational acceleration"]', 1, 'Strain is a ratio of two lengths, [L]/[L], so it has no dimensions.', '{"0": "Angular velocity has dimensions [T⁻¹].", "2": "Frequency has dimensions [T⁻¹].", "3": "g has dimensions [LT⁻²]."}'),
  ('PHY-0498', 'Measurements', 'Homogeneity of equations', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 12 (§1.7)', 'The principle of homogeneity says that a physical equation is dimensionally correct when:', '["the numerical values on both sides are equal", "all the quantities in it are base quantities", "the dimensions on both sides of the equation are the same", "it contains no numerical constants"]', 2, 'The correctness of an equation can be checked by showing that the dimensions of the quantities on both sides are the same; this is the principle of homogeneity.', '{"0": "Numerical equality is not what dimensional analysis tests.", "1": "Equations normally contain derived quantities too.", "3": "Constants like 1/2 are allowed; they are simply dimensionless."}'),
  ('PHY-0499', 'Measurements', 'Homogeneity of equations', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 13 (§1.7)', 'Which equation is NOT dimensionally correct? (v, u = speeds, a = acceleration, t = time, S = distance)', '["v = u + at", "v² = u² + 2aS", "S = ut + ½at²", "S = vt²"]', 3, 'For S = vt², the right side is [LT⁻¹][T²] = [LT], which does not match [L] on the left. The other three are homogeneous.', '{"0": "[LT⁻¹] = [LT⁻¹] + [LT⁻²][T]: consistent.", "1": "[L²T⁻²] = [L²T⁻²] + [LT⁻²][L]: consistent.", "2": "[L] = [LT⁻¹][T] + [LT⁻²][T²]: consistent."}'),
  ('PHY-0500', 'Measurements', 'Deriving a formula', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 13 (Example 1.4)', 'Assume the speed v of a wave on a stretched string depends on the tension T and the mass per unit length μ, as v = k Tᵃ μᵇ. Dimensional analysis gives:', '["a = ½, b = −½", "a = 1, b = −1", "a = −½, b = ½", "a = ½, b = ½"]', 0, '[LT⁻¹] = [MLT⁻²]ᵃ[ML⁻¹]ᵇ. M: a + b = 0; L: a − b = 1; T: −2a = −1. So a = ½, b = −½, giving v = k√(T/μ).', '{"1": "v = kT/μ would have dimensions [L²T⁻²], not [LT⁻¹].", "2": "This gives [L⁻¹T], the inverse of a speed.", "3": "[MLT⁻²]^½[ML⁻¹]^½ = [MT⁻¹], not a speed."}'),
  ('PHY-0501', 'Measurements', 'Deriving a formula', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 14 (§1.7)', 'Assume the period T of a simple pendulum depends on its length l, the mass of the bob m and g, as T = k lᵃ mᵇ gᶜ. Dimensional analysis gives:', '["a = 1, b = 0, c = −1", "a = ½, b = 0, c = −½", "a = ½, b = 1, c = −½", "a = −½, b = 0, c = ½"]', 1, '[T] = [L]ᵃ[M]ᵇ[LT⁻²]ᶜ. M: b = 0. T: −2c = 1, so c = −½. L: a + c = 0, so a = ½. Hence T = k√(l/g).', '{"0": "l/g has dimensions [T²], not [T].", "2": "The mass power must be zero since T has no [M].", "3": "This gives [T⁻¹], a frequency."}'),
  ('PHY-0502', 'Measurements', 'Limitations of dimensional analysis', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 14 (§1.7)', 'Which is a limitation of dimensional analysis stated in the book?', '["It cannot be applied to equations of motion.", "It can only be used for quantities with SI units.", "It cannot give the value of dimensionless constants such as the 2π in a formula.", "It shows exactly which term of a wrong equation is wrong."]', 2, 'Dimensional analysis provides no check on numerical factors or constants; these must be found by experiment. Also, a dimensionally correct equation is only possibly correct.', '{"0": "The book itself checks S = ut + ½at² this way.", "1": "Dimensions do not depend on the system of units.", "3": "The book says it cannot identify where an equation is wrong."}'),
  ('PHY-0503', 'Measurements', 'Measurement basics', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Measurements, p. 3 (§1.2)', 'The angstrom and the barn are described in the book as units used in:', '["meteorology", "land measurement", "everyday shopping", "physics and astronomy"]', 3, 'The book lists the angstrom and the barn as units used in physics and astronomy.', '{"0": "The book links the millibar and the knot with meteorology.", "1": "The acre and the hectare are the land units.", "2": "The litre and the tonne are the everyday metric units."}'),
  ('PHY-0504', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalars and vectors', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 20 (§2.1)', 'Which of the following is a scalar quantity?', '["Energy", "Displacement", "Acceleration", "Force"]', 0, 'Scalars are described by magnitude alone. The book lists mass, distance, speed, time, energy and temperature as scalars.', '{"1": "Displacement has both magnitude and direction (e.g. 10 m towards west).", "2": "Acceleration is a rate of change of velocity and has a direction.", "3": "Force is a push or pull with a definite direction."}'),
  ('PHY-0505', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalars and vectors', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 20 (§2.2)', 'Which group contains only vector quantities?', '["Distance, speed, time", "Mass, energy, temperature", "Speed, velocity, acceleration", "Displacement, velocity, force"]', 3, 'Displacement, velocity, acceleration and force need both magnitude and direction for complete specification, so they are vectors.', '{"0": "All three are scalars.", "1": "All three are scalars.", "2": "Speed is a scalar."}'),
  ('PHY-0506', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalars and vectors', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 20 (§2.1)', 'In the book, temperature is described as a measure of the:', '["average kinetic energy of the particles of a substance", "total heat contained in a body", "direction of heat flow", "potential energy of the molecules only"]', 0, 'The book defines temperature, a scalar, as a measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles in a substance (e.g. 20 °C).', '{"1": "Heat is energy in transfer; temperature is linked to average kinetic energy.", "2": "Temperature is a scalar with no direction.", "3": "The book links temperature to average kinetic energy."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0507', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Rectangular components', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 21 (§2.2)', 'A vector A makes an angle θ with the x-axis. Its rectangular components are:', '["Aₓ = A sin θ and A_y = A cos θ", "Aₓ = A tan θ and A_y = A/tan θ", "Aₓ = A_y = A/2", "Aₓ = A cos θ and A_y = A sin θ"]', 3, 'From the right-angled triangle OMP: Aₓ = A cos θ along x, A_y = A sin θ along y.', '{"0": "These are swapped; for an angle measured from the x-axis, the x-component uses cos θ.", "1": "Components come from cos and sin of the angle, not tan.", "2": "Components are equal only at 45°, and even then each is A/√2."}'),
  ('PHY-0508', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Rectangular components', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 21 (§2.2)', 'A force of 20 N acts at 30° to the x-axis. Its y-component is:', '["10 N", "11.5 N", "17.3 N", "20 N"]', 0, 'F_y = F sin θ = 20 × sin 30° = 20 × 0.5 = 10 N.', '{"1": "11.5 N is 20 tan 30°, not a component.", "2": "17.3 N is the x-component (20 cos 30°).", "3": "The component cannot equal the whole vector unless θ = 90°."}'),
  ('PHY-0509', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Rectangular components', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 21 (§2.2)', 'A vector has rectangular components Aₓ = −3 units and A_y = 4 units. Its magnitude is:', '["1 unit", "5 units", "7 units", "25 units"]', 1, 'A = √(Aₓ² + A_y²) = √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5 units.', '{"0": "Components are combined by Pythagoras, not by simple addition.", "2": "Adding the magnitudes ignores that the components are perpendicular.", "3": "25 is A², not A."}'),
  ('PHY-0510', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Rectangular components', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 21 (§2.2)', 'A vector has components Aₓ = 5 units and A_y = 5√3 units. The angle it makes with the x-axis is:', '["30°", "45°", "60°", "90°"]', 2, 'θ = tan⁻¹(A_y/Aₓ) = tan⁻¹(√3) = 60°.', '{"0": "tan 30° = 1/√3, which would need A_y = Aₓ/√3.", "1": "45° needs equal components.", "3": "90° needs Aₓ = 0."}'),
  ('PHY-0511', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Rectangular components', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 22 (Example 2.1)', 'Two forces of equal magnitude F act at a point. Their resultant also has magnitude F. The angle between the forces is:', '["60°", "90°", "120°", "180°"]', 2, 'R² = F² + F² + 2F² cos θ. Setting R = F gives 2 cos θ + 1 = 0, so cos θ = −0.5 and θ = 120°.', '{"0": "At 60° the resultant is F√3.", "1": "At 90° the resultant is F√2.", "3": "At 180° the forces cancel and the resultant is zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0512', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar product', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 22 (§2.3)', 'The scalar (dot) product of two vectors A and B is defined as:', '["AB cos θ", "AB sin θ", "AB tan θ", "A + B cos θ"]', 0, 'A·B = AB cos θ, where θ is the angle between the vectors. The result is a scalar.', '{"1": "AB sin θ is the magnitude of the vector (cross) product.", "2": "No vector product is defined this way.", "3": "The product multiplies the magnitudes; it does not add them."}'),
  ('PHY-0513', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar product', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 23 (§2.3)', 'Work done by a force is an example of a scalar product because:', '["W = F × d, the work is perpendicular to both F and d", "work has the same direction as the force", "work is always equal to Fd", "W = F·d = Fd cos θ, only the component of force along the displacement does work"]', 3, 'Work = (effective component of force along the motion) × distance = (F cos θ)d = F·d.', '{"0": "Work is a scalar, so it is not a cross product.", "1": "Work has no direction.", "2": "It equals Fd only when force and displacement are parallel."}'),
  ('PHY-0514', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar product', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 23 (§2.3)', 'Which property of the scalar product is correct?', '["A·B = B·A", "A·B = −B·A", "A·A = 0", "A·B is maximum when A ⊥ B"]', 0, 'Since AB cos θ = BA cos θ, the order of multiplication does not matter: the scalar product is commutative.', '{"1": "The sign change belongs to the cross product: A × B = −B × A.", "2": "A·A = A², the square of the magnitude.", "3": "For perpendicular vectors, A·B = 0."}'),
  ('PHY-0515', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar product', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 23 (§2.3)', 'For two antiparallel vectors A and B, the scalar product A·B equals:', '["AB", "0", "A²", "−AB"]', 3, 'For antiparallel vectors θ = 180°, so A·B = AB cos 180° = −AB.', '{"0": "AB is for parallel vectors (θ = 0°).", "1": "Zero is for perpendicular vectors.", "2": "A² is A·A."}'),
  ('PHY-0516', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar product', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 23 (§2.3)', 'If A = 2i + 3j − k and B = i − j + k, then A·B is:', '["−2", "0", "2", "6"]', 0, 'A·B = AₓBₓ + A_yB_y + A_zB_z = (2)(1) + (3)(−1) + (−1)(1) = 2 − 3 − 1 = −2.', '{"1": "Zero would mean the vectors are perpendicular.", "2": "2(1) + 3(−1) + (−1)(1) = 2 − 3 − 1 = −2, not +2.", "3": "This multiplies magnitudes of components without signs."}'),
  ('PHY-0517', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar product', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 23 (§2.3)', 'Vectors A = 3i + 4j and B = 4i − 3j are:', '["perpendicular to each other", "parallel to each other", "antiparallel to each other", "at 45° to each other"]', 0, 'A·B = (3)(4) + (4)(−3) = 12 − 12 = 0. A zero dot product between non-zero vectors means θ = 90°.', '{"1": "For parallel vectors, A·B = AB = 25, not 0.", "2": "For antiparallel vectors, A·B = −25.", "3": "At 45°, A·B = 25 × 0.707 ≈ 17.7."}'),
  ('PHY-0518', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Vector product', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 24 (§2.3)', 'The direction of A × B is found by the right-hand rule. It is:', '["along A", "along B", "along A + B", "perpendicular to the plane containing A and B"]', 3, 'A × B = AB sin θ n̂, where n̂ is a unit vector perpendicular to the plane of A and B; curling the fingers from A to B, the thumb gives its direction.', '{"0": "The cross product is perpendicular to A.", "1": "The cross product is perpendicular to B.", "2": "A + B lies in the plane of A and B."}'),
  ('PHY-0519', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Vector product', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 24 (§2.3)', 'Which statement about the vector product is correct?', '["A × B = −(B × A)", "A × B = B × A", "A × A = A²", "A × B is maximum for parallel vectors"]', 0, 'Reversing the order reverses the sense of rotation in the right-hand rule, so B × A points opposite to A × B: A × B = −B × A.', '{"1": "The cross product is not commutative.", "2": "A × A is a null vector because θ = 0°.", "3": "For parallel vectors, sin 0° = 0, so the product is zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0520', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Vector product', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 24 (§2.3)', 'Two vectors of magnitudes 4 units and 5 units are at 30° to each other. The area of the parallelogram formed with them as adjacent sides is:', '["5 square units", "10 square units", "17.3 square units", "20 square units"]', 1, 'The area of the parallelogram equals |A × B| = AB sin θ = 4 × 5 × sin 30° = 10 square units.', '{"0": "This uses half of AB sin θ, which is the triangle, not the parallelogram.", "2": "17.3 = AB cos 30°, the scalar product.", "3": "20 = AB, which would need θ = 90°."}'),
  ('PHY-0521', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Vector product', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 25 (§2.3)', 'Which physical quantity is given by a vector (cross) product in the book?', '["Work, W = F·d", "Power, P = F·v", "Kinetic energy, ½mv²", "Torque, τ = r × F"]', 3, 'The book gives torque τ = r × F and the magnetic force F = q(v × B) as examples of vector products.', '{"0": "Work is a scalar (dot) product.", "1": "Power is a scalar product of force and velocity.", "2": "Kinetic energy is not a product of two vectors."}'),
  ('PHY-0522', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Vector product', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 25 (§2.3)', 'A charge q moves with velocity v parallel to a magnetic field B. The magnetic force on it, F = q(v × B), is:', '["zero", "qvB", "qvB/2", "maximum"]', 0, 'For parallel vectors θ = 0°, so v × B = vB sin 0° n̂ = 0 and the force is zero.', '{"1": "qvB is the force when v is perpendicular to B.", "2": "There is no factor of ½.", "3": "The force is maximum when v ⊥ B."}'),
  ('PHY-0523', 'Force and motion', 'Equations of motion', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 25 (§2.4)', 'The equations of motion apply only to bodies moving:', '["in a circle with constant speed", "with any varying acceleration", "only when they start from rest", "in a straight line with constant acceleration"]', 3, 'The book states that the equations of motion can only be applied to objects moving in a straight line with constant acceleration.', '{"0": "Circular motion has a changing direction, so the straight-line equations do not apply.", "1": "The equations assume the acceleration is constant.", "2": "They include an initial velocity vᵢ, so the body need not start from rest."}'),
  ('PHY-0524', 'Force and motion', 'Equations of motion', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 26 (§2.4)', 'On a velocity–time graph of uniformly accelerated motion, the slope of the line gives the:', '["acceleration", "displacement", "initial velocity", "time of motion"]', 0, 'The slope CB/AC of the v–t graph equals the change in velocity per unit time, which is the acceleration a.', '{"1": "Displacement is the area under the velocity–time graph.", "2": "The initial velocity is the intercept on the velocity axis.", "3": "Time is read along the horizontal axis."}'),
  ('PHY-0525', 'Force and motion', 'Equations of motion', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 27 (§2.4)', 'In the graphical derivation of the second equation of motion, the displacement is found as the:', '["slope of the velocity–time graph", "area under the velocity–time graph", "intercept of the graph on the time axis", "product of the final velocity and acceleration"]', 1, 'S = area of figure OABD = area of rectangle + area of triangle = vᵢt + ½at².', '{"0": "The slope gives acceleration.", "2": "The intercept is not the displacement.", "3": "That product has units of m² s⁻³, not of displacement."}'),
  ('PHY-0526', 'Force and motion', 'Equations of motion', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 29 (Example 2.2)', 'A cyclist moving at 4 m s⁻¹ accelerates uniformly at 1.5 m s⁻² for 6 s. Her final velocity is:', '["9 m s⁻¹", "11.5 m s⁻¹", "13 m s⁻¹", "31 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'v_f = v_i + at = 4 + (1.5)(6) = 4 + 9 = 13 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This uses 1.5 × 6 = 9 but forgets the initial 4 m s⁻¹.", "1": "4 + 1.5 + 6 adds the numbers instead of using vf = vi + at.", "3": "4 + 1.5 × 6² uses t² wrongly."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0527', 'Force and motion', 'Equations of motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 29 (Example 2.3)', 'A car moving at 12 m s⁻¹ accelerates at 3 m s⁻² for 4 s. The distance covered in this time is:', '["24 m", "48 m", "72 m", "96 m"]', 2, 'S = vᵢt + ½at² = (12)(4) + ½(3)(16) = 48 + 24 = 72 m.', '{"0": "24 m = ½at², ignoring the initial speed.", "1": "48 m = vᵢt only; it ignores the ½at² term.", "3": "96 m uses at² instead of ½at²."}'),
  ('PHY-0528', 'Force and motion', 'Equations of motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 30 (Example 2.4)', 'A car starting from rest reaches 30 m s⁻¹ after covering 150 m with constant acceleration. Its acceleration is:', '["0.2 m s⁻²", "1.5 m s⁻²", "3 m s⁻²", "6 m s⁻²"]', 2, 'v_f² − v_i² = 2aS → 900 − 0 = 2a(150) → a = 900/300 = 3 m s⁻².', '{"0": "30/150 divides speed by distance, which is not an acceleration.", "1": "This divides by 4S instead of 2S.", "3": "This forgets the factor 2 in v_f² − v_i² = 2aS."}'),
  ('PHY-0529', 'Force and motion', 'Equations of motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 28 (§2.4)', 'A train moving at 20 m s⁻¹ is brought to rest by a constant retardation of 0.5 m s⁻². How far does it travel before stopping?', '["40 m", "200 m", "400 m", "800 m"]', 2, '0 − (20)² = 2(−0.5)S → S = 400/1 = 400 m. The retardation is negative because it opposes the initial velocity.', '{"0": "40 s is the stopping time, not the distance.", "1": "This forgets the factor 2 in 2aS.", "3": "This uses v² ÷ a instead of v² ÷ 2a."}'),
  ('PHY-0530', 'Force and motion', 'Equations of motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 28 (§2.4)', 'When using the equations of motion for a body that does not reverse direction, the book''s sign convention is:', '["upward is always positive and downward always negative", "all quantities are taken as positive", "acceleration is always positive", "the initial velocity is taken as positive, and quantities opposite to it are negative"]', 3, 'The book says the initial velocity is taken as positive and a negative sign is assigned to quantities whose direction is opposite to it.', '{"0": "The book ties the sign to the direction of the initial velocity.", "1": "A retardation must be given a negative sign.", "2": "A retardation opposite to the initial velocity is negative."}'),
  ('PHY-0531', 'Force and motion', 'Motion under gravity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 30 (§2.5)', 'According to Galileo, when different bodies are dropped together from the same height in a vacuum:', '["they strike the ground with the same velocity", "heavier bodies land first", "lighter bodies land first", "they fall with constant velocity"]', 0, 'All bodies fall freely in a vacuum with the same acceleration g, so from the same height they reach the ground with the same velocity.', '{"1": "In a vacuum all bodies fall with the same acceleration g.", "2": "Without air, mass does not affect the motion.", "3": "They accelerate at g."}'),
  ('PHY-0532', 'Force and motion', 'Motion under gravity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 30 (Example 2.5)', 'A stone is dropped from rest and reaches the ground in 2.0 s. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², the height it fell from is:', '["4.9 m", "9.8 m", "19.6 m", "39.2 m"]', 2, 'h = vᵢt + ½gt² = 0 + ½(9.8)(2.0)² = 19.6 m.', '{"0": "4.9 m is the fall in the first second only.", "1": "9.8 m uses ½gt with t, not t².", "3": "39.2 m uses gt² without the ½."}'),
  ('PHY-0533', 'Force and motion', 'Motion under gravity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 30 (§2.5)', 'A ball is thrown vertically upward at 14.7 m s⁻¹. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², the time it takes to reach its highest point is:', '["0.67 s", "1.5 s", "3.0 s", "14.7 s"]', 1, 'At the top v_f = 0. Using v_f = vᵢ − gt: 0 = 14.7 − 9.8t, so t = 1.5 s.', '{"0": "This divides g by the speed instead of the speed by g.", "2": "3.0 s is the time to go up and come back down.", "3": "The speed must be divided by g."}'),
  ('PHY-0534', 'Force and motion', 'Motion under gravity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 30 (§2.5)', 'A ball is thrown vertically upward at 19.6 m s⁻¹. The maximum height it reaches is (g = 9.8 m s⁻²):', '["2.0 m", "9.8 m", "19.6 m", "39.2 m"]', 2, '0 − vᵢ² = −2gh → h = (19.6)²/(2 × 9.8) = 384.16/19.6 = 19.6 m.', '{"0": "2.0 s is the time to reach the top, not the height.", "1": "This divides by 4g.", "3": "This forgets the factor 2 in 2gh."}'),
  ('PHY-0535', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 31 (§2.6)', 'A ball is thrown horizontally from a cliff. Ignoring air resistance, its horizontal velocity during flight:', '["increases at the rate g", "remains constant", "decreases to zero at the ground", "first increases then decreases"]', 1, 'Only gravity acts during flight and it is vertical. By Newton''s first law, with no horizontal force the horizontal velocity stays at its initial value until the ball lands.', '{"0": "g acts vertically, not horizontally.", "2": "No horizontal force acts, so it does not slow down.", "3": "With no horizontal force, the horizontal velocity cannot change."}'),
  ('PHY-0536', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 32 (§2.6)', 'Projectile motion is best described as:', '["one-dimensional motion with constant velocity", "two-dimensional motion under the constant acceleration due to gravity", "circular motion at constant speed", "motion with a horizontal acceleration g"]', 1, 'The book defines projectile motion as two-dimensional motion under constant acceleration due to gravity, which can be split into independent horizontal and vertical motions.', '{"0": "A projectile moves both horizontally and vertically.", "2": "The path is a parabola, not a circle.", "3": "The acceleration g is vertical."}'),
  ('PHY-0537', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 32 (§2.6)', 'One ball is dropped and, at the same moment, another is projected horizontally from the same height. Ignoring air resistance:', '["the dropped ball lands first", "the projected ball lands first", "the heavier ball lands first", "both reach the ground at the same time"]', 3, 'Horizontal and vertical motions are independent. Both balls start with zero vertical velocity and accelerate down at g, so they are always at the same level (the book''s photograph of two balls shows this).', '{"0": "Both have the same vertical motion, starting from zero vertical velocity.", "1": "Horizontal velocity does not change the vertical fall.", "2": "Mass does not affect free fall in the absence of air resistance."}'),
  ('PHY-0538', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 32 (§2.6)', 'For a projectile launched at angle θ with speed vᵢ, the vertical component of velocity after time t is:', '["vᵢ sin θ − gt", "vᵢ cos θ − gt", "vᵢ sin θ + gt", "vᵢ sin θ"]', 0, 'Treating the upward motion with a_y = −g: v_y = vᵢ sin θ − gt (Eq. 2.19).', '{"1": "vᵢ cos θ is the horizontal component, which stays constant.", "2": "Gravity decreases the upward velocity, so gt is subtracted.", "3": "That is only the initial vertical component; it changes with time."}'),
  ('PHY-0539', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 33 (§2.6)', 'At the highest point of its path, an ideal projectile has:', '["a velocity equal to its horizontal component, vᵢ cos θ", "zero velocity", "zero acceleration", "maximum speed"]', 0, 'At the top v_y = 0, but vₓ = vᵢ cos θ is unchanged, so the velocity is horizontal and equal to vᵢ cos θ.', '{"1": "Only the vertical component is zero; the horizontal component remains.", "2": "The acceleration is g downward throughout the flight.", "3": "The speed is minimum at the top."}'),
  ('PHY-0540', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 33 (§2.6)', 'The maximum height reached by a projectile launched at speed vᵢ and angle θ is:', '["vᵢ² sin 2θ / g", "vᵢ² sin²θ / 2g", "2vᵢ sin θ / g", "vᵢ² / 2g"]', 1, 'Using 2aS = v_f² − v_i² with a = −g, v_f = 0 and v_i = vᵢ sin θ: h = vᵢ² sin²θ / 2g (Eq. 2.22).', '{"0": "This is the range.", "2": "This is the time of flight.", "3": "This is the height only for vertical launch (θ = 90°)."}'),
  ('PHY-0541', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 33 (§2.6)', 'A ball is projected at 20 m s⁻¹ at 30° above the horizontal. Its time of flight on level ground is (g = 10 m s⁻²):', '["1 s", "2 s", "3.5 s", "4 s"]', 1, 'T = 2vᵢ sin θ / g = 2(20)(0.5)/10 = 2 s.', '{"0": "1 s is the time to reach the highest point.", "2": "This uses cos 30° instead of sin 30°.", "3": "This forgets sin 30° = 0.5."}'),
  ('PHY-0542', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 34 (§2.6)', 'A projectile is launched at 40 m s⁻¹ at 15° to the horizontal. Its range on level ground is (g = 10 m s⁻²):', '["41 m", "80 m", "138.6 m", "160 m"]', 1, 'R = vᵢ² sin 2θ / g = (1600)(sin 30°)/10 = 1600 × 0.5/10 = 80 m.', '{"0": "This uses sin 15° instead of sin 2θ = sin 30°.", "2": "This uses sin 60° instead of sin 30°.", "3": "160 m is the maximum range at 45°, where sin 2θ = 1."}'),
  ('PHY-0543', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 34 (§2.6)', 'A projectile has a range R at 25°. At which other launch angle, with the same speed, will it have the same range?', '["45°", "50°", "65°", "155°"]', 2, 'R ∝ sin 2θ, and sin 2θ = sin(180° − 2θ). So θ and 90° − θ give the same range: 90° − 25° = 65°.', '{"0": "45° gives the maximum range, which is larger.", "1": "50° is double the angle; the matching angle is 90° − 25°.", "3": "155° = 180° − 25°, which is not a launch angle above the horizontal."}'),
  ('PHY-0544', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 33 (§2.6)', 'For a projectile, at what angle of projection does the maximum height equal the range?', '["45°", "tan θ = 1/4 (about 14°)", "tan θ = 4 (about 76°)", "90°"]', 2, 'Setting vᵢ² sin²θ / 2g = 2vᵢ² sin θ cos θ / g gives sin θ = 4 cos θ, so tan θ = 4 and θ ≈ 76°.', '{"0": "At 45°, H = R/4.", "1": "This makes H much smaller than R.", "3": "At 90°, the range is zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0545', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 31 (§2.6)', 'A package is dropped from a plane flying horizontally at 100 m s⁻¹ at a height of 500 m. How far horizontally from the drop point does it land? (g = 10 m s⁻², no air resistance)', '["100 m", "500 m", "1000 m", "2000 m"]', 2, 'Fall time: 500 = ½(10)t², so t = 10 s. Horizontal distance x = vₓt = 100 × 10 = 1000 m.', '{"0": "This uses a fall time of 1 s.", "1": "500 m is the height, not the horizontal distance.", "3": "This doubles the fall time."}'),
  ('PHY-0546', 'Force and motion', 'Effect of air resistance', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 34 (§2.6)', 'How does air resistance affect the path of a high-speed projectile, according to the book?', '["It increases the range because the projectile stays in the air longer.", "It has no effect on the horizontal motion.", "It makes the path a perfect parabola.", "It reduces the range and height, and the path becomes skewed, with a steeper descent than ascent."]', 3, 'Air resistance slows the forward motion and reduces the upward velocity, so both the range and the height decrease. Because it is not constant, the path is not a perfect parabola: it is skewed, with a steeper descent than ascent.', '{"0": "Air resistance reduces the horizontal velocity, so the range decreases.", "1": "Air resistance slows the forward motion.", "2": "The ideal (no-air) path is the parabola; air resistance distorts it."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0547', 'Force and motion', 'Momentum', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 35 (§2.7)', 'Which unit is equivalent to the SI unit of linear momentum, kg m s⁻¹?', '["N s", "N m", "N s⁻¹", "J s"]', 0, 'p = mv has units kg m s⁻¹. Since 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻², 1 N s = 1 kg m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "N m is the joule (work) or the unit of torque.", "2": "N s⁻¹ = kg m s⁻³, not momentum.", "3": "J s = kg m² s⁻¹, the unit of angular momentum."}'),
  ('PHY-0548', 'Force and motion', 'Newton''s second law and momentum', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 35 (§2.7)', 'The book says the momentum form of Newton''s second law is more general than F = ma because it:', '["applies only to bodies at rest", "can be extended to bodies whose mass changes as they accelerate, like a rocket", "does not involve force", "ignores the direction of motion"]', 1, 'F = (mv_f − mv_i)/t, the time rate of change of momentum, can account for a changing mass, for example a rocket that loses mass as its fuel burns.', '{"0": "It applies to any motion.", "2": "It equates force to rate of change of momentum.", "3": "Momentum is a vector, so direction is included."}'),
  ('PHY-0549', 'Force and motion', 'Impulse', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 36 (§2.7)', 'A 0.15 kg ball moving at 20 m s⁻¹ is hit straight back at 30 m s⁻¹ by a bat. The magnitude of the impulse on the ball is:', '["1.5 N s", "3.0 N s", "4.5 N s", "7.5 N s"]', 3, 'Taking the final direction as positive: Δp = 0.15(30) − 0.15(−20) = 4.5 + 3.0 = 7.5 N s.', '{"0": "This subtracts the speeds, ignoring the reversal of direction.", "1": "This uses only the initial momentum.", "2": "This uses only the final momentum."}'),
  ('PHY-0550', 'Force and motion', 'Impulse', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 36 (Example 2.7)', 'A 1200 kg car slows from 25 m s⁻¹ to 10 m s⁻¹ in 4.0 s. The average retarding force is:', '["3000 N", "4500 N", "7500 N", "18000 N"]', 1, 'F = (mv_f − mv_i)/t = 1200(10 − 25)/4.0 = −4500 N. The magnitude is 4500 N; the minus sign shows it is a retarding force.', '{"0": "3000 N would be the force for a velocity change of 10 m s⁻¹.", "2": "7500 N is the force that would stop the car completely from 25 m s⁻¹ in 4.0 s.", "3": "18000 N is the change in momentum, not the force."}'),
  ('PHY-0551', 'Force and motion', 'Impulse', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 35 (§2.7)', 'Why is landing on a heap of sand less painful than landing on a concrete floor from the same height?', '["The sand reduces the change in momentum.", "The sand reduces the body''s mass.", "The sand increases the stopping time, so a smaller force gives the same change in momentum.", "The concrete floor increases the speed of impact."]', 2, 'Impulse F × t = change in momentum is fixed. Sand lets the body stop over a longer time t, so the average force F is smaller.', '{"0": "The body comes to rest either way, so the change in momentum is the same.", "1": "Mass does not change.", "3": "The impact speed is the same from the same height."}'),
  ('PHY-0552', 'Force and motion', 'Conservation of momentum', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 37 (§2.7)', 'The law of conservation of linear momentum applies to:', '["any system, even if a large external force acts", "only elastic collisions", "only bodies at rest", "an isolated system, on which no external force acts"]', 3, 'The total linear momentum of an isolated system (no external force) remains constant.', '{"0": "An external force changes the total momentum.", "1": "Momentum is conserved in both elastic and inelastic collisions.", "2": "It applies to moving bodies too."}'),
  ('PHY-0553', 'Force and motion', 'Conservation of momentum', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 37 (Example 2.8)', 'A 3.0 kg ball moving at 4.0 m s⁻¹ hits a 1.0 kg ball at rest. After the collision the 3.0 kg ball moves on at 2.0 m s⁻¹ in the same direction. The velocity of the 1.0 kg ball is:', '["2.0 m s⁻¹ forward", "4.0 m s⁻¹ forward", "6.0 m s⁻¹ forward", "12.0 m s⁻¹ forward"]', 2, 'Before: 3.0 × 4.0 = 12 kg m s⁻¹. After: 3.0 × 2.0 + 1.0 × v = 12, so v = 6.0 m s⁻¹ forward.', '{"0": "This assumes both move together, which breaks momentum conservation here.", "1": "This would need the 3 kg ball''s momentum loss to be 4 kg m s⁻¹.", "3": "12 kg m s⁻¹ is the total momentum, not the velocity."}'),
  ('PHY-0554', 'Force and motion', 'Conservation of momentum', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 37 (§2.7)', 'A 60 kg skater at rest pushes a 40 kg skater, also at rest, who moves off at 3 m s⁻¹. The 60 kg skater moves back at:', '["0 m s⁻¹", "2 m s⁻¹", "3 m s⁻¹", "4.5 m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'Initial momentum = 0. So 60v = 40 × 3, v = 2 m s⁻¹, in the opposite direction.', '{"0": "Total momentum must stay zero, so the 60 kg skater must recoil.", "2": "Equal speeds would need equal masses.", "3": "This multiplies by 60/40 instead of 40/60."}'),
  ('PHY-0555', 'Force and motion', 'Elastic and inelastic collisions', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 38 (§2.8)', 'In an inelastic collision:', '["momentum is conserved but kinetic energy is not", "kinetic energy is conserved but momentum is not", "neither momentum nor total energy is conserved", "both momentum and kinetic energy are conserved"]', 0, 'The book: momentum and total energy are conserved in all collisions, but kinetic energy is conserved only in elastic collisions.', '{"1": "Momentum is conserved in all collisions.", "2": "Total energy and momentum are always conserved.", "3": "That describes an elastic collision."}'),
  ('PHY-0556', 'Force and motion', 'Elastic collisions in one dimension', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 39 (§2.8)', 'In a one-dimensional elastic collision between two balls, which statement is always true?', '["The two balls stick together after the collision.", "The relative speed of approach equals the relative speed of separation.", "Each ball keeps its own kinetic energy.", "The lighter ball always stops."]', 1, 'From conservation of momentum and kinetic energy: v₁ − v₂ = −(v₁′ − v₂′). The relative velocity has the same magnitude but is reversed.', '{"0": "Sticking together is a perfectly inelastic collision.", "2": "Kinetic energy can be transferred between the balls; only the total is conserved.", "3": "The outcome depends on the masses and velocities."}'),
  ('PHY-0557', 'Force and motion', 'Elastic collisions in one dimension', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 39 (§2.8)', 'A billiard ball moving at 3 m s⁻¹ collides head-on and elastically with an identical ball at rest. After the collision:', '["both move together at 1.5 m s⁻¹", "the first ball bounces back at 3 m s⁻¹", "the first ball stops and the second moves off at 3 m s⁻¹", "the second ball moves at 6 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'For m₁ = m₂ and v₂ = 0, the equations give v₁′ = 0 and v₂′ = v₁: the velocities are exchanged.', '{"0": "That is a perfectly inelastic collision.", "1": "That happens when a light ball hits a very massive one.", "3": "About twice the speed happens only when a very massive body hits a light one."}'),
  ('PHY-0558', 'Force and motion', 'Elastic collisions in one dimension', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 40 (§2.8)', 'A light rubber ball hits a massive wall elastically. According to the book''s result for this case:', '["the ball stops and the wall moves at the ball''s speed", "the wall moves off at twice the ball''s speed", "the ball and the wall move together", "the ball bounces back with nearly the same speed while the wall stays at rest"]', 3, 'When m₂ ≫ m₁ and v₂ = 0, Eqs. 2.32–2.33 give v₁′ ≈ −v₁ and v₂′ ≈ 0. The book notes squash players use this.', '{"0": "That is the case of equal masses.", "1": "That is when a massive body hits a light one.", "2": "That would be a perfectly inelastic collision."}'),
  ('PHY-0559', 'Force and motion', 'Elastic collisions in one dimension', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 40 (§2.8)', 'A heavy truck moving at 10 m s⁻¹ collides elastically head-on with a light ball at rest. After the collision, the ball moves at about:', '["0 m s⁻¹", "5 m s⁻¹", "10 m s⁻¹", "20 m s⁻¹"]', 3, 'When m₁ ≫ m₂ and v₂ = 0: v₁′ ≈ v₁ and v₂′ ≈ 2v₁. So the ball moves at about 2 × 10 = 20 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "The ball must move off since momentum is transferred.", "1": "The light body gains speed, it does not move slower than the truck.", "2": "Equal velocities would result from equal masses."}'),
  ('PHY-0560', 'Force and motion', 'Elastic collisions in one dimension', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 41 (Example 2.9)', 'A 1 kg ball moving at 6 m s⁻¹ collides head-on elastically with a 2 kg ball at rest. The velocity of the 1 kg ball after the collision is:', '["0", "2 m s⁻¹ backward", "2 m s⁻¹ forward", "4 m s⁻¹ forward"]', 1, 'v₁′ = [(m₁ − m₂)/(m₁ + m₂)]v₁ = [(1 − 2)/3](6) = −2 m s⁻¹, i.e. 2 m s⁻¹ backward. (v₂′ = [2m₁/(m₁ + m₂)]v₁ = 4 m s⁻¹.)', '{"0": "The ball stops only when the masses are equal.", "2": "(m₁ − m₂) is negative, so the 1 kg ball rebounds.", "3": "4 m s⁻¹ forward is the velocity of the 2 kg ball."}'),
  ('PHY-0561', 'Force and motion', 'Inelastic collision in one dimension', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 40 (§2.9)', 'A 2 kg trolley moving at 6 m s⁻¹ collides with a 1 kg trolley at rest and they stick together. Their common velocity is:', '["2 m s⁻¹", "3 m s⁻¹", "4 m s⁻¹", "6 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'v = m₁v₁/(m₁ + m₂) = (2 × 6)/3 = 4 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This uses only the 1 kg trolley''s share.", "1": "This divides the momentum by 4 kg instead of 3 kg.", "3": "The combined mass is larger, so the speed must drop."}'),
  ('PHY-0562', 'Force and motion', 'Inelastic collision in one dimension', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 43 (§2.11)', 'In the previous kind of collision, a 2 kg trolley at 6 m s⁻¹ sticks to a 1 kg trolley at rest and they move at 4 m s⁻¹. How much kinetic energy is lost?', '["0 J", "12 J", "24 J", "36 J"]', 1, 'Initial KE = ½(2)(6)² = 36 J. Final KE = ½(3)(4)² = 24 J. Loss = 36 − 24 = 12 J, changed into heat, sound and deformation.', '{"0": "Kinetic energy is lost in a perfectly inelastic collision.", "2": "24 J is the final kinetic energy.", "3": "36 J is the initial kinetic energy, not the loss."}'),
  ('PHY-0563', 'Force and motion', 'Inelastic collisions', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 43 (§2.11)', 'Which example does the book give of an inelastic collision?', '["A car crash in which the vehicles crumple", "A hard ball rebounding almost to its original height from a marble floor", "Two identical billiard balls exchanging velocities", "A squash ball bouncing back from a wall at the same speed"]', 0, 'In a car crash the vehicles absorb the impact energy, crumple and deform; most kinetic energy turns into heat, sound and damage, so the collision is inelastic.', '{"1": "The book uses this as a near-elastic collision.", "2": "That is the elastic case with equal masses.", "3": "The book uses this as the elastic light-hits-massive case."}'),
  ('PHY-0564', 'Force and motion', 'Collisions in two dimensions', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 41 (§2.10)', 'For an elastic collision in two dimensions, the book applies conservation of momentum:', '["only along the direction of the first ball", "separately along the x-axis and the y-axis, together with conservation of kinetic energy", "by adding the magnitudes of all momenta", "without using kinetic energy"]', 1, 'Momentum is a vector, so it is resolved into rectangular components and conserved along both x and y; for an elastic collision kinetic energy is also conserved (Eqs. 2.35–2.37).', '{"0": "Momentum must be conserved in both perpendicular directions.", "2": "Momentum is a vector, so it must be resolved into components.", "3": "Kinetic energy conservation is used because the collision is elastic."}'),
  ('PHY-0565', 'Force and motion', 'Rocket propulsion', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 44 (§2.12)', 'A rocket gains momentum because:', '["the expelled gases push on the air behind it", "its fuel becomes lighter, which pulls it upward", "it gains momentum equal and opposite to that of the hot gases it expels", "gravity decreases as it rises"]', 2, 'The rocket expels high-speed gases backward; by conservation of momentum it gains equal momentum in the opposite direction. It works at great heights where there is little or no air.', '{"0": "Rockets work even where there is no air.", "1": "Losing mass does not by itself produce a force.", "3": "The thrust comes from the gas expulsion, not from weaker gravity."}'),
  ('PHY-0566', 'Force and motion', 'Rocket propulsion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 44 (§2.12)', 'A rocket of mass 5.0 × 10⁴ kg ejects 200 kg of gas per second at 3000 m s⁻¹ relative to the rocket. The acceleration produced by this thrust alone (ignoring gravity) is:', '["0.06 m s⁻²", "1.2 m s⁻²", "6.0 × 10⁵ m s⁻²", "12 m s⁻²"]', 3, 'Thrust = mv = 200 × 3000 = 6.0 × 10⁵ N. a = mv/M = 6.0 × 10⁵ / 5.0 × 10⁴ = 12 m s⁻².', '{"0": "This divides the ejection speed by the mass.", "1": "This is ten times too small: 6.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 5.0 × 10⁴ = 12.", "2": "6.0 × 10⁵ N is the thrust, which must be divided by the mass."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0567', 'Force and motion', 'Rocket propulsion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 2 Force and Motion, p. 44 (§2.12)', 'Why are rockets often made of several stages linked together?', '["So that the rocket can push against the air more strongly", "To keep the mass constant throughout the flight", "Because one stage cannot carry any fuel", "So that empty stages can be discarded, leaving less mass for the remaining rockets to accelerate"]', 3, 'More than 80% of a rocket''s launch mass is fuel. Discarding a stage once it has done its job leaves less mass, so the remaining rockets can take the spacecraft higher and faster.', '{"0": "Rockets do not push against air.", "1": "Dropping stages deliberately reduces the mass.", "2": "Each stage carries fuel."}'),
  ('PHY-0568', 'Circular motion', 'Angular measurement', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 49 (§3.1)', 'One radian is the angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc whose length is:', '["equal to the radius of the circle", "equal to the diameter of the circle", "equal to the circumference", "one quarter of the circumference"]', 0, 'If arc AB equals the radius r, the angle at the centre is one radian, the SI unit of angle.', '{"1": "An arc equal to the diameter subtends 2 rad.", "2": "The full circumference subtends 2π rad.", "3": "A quarter circle subtends π/2 rad."}'),
  ('PHY-0569', 'Circular motion', 'Angular measurement', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 50 (§3.1)', 'One revolution is equal to:', '["2π rad", "π rad", "π/2 rad", "57.3 rad"]', 0, 'In one revolution the point covers S = 2πr, so θ = S/r = 2π rad = 360°.', '{"1": "π rad is half a revolution (180°).", "2": "π/2 rad is a quarter revolution (90°).", "3": "1 rad is about 57.3°; that does not make 57.3 rad a revolution."}'),
  ('PHY-0570', 'Circular motion', 'Angular measurement', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 50 (§3.1)', 'One radian is approximately equal to:', '["3.14°", "57.3°", "90°", "180°"]', 1, '1 rad = 360°/2π ≈ 57.3°.', '{"0": "3.14 is the value of π, not the degrees in a radian.", "2": "90° is π/2 rad ≈ 1.57 rad.", "3": "180° is π rad."}'),
  ('PHY-0571', 'Circular motion', 'Angular displacement', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 50 (§3.1)', 'A point on the rim of a wheel of radius 0.40 m moves through an arc of 1.2 m. The angular displacement of the wheel is:', '["0.33 rad", "0.48 rad", "1.6 rad", "3.0 rad"]', 3, 'θ = S/r = 1.2/0.40 = 3.0 rad.', '{"0": "0.33 = 0.40/1.2, the inverse ratio.", "1": "0.48 = 1.2 × 0.40; the arc must be divided by the radius.", "2": "1.6 = 1.2 + 0.40; the arc must be divided by the radius, not added to it."}'),
  ('PHY-0572', 'Circular motion', 'Angular displacement', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 49 (§3.1)', 'According to the book, the direction of a small angular displacement is:', '["along the tangent to the circle", "along the radius towards the centre", "in the plane of rotation, opposite to the motion", "along the axis of rotation, given by the right-hand rule"]', 3, 'Grasp the axis with the right hand, fingers curling in the sense of rotation; the thumb points in the direction of the angular displacement, along the axis.', '{"0": "The tangent gives the direction of linear velocity.", "1": "That is the direction of centripetal acceleration.", "2": "Angular quantities point along the axis, not in the plane."}'),
  ('PHY-0573', 'Circular motion', 'Angular displacement', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 49 (§3.1)', 'By the book''s convention, angular displacement is taken as positive when the rotation is:', '["clockwise", "along the radius", "counter-clockwise", "faster than 1 rad s⁻¹"]', 2, 'The book assigns a positive sign to Δθ when the sense of rotation is counter-clockwise.', '{"0": "Clockwise rotation is taken as negative.", "1": "Angular displacement is not measured along the radius.", "3": "The sign depends on the sense of rotation, not on speed."}'),
  ('PHY-0574', 'Circular motion', 'Angular velocity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 50 (§3.1)', 'The SI unit of angular velocity is:', '["rev min⁻¹", "m s⁻¹", "rad s⁻²", "rad s⁻¹"]', 3, 'Angular velocity is the rate of change of angular displacement, measured in radians per second.', '{"0": "rpm is used sometimes, but it is not the SI unit.", "1": "m s⁻¹ is the unit of linear velocity.", "2": "rad s⁻² is the unit of angular acceleration."}'),
  ('PHY-0575', 'Circular motion', 'Angular velocity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 54 (Example 3.2)', 'A turntable rotates at 45 rpm. Its angular velocity is about:', '["0.75 rad s⁻¹", "4.7 rad s⁻¹", "45 rad s⁻¹", "283 rad s⁻¹"]', 1, 'f = 45/60 = 0.75 rev s⁻¹. ω = 2πf = 2π × 0.75 ≈ 4.7 rad s⁻¹.', '{"0": "0.75 rev s⁻¹ is the frequency; it must be multiplied by 2π.", "2": "45 is in revolutions per minute, not radians per second.", "3": "This multiplies 45 by 2π without converting minutes to seconds."}'),
  ('PHY-0576', 'Circular motion', 'Angular acceleration', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 51 (§3.1)', 'A fan''s angular velocity rises from 0 to 30 rad s⁻¹ in 6 s. Its average angular acceleration is:', '["0.2 rad s⁻²", "5 rad s⁻²", "30 rad s⁻²", "180 rad s⁻²"]', 1, 'α = Δω/Δt = (30 − 0)/6 = 5 rad s⁻².', '{"0": "0.2 = 6/30, the inverse ratio.", "2": "30 rad s⁻¹ is the final angular velocity.", "3": "180 = 30 × 6; the change in ω must be divided by time."}'),
  ('PHY-0577', 'Circular motion', 'Linear and angular quantities', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 52 (§3.1)', 'A point at distance r from the axis of a body rotating with angular velocity ω has linear speed:', '["v = rω", "v = ω/r", "v = r/ω", "v = rω²"]', 0, 'From S = rθ, dividing by Δt gives v = rω (Eq. 3.7).', '{"1": "Speed increases with distance from the axis, so r multiplies ω.", "2": "This has units of m s rad⁻¹, not of speed.", "3": "rω² is the centripetal acceleration."}'),
  ('PHY-0578', 'Circular motion', 'Linear and angular quantities', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 52 (§3.1)', 'A wheel of radius 0.5 m rotates at 8 rad s⁻¹. The linear speed of a point on its rim is:', '["4 m s⁻¹", "8.5 m s⁻¹", "16 m s⁻¹", "32 m s⁻¹"]', 0, 'v = rω = 0.5 × 8 = 4 m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "Quantities must be multiplied, not added.", "2": "16 = 8/0.5; v = rω, not ω/r.", "3": "32 = rω², which is the centripetal acceleration in m s⁻²."}'),
  ('PHY-0579', 'Circular motion', 'Linear and angular quantities', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 52 (§3.1)', 'A wheel''s angular acceleration is 4 rad s⁻². The tangential acceleration of a point 0.25 m from the axis is:', '["0.0625 m s⁻²", "1 m s⁻²", "4 m s⁻²", "16 m s⁻²"]', 1, 'a_t = rα = 0.25 × 4 = 1 m s⁻² (Eq. 3.8).', '{"0": "This squares r.", "2": "4 rad s⁻² is the angular acceleration.", "3": "16 = 4/0.25, dividing instead of multiplying."}'),
  ('PHY-0580', 'Circular motion', 'Linear and angular quantities', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 52 (§3.1)', 'For a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis, which quantity is the SAME for all its points at any instant?', '["Linear speed", "Tangential acceleration", "Angular velocity", "Distance travelled along the path"]', 2, 'All points of a rigid body share the same angular displacement, angular velocity and angular acceleration; linear quantities depend on distance from the axis.', '{"0": "v = rω, so points farther from the axis move faster.", "1": "a_t = rα depends on r.", "3": "S = rθ depends on r."}'),
  ('PHY-0581', 'Circular motion', 'Linear and angular quantities', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 52 (§3.1)', 'The linear velocity of a point moving on a circle is directed:', '["towards the centre of the circle", "away from the centre of the circle", "along the axis of rotation", "along the tangent to the circle at that point"]', 3, 'As Δt → 0, the arc P₁P₂ points along the tangent at P₁, so the linear velocity is tangential; it is also called tangential velocity.', '{"0": "That is the direction of centripetal force and acceleration.", "1": "Velocity is perpendicular to the radius, not along it.", "2": "Angular velocity, not linear velocity, lies along the axis."}'),
  ('PHY-0582', 'Circular motion', 'Equations of angular motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 52 (§3.1)', 'A wheel starts from rest with a constant angular acceleration of 2 rad s⁻². Through what angle does it turn in 5 s?', '["5 rad", "10 rad", "25 rad", "50 rad"]', 2, 'θ = ωᵢt + ½αt² = 0 + ½(2)(5)² = 25 rad.', '{"0": "This uses ½αt instead of ½αt².", "1": "10 rad s⁻¹ is its angular velocity after 5 s.", "3": "This uses αt² without the ½."}'),
  ('PHY-0583', 'Circular motion', 'Equations of angular motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 53 (Example 3.1)', 'A grinder rotating at 4.0 rev s⁻¹ is switched off and stops uniformly in 10 s. How many revolutions does it make while stopping?', '["0.4 rev", "10 rev", "20 rev", "40 rev"]', 2, 'α = (0 − 4.0)/10 = −0.4 rev s⁻². θ = ωᵢt + ½αt² = 40 − ½(0.4)(100) = 40 − 20 = 20 rev. (Equivalently, average speed 2 rev s⁻¹ × 10 s.)', '{"0": "0.4 rev s⁻² is the size of the deceleration.", "1": "This uses a quarter of the correct average speed.", "3": "40 rev would be at a constant 4 rev s⁻¹ for 10 s; it is slowing down."}'),
  ('PHY-0584', 'Circular motion', 'Equations of angular motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 52 (§3.1)', 'Which angular equation corresponds to the linear equation 2aS = v_f² − v_i²?', '["2αθ = ω_f² − ω_i²", "θ = ω_i t + ½αt²", "ω_f = ω_i + αt", "τ = Iα"]', 0, 'Replacing S, v and a by θ, ω and α in 2aS = v_f² − v_i² gives 2αθ = ω_f² − ω_i².', '{"1": "This corresponds to S = v_i t + ½at².", "2": "This corresponds to v_f = v_i + at.", "3": "This is the rotational form of F = ma, not an equation of angular motion."}'),
  ('PHY-0585', 'Circular motion', 'Equations of angular motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 53 (§3.1)', 'The equations of angular motion can be handled as scalar equations only when:', '["the axis of rotation is fixed, so all angular vectors have the same direction", "the body rotates at constant angular velocity", "the body is a point mass", "the angle is measured in degrees"]', 0, 'The book says the angular equations hold when the axis of rotation is fixed, so all angular vectors share a direction and can be manipulated as scalars.', '{"1": "They include angular acceleration, so ω need not be constant.", "2": "They also apply to rigid bodies.", "3": "The condition concerns the axis, not the unit."}'),
  ('PHY-0586', 'Circular motion', 'Centripetal force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 53 (§3.2)', 'A body moves in a circle at constant speed. The force that keeps it on the circle:', '["acts along the velocity and increases the speed", "acts outward from the centre", "acts towards the centre and changes only the direction of the velocity", "is zero because the speed is constant"]', 2, 'A force perpendicular to the velocity changes direction but not speed. Acting along the radius towards the centre, it is called the centripetal (centre-seeking) force.', '{"0": "A force along the velocity changes the speed, which stays constant here.", "1": "The required (centripetal) force acts inward.", "3": "A changing direction means a changing velocity, which needs a force."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0587', 'Circular motion', 'Centripetal force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 53 (§3.2)', 'A ball whirled on a string moves in a horizontal circle. If the string snaps, the ball moves:', '["radially outward from the centre", "along the circle as before", "straight towards the centre", "along the tangent to the circle at the point where it snapped"]', 3, 'Once the centripetal force (tension) is gone, the ball continues in a straight line with its instantaneous velocity, which is tangent to the circle.', '{"0": "There is no outward force pushing it; it simply continues with its velocity.", "1": "Without the centripetal force it cannot keep turning.", "2": "The inward force has disappeared."}'),
  ('PHY-0588', 'Circular motion', 'Centripetal force', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 54 (§3.2)', 'A 0.5 kg stone moves in a circle of radius 2 m at 4 m s⁻¹. The centripetal force on it is:', '["1 N", "4 N", "8 N", "16 N"]', 1, 'F = mv²/r = 0.5 × 4²/2 = 0.5 × 16/2 = 4 N.', '{"0": "1 N uses v instead of v².", "2": "8 N is mv²/r with m = 1 kg.", "3": "16 N = mv²r; the radius must divide, not multiply."}'),
  ('PHY-0589', 'Circular motion', 'Centripetal force', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 54 (§3.2)', 'If the speed of a body in uniform circular motion is doubled while the radius stays the same, the centripetal force becomes:', '["twice as large", "half as large", "unchanged", "four times as large"]', 3, 'F = mv²/r; doubling v multiplies v² by 4, so the force becomes four times as large.', '{"0": "Force depends on v², not on v.", "1": "The force increases with speed.", "2": "The force depends on the speed."}'),
  ('PHY-0590', 'Circular motion', 'Centripetal force', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 54 (§3.2)', 'Centripetal force can also be written in terms of angular velocity as:', '["F = mrω²", "F = mω/r", "F = mr²ω", "F = mω²/r"]', 0, 'Since v = rω, F = mv²/r = m(rω)²/r = mrω² (Eq. 3.13).', '{"1": "Substituting v = rω into mv²/r gives mrω².", "2": "This has the form of angular momentum, mr²ω.", "3": "The radius multiplies, it does not divide."}'),
  ('PHY-0591', 'Circular motion', 'Centripetal acceleration', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 54 (Example 3.2)', 'A disc 20 cm in diameter spins at 300 rpm. The centripetal acceleration of a point on its rim is about:', '["2.5 m s⁻²", "3.1 m s⁻²", "99 m s⁻²", "197 m s⁻²"]', 2, 'f = 300/60 = 5 Hz, ω = 2πf = 31.4 rad s⁻¹. a = rω² = 0.10 × (31.4)² ≈ 99 m s⁻².', '{"0": "This uses f = 5 rev s⁻¹ in place of ω.", "1": "This uses rω instead of rω²; ω must be squared.", "3": "This uses the diameter (0.20 m) instead of the radius (0.10 m)."}'),
  ('PHY-0592', 'Circular motion', 'Vertical circle', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 54 (Example 3.3)', 'A ball of mass m moves in a vertical circle of radius r on a string. At the top of the circle its speed is v. The tension in the string there is:', '["m(v²/r + g)", "mg", "m(v²/r − g)", "mv²/r"]', 2, 'At the top both tension T and weight mg point towards the centre: T + mg = mv²/r, so T = m(v²/r − g).', '{"0": "At the bottom the tension is m(v²/r + g); at the top weight helps provide the centripetal force.", "1": "The tension depends on the speed.", "3": "Weight also points towards the centre at the top, so it supplies part of the force."}'),
  ('PHY-0593', 'Circular motion', 'Vertical circle', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 54 (Example 3.3)', 'A bucket of water is swung in a vertical circle of radius 0.9 m. What is the least speed at the top for the water to stay in (g = 10 m s⁻²)?', '["0.3 m s⁻¹", "3 m s⁻¹", "4.2 m s⁻¹", "9 m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'At the minimum speed the tension (or the bucket''s push) is zero and weight alone provides the centripetal force: mg = mv²/r, so v = √(gr) = √(10 × 0.9) = 3 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This divides instead of taking √(gr).", "2": "This is √(2gr), which is not the condition at the top.", "3": "9 = gr; the speed is √(gr)."}'),
  ('PHY-0594', 'Circular motion', 'Examples of centripetal force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 55 (§3.2)', 'For a coin resting on a rotating turntable without sliding, the centripetal force is provided by:', '["the coin''s weight", "the normal force of the turntable", "friction between the coin and the turntable", "an outward centrifugal force"]', 2, 'The book: for an object placed on a turntable, friction is the centripetal force.', '{"0": "Weight acts vertically, not towards the centre.", "1": "The normal force is vertical.", "3": "The book describes the needed force as inward, supplied by friction."}'),
  ('PHY-0595', 'Circular motion', 'Examples of centripetal force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 55 (§3.2)', 'The centripetal force that keeps the Moon in orbit around the Earth is provided by:', '["friction", "magnetic force", "tension", "gravitational attraction"]', 3, 'The book lists the gravitational force as the cause of the Earth orbiting the Sun and the Moon and satellites revolving around the Earth.', '{"0": "There is no contact between the Moon and the Earth.", "1": "The book gives the magnetic force for charged particles, not for the Moon.", "2": "Tension needs a string."}'),
  ('PHY-0596', 'Circular motion', 'Banked roads', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 55 (§3.2)', 'Why are roads banked at turns, with the outer edge kept higher?', '["Because friction alone may not supply enough centripetal force at high speed, especially on a slippery road", "To increase the friction between the tyres and the road", "To make vehicles slow down at turns", "To let rain water drain from the inner edge"]', 0, 'On a turn the centripetal force comes from friction. On a slippery road or at high speed friction may fall short, so the vehicle may skid; banking the road helps provide the needed centripetal force.', '{"1": "Banking does not depend on increasing friction.", "2": "Banking helps vehicles turn safely; it does not slow them.", "3": "Drainage is not the reason the book gives."}'),
  ('PHY-0597', 'Circular motion', 'Centrifuge', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 55 (§3.2)', 'In a laboratory centrifuge, what happens to the particles in a spinning sample tube?', '["All particles collect evenly through the tube.", "The lighter particles move outward to the bottom.", "The denser particles settle at the bottom (outer end) and the lighter ones rise to the top.", "The particles move in straight lines out of the tube."]', 2, 'When the needed centripetal force is not supplied, matter moves away from the centre. Denser particles go to the outer end (bottom) of the tubes and lighter ones to the top.', '{"0": "The point of a centrifuge is to separate them.", "1": "Lighter particles stay nearer the axis, at the top.", "3": "They stay in the tube but separate."}'),
  ('PHY-0598', 'Circular motion', 'Centrifuge', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 56 (§3.2)', 'In a cream separator, cream collects near the axis of rotation because:', '["cream is heavier than milk", "friction pushes the cream inward", "the cream is attracted by the axle", "the lighter particles gather near the axis while the heavier particles move outward"]', 3, 'Milk is a mixture of light and heavy particles. When it is whirled, the light particles (cream) gather near the axis and the heavy ones go outward.', '{"0": "The heavy particles move outward; cream is the lighter part.", "1": "The separation is due to rotation, not friction.", "2": "There is no such attraction."}'),
  ('PHY-0599', 'Circular motion', 'Artificial satellites', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 56 (§3.3)', 'The critical velocity for a satellite in an orbit just above the Earth''s surface is about:', '["3.0 km s⁻¹", "7.9 km s⁻¹", "9.8 km s⁻¹", "11.2 km s⁻¹"]', 1, 'For a low orbit, g = v²/R, so v = √(gR) = √(9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶) ≈ 7.9 × 10³ m s⁻¹ = 7.9 km s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This is far too slow to stay in a low orbit.", "2": "9.8 m s⁻² is g, not an orbital speed.", "3": "11.2 km s⁻¹ is the escape velocity, not the orbital speed."}'),
  ('PHY-0600', 'Circular motion', 'Artificial satellites', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 56 (§3.3)', 'The period of a satellite orbiting just above the Earth''s surface is about:', '["8 minutes", "24 hours", "27 days", "84 minutes"]', 3, 'T = 2πR/v = 2 × 3.14 × 6.4 × 10⁶ / 7.9 × 10³ ≈ 5.1 × 10³ s ≈ 84 min.', '{"0": "The orbit is 2π × 6400 km long; at 7.9 km s⁻¹ it takes much longer.", "1": "24 h is the period of a geostationary orbit, far above the surface.", "2": "About 27 days is the Moon''s period."}'),
  ('PHY-0601', 'Circular motion', 'Orbital velocity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 57 (§3.3)', 'The orbital speed of a satellite at distance r from the Earth''s centre is v = √(GM/r). This shows that the orbital speed:', '["does not depend on the mass of the satellite", "increases with the radius of the orbit", "depends on the satellite''s mass", "is the same for all orbits"]', 0, 'Equating GmM/r² to mv²/r gives v = √(GM/r); the satellite mass m cancels, so it does not affect the orbit.', '{"1": "v ∝ 1/√r, so it decreases as r increases.", "2": "The satellite mass cancels out of the equation.", "3": "It depends on r."}'),
  ('PHY-0602', 'Circular motion', 'Orbital velocity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 56 (§3.3)', 'Compared with a satellite in a low orbit, a satellite in a higher circular orbit has:', '["a lower speed and a longer period", "a higher speed and a shorter period", "the same speed but a longer period", "a higher speed and a longer period"]', 0, 'The book: the higher the satellite, the slower the required speed and the longer it takes to complete one revolution.', '{"1": "Gravity is weaker higher up, so less speed is needed.", "2": "v = √(GM/r) changes with r.", "3": "Higher orbits need less speed."}'),
  ('PHY-0603', 'Circular motion', 'Orbital velocity', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 57 (§3.3)', 'Satellite X orbits at radius r and satellite Y at radius 4r around the same planet. The ratio of their orbital speeds vₓ : v_Y is:', '["1 : 2", "2 : 1", "4 : 1", "16 : 1"]', 1, 'v = √(GM/r) ∝ 1/√r. vₓ/v_Y = √(4r/r) = √4 = 2, so 2 : 1.', '{"0": "The inner satellite moves faster, not slower.", "2": "v ∝ 1/√r, not 1/r.", "3": "This uses r² instead of √r."}'),
  ('PHY-0604', 'Circular motion', 'Weightlessness', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 57 (§3.3)', 'Astronauts in an orbiting satellite feel weightless because:', '["there is no gravity at the height of the satellite", "the satellite and everything in it are falling freely towards the Earth, so the supporting force is zero", "the Moon''s pull balances the Earth''s pull", "they are too far from the Earth to have weight"]', 1, 'The satellite accelerates towards the Earth as a freely falling body. Gravity provides exactly the centripetal force, so the normal (supporting) force on objects inside is zero: apparent weightlessness.', '{"0": "Gravity still acts and supplies the centripetal force.", "2": "That is not the reason.", "3": "Low satellites are only a few hundred km up, where g is still large."}'),
  ('PHY-0605', 'Circular motion', 'Artificial gravity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 58 (§3.3)', 'How can artificial gravity be created in a spaceship?', '["By switching off its engines", "By filling it with denser air", "By spinning the spaceship about its own axis so occupants are pressed against the outer rim", "By keeping it still in space"]', 2, 'Rotating the spaceship makes the outer rim push the astronauts inward (centripetal force). They press on this ''floor'' as they would on the Earth.', '{"0": "That makes everyone weightless.", "1": "Air density does not produce weight.", "3": "A still spaceship gives no force on its occupants."}'),
  ('PHY-0606', 'Circular motion', 'Artificial gravity', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 59 (§3.3)', 'A rotating space station has radius 250 m. About what frequency of rotation gives artificial gravity equal to g = 9.8 m s⁻² at the rim?', '["0.032 rev s⁻¹ (about 1.9 rev per minute)", "0.039 rev s⁻¹", "0.20 rev s⁻¹", "6.3 rev s⁻¹"]', 0, 'f = (1/2π)√(g/R) = (1/6.28)√(9.8/250) = (1/6.28)(0.198) ≈ 0.032 rev s⁻¹.', '{"1": "This uses g/R without the square root.", "2": "0.20 is ω in rad s⁻¹, not the frequency in rev s⁻¹.", "3": "This is far too fast."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0607', 'Circular motion', 'Moment of inertia', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 59 (§3.4)', 'Moment of inertia plays the same role in rotational motion as:', '["force plays in linear motion", "velocity plays in linear motion", "momentum plays in linear motion", "mass plays in linear motion"]', 3, 'In τ = Iα, the rotational form of F = ma, the moment of inertia I takes the place of mass m.', '{"0": "Torque is the rotational analogue of force.", "1": "Angular velocity is the analogue of velocity.", "2": "Angular momentum is the analogue of momentum."}'),
  ('PHY-0608', 'Circular motion', 'Moment of inertia', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 59 (§3.4)', 'The rotational analogue of Newton''s second law F = ma is:', '["τ = Iα", "L = Iω", "v = rω", "F = mrω²"]', 0, 'Multiplying F = mrα by r gives τ = mr²α = Iα; torque replaces force, angular acceleration replaces acceleration, and I = mr² replaces mass.', '{"1": "This defines angular momentum.", "2": "This relates linear and angular speed.", "3": "This is the centripetal force."}'),
  ('PHY-0609', 'Circular motion', 'Moment of inertia', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 60 (§3.4)', 'A point mass of 2 kg is 0.3 m from an axis. Its moment of inertia about that axis is:', '["0.09 kg m²", "0.18 kg m²", "0.6 kg m²", "1.2 kg m²"]', 1, 'I = mr² = 2 × (0.3)² = 2 × 0.09 = 0.18 kg m².', '{"0": "0.09 is r² only, without the mass.", "2": "0.6 = m × r; r must be squared.", "3": "This uses r = 0.6 m."}'),
  ('PHY-0610', 'Circular motion', 'Moment of inertia', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 60 (§3.4)', 'The moment of inertia of a body depends on:', '["its mass only", "its mass and how that mass is distributed about the axis of rotation", "its angular velocity", "the torque applied to it"]', 1, 'I = Σmᵢrᵢ² depends on each mass and its distance from the axis, so on the mass and its distribution.', '{"0": "Two bodies of equal mass can have different I if their mass is spread differently.", "2": "I does not depend on how fast the body spins.", "3": "Torque produces angular acceleration; it does not change I."}'),
  ('PHY-0611', 'Circular motion', 'Moment of inertia', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 60 (§3.4)', 'A thin ring and a solid disc have the same mass M and radius R. About their central axes:', '["the disc has the larger moment of inertia", "both have the same moment of inertia", "the ring has the larger moment of inertia (MR² against ½MR²)", "the moment of inertia depends only on R, so both are zero"]', 2, 'For a thin ring all mass is at R, so I = MR². For a solid disc, I = ½MR², since much of its mass is closer to the axis.', '{"0": "In the disc much of the mass is near the axis.", "1": "Their mass distributions differ.", "3": "Moment of inertia is not zero for a body of finite size."}'),
  ('PHY-0612', 'Circular motion', 'Moment of inertia', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 59 (§3.4)', 'A net torque of 6 N m acts on a wheel of moment of inertia 0.5 kg m². The angular acceleration produced is:', '["0.083 rad s⁻²", "3 rad s⁻²", "6 rad s⁻²", "12 rad s⁻²"]', 3, 'α = τ/I = 6/0.5 = 12 rad s⁻².', '{"0": "0.083 = I/τ, the inverse.", "1": "3 = τ × I; α = τ/I.", "2": "This ignores the moment of inertia."}'),
  ('PHY-0613', 'Circular motion', 'Angular momentum', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 61 (§3.5)', 'The angular momentum of a particle about a point is defined as:', '["L = p × r", "L = r·p", "L = mv", "L = r × p"]', 3, 'L = r × p, magnitude rp sin θ = mrv sin θ, perpendicular to the plane of r and p.', '{"0": "The order is r × p; reversing it reverses the direction.", "1": "The scalar product gives a scalar, but L is a vector.", "2": "mv is linear momentum."}'),
  ('PHY-0614', 'Circular motion', 'Angular momentum', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 61 (§3.5)', 'The SI unit of angular momentum is:', '["kg m² s⁻¹ (J s)", "kg m s⁻¹", "kg m² s⁻²", "N m"]', 0, 'L = mrv has units kg × m × m s⁻¹ = kg m² s⁻¹, which equals J s.', '{"1": "This is the unit of linear momentum.", "2": "This is the unit of energy or torque.", "3": "N m is the unit of torque."}'),
  ('PHY-0615', 'Circular motion', 'Angular momentum', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 61 (§3.5)', 'A 0.2 kg ball moves in a circle of radius 0.5 m at 3 m s⁻¹. Its angular momentum about the centre is:', '["0.15 kg m² s⁻¹", "0.3 kg m² s⁻¹", "0.6 kg m² s⁻¹", "0.9 kg m² s⁻¹"]', 1, 'For circular motion, θ = 90° between r and v, so L = mrv = 0.2 × 0.5 × 3 = 0.3 kg m² s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This includes an extra factor of ½.", "2": "0.6 kg m s⁻¹ is its linear momentum mv.", "3": "This uses r = 1.5 m."}'),
  ('PHY-0616', 'Circular motion', 'Angular momentum', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 61 (§3.5)', 'For a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis, the angular momentum is:', '["L = ½Iω²", "L = Iω", "L = Iα", "L = mω"]', 1, 'Summing mᵢrᵢ²ω over all particles gives L = (Σmᵢrᵢ²)ω = Iω.', '{"0": "½Iω² is rotational kinetic energy.", "2": "Iα is the torque.", "3": "The moment of inertia, not just the mass, is needed."}'),
  ('PHY-0617', 'Circular motion', 'Conservation of angular momentum', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 62 (§3.6)', 'The angular momentum of a system remains constant when:', '["no external force acts on it", "its moment of inertia stays constant", "no external torque acts on it", "it rotates at constant speed"]', 2, 'The law of conservation of angular momentum: if no external torque acts on a system, its total angular momentum stays constant.', '{"0": "A force through the axis gives no torque; the condition is about torque.", "1": "I can change while L stays constant.", "3": "Constant speed is a result, not the condition."}'),
  ('PHY-0618', 'Circular motion', 'Conservation of angular momentum', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 63 (§3.6)', 'A spinning ice skater pulls her arms in close to her body. What happens?', '["Her moment of inertia increases and her angular speed decreases.", "Her angular momentum increases.", "Her angular speed stays the same.", "Her moment of inertia decreases and her angular speed increases."]', 3, 'With no external torque, I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂. Folding the arms reduces I, so ω increases.', '{"0": "Pulling the arms in brings mass closer to the axis.", "1": "With no external torque, angular momentum stays the same.", "2": "I changes, so ω must change to keep Iω constant."}'),
  ('PHY-0619', 'Circular motion', 'Conservation of angular momentum', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 62 (§3.6)', 'A turntable with moment of inertia 6 kg m² rotates at 2 rad s⁻¹. A person on it pulls in his arms, reducing the total moment of inertia to 4 kg m². The new angular velocity is:', '["1.33 rad s⁻¹", "2 rad s⁻¹", "3 rad s⁻¹", "12 rad s⁻¹"]', 2, 'I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂ → 6 × 2 = 4 × ω₂ → ω₂ = 3 rad s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This multiplies by 4/6 instead of 6/4.", "1": "Angular velocity changes when I changes.", "3": "12 kg m² s⁻¹ is the angular momentum, not the angular velocity."}'),
  ('PHY-0620', 'Circular motion', 'Conservation of angular momentum', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 63 (§3.6)', 'A diver curls her body in mid-air after leaving the springboard. This lets her:', '["spin faster, because her moment of inertia decreases", "spin slower, because her moment of inertia increases", "gain angular momentum from the air", "fall faster towards the water"]', 0, 'Curling up decreases I; to keep L = Iω constant, ω increases, so she spins faster. Stretching out before entry slows the spin.', '{"1": "Curling up brings mass closer to the axis, reducing I.", "2": "No external torque of note acts, so L stays constant.", "3": "Curling does not change her free-fall acceleration."}'),
  ('PHY-0621', 'Circular motion', 'Conservation of angular momentum', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 63 (§3.6)', 'According to the book, if polar ice melts and the water flows towards the equator, the length of the day would:', '["decrease slightly, because the Earth spins faster", "increase slightly, because the Earth''s moment of inertia increases", "stay the same, because the Earth''s mass is unchanged", "double, because angular momentum doubles"]', 1, 'Water moving away from the axis increases the Earth''s moment of inertia; with L conserved, ω decreases, so the day becomes slightly longer.', '{"0": "Moving mass away from the axis increases I, so ω decreases.", "2": "I depends on how the mass is distributed, not just its total.", "3": "Angular momentum stays the same."}'),
  ('PHY-0622', 'Circular motion', 'Flywheel and gyroscope', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 64 (§3.6)', 'A flywheel is used mainly to:', '["increase friction in a machine", "change the direction of rotation repeatedly", "store rotational energy and smooth out fluctuations in output", "reduce the mass of a vehicle"]', 2, 'The book describes a flywheel as a heavy wheel on an axle used to store rotational energy, smooth output fluctuations and provide stability.', '{"0": "A flywheel is not a brake.", "1": "Its large angular momentum resists changes.", "3": "A flywheel is a heavy wheel."}'),
  ('PHY-0623', 'Circular motion', 'Flywheel and gyroscope', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 3 Circular and Rotational Motion, p. 64 (§3.6)', 'A spinning gyroscope keeps the direction of its axis because:', '["it has no moment of inertia", "gravity does not act on a spinning object", "friction locks its axis in place", "its large angular momentum can be changed in direction only by a large torque"]', 3, 'The axis of a spinning object does not change orientation unless an external torque acts. A fast gyroscope has large angular momentum, so a large torque would be needed; this is why it is used in navigation of aeroplanes, submarines and spacecraft.', '{"0": "It has a large moment of inertia.", "1": "Gravity acts on it, but it keeps its orientation.", "2": "It is mounted to rotate freely."}'),
  ('PHY-0624', 'Work and energy', 'Work by a constant force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 69 (§4.1)', 'A student pushes hard against a fixed wall for one minute. The work done on the wall is:', '["zero, because there is no displacement", "large, because a large force is applied", "equal to the force times one minute", "negative, because the wall pushes back"]', 0, 'W = Fd cos θ. With d = 0, no work is done even though the muscles tire.', '{"1": "Work needs displacement in the direction of the force.", "2": "Work is force times displacement, not force times time.", "3": "With no displacement, no work is done at all."}'),
  ('PHY-0625', 'Work and energy', 'Work by a constant force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 70 (§4.1)', 'A force of 50 N pulls a box 4 m along the floor. The force acts at 60° to the direction of motion. The work done by the force is:', '["12.5 J", "100 J", "173 J", "200 J"]', 1, 'W = Fd cos θ = 50 × 4 × cos 60° = 200 × 0.5 = 100 J.', '{"0": "12.5 divides the force by the distance.", "2": "173 J uses sin 60° instead of cos 60°.", "3": "200 J would be the work if the force were along the motion."}'),
  ('PHY-0626', 'Work and energy', 'Work by a constant force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 70 (§4.1)', 'When the angle between force and displacement is more than 90°, the work done by the force is:', '["positive", "zero", "always maximum", "negative"]', 3, 'W = Fd cos θ, and cos θ is negative for 90° < θ ≤ 180°, so the work is negative.', '{"0": "Work is positive only when θ < 90°.", "1": "Work is zero when θ = 90° exactly.", "2": "Work is maximum when θ = 0°."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0627', 'Work and energy', 'Work by a constant force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 70 (§4.1)', 'A satellite moves in a circular orbit. The work done on it by the Earth''s gravitational force is:', '["positive, because gravity pulls the satellite", "negative, because the satellite moves away from Earth", "equal to mgh", "zero, because the force is perpendicular to the displacement"]', 3, 'In a circular orbit the gravitational force acts along the radius, perpendicular to the velocity. With θ = 90°, W = Fd cos 90° = 0.', '{"0": "The pull is along the radius, at right angles to the motion.", "1": "In a circular orbit the distance from Earth stays the same.", "2": "mgh applies to a vertical displacement near the surface."}'),
  ('PHY-0628', 'Work and energy', 'Work by a constant force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 70 (§4.1)', 'Work is a scalar quantity because it is:', '["the scalar (dot) product of force and displacement", "the vector product of force and displacement", "measured in joules", "always positive"]', 0, 'W = F·d = Fd cos θ. The scalar product of two vectors is a scalar.', '{"1": "The vector product would give a vector.", "2": "A unit does not decide whether a quantity is scalar.", "3": "Work can be negative, but is still a scalar."}'),
  ('PHY-0629', 'Work and energy', 'Work from a graph', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 70 (§4.1)', 'On a force–displacement graph, the work done is given by:', '["the area under the graph", "the slope of the graph", "the maximum force times the time", "the intercept on the force axis"]', 0, 'For a constant force, the area of the rectangle is Fd = W. For a variable force, the area under the F cos θ–d curve between two points gives the work.', '{"1": "The slope of a force–displacement graph has units of N m⁻¹, not of work.", "2": "Work is not force × time.", "3": "The intercept is a force, not work."}'),
  ('PHY-0630', 'Work and energy', 'Work from a graph', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 71 (Example 4.1)', 'A force is constant at 8 N from d = 0 to 3 m, then falls steadily to zero at d = 5 m. The total work done is:', '["16 J", "24 J", "32 J", "40 J"]', 2, 'Area = rectangle (8 N × 3 m = 24 J) + triangle (½ × 2 m × 8 N = 8 J) = 32 J.', '{"0": "16 J uses the full rectangle for the last 2 m instead of the triangle.", "1": "24 J is only the rectangle (8 × 3); the triangle must be added.", "3": "40 J treats the whole 5 m as a rectangle of 8 N."}'),
  ('PHY-0631', 'Work and energy', 'Work by a variable force', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 71 (§4.2)', 'For a variable force, the book finds the total work by:', '["multiplying the maximum force by the total displacement", "multiplying the average speed by the time", "using only the force at the starting point", "dividing the path into small steps, treating the force as constant in each, and adding F cos θ Δd as Δd → 0"]', 3, 'Over each small step the force is nearly constant, so ΔW = F cos θ Δd. Adding these and letting Δd → 0 gives the exact work, the area under the F cos θ–d curve.', '{"0": "That overestimates the work when the force varies.", "1": "That gives a distance, not work.", "2": "The force changes along the path."}'),
  ('PHY-0632', 'Work and energy', 'Conservative forces', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 73 (§4.3)', 'A force is called conservative if:', '["it always does positive work", "it conserves the kinetic energy of the body", "it acts only on stationary bodies", "the work it does between two points is independent of the path, or zero round a closed path"]', 3, 'If the work done by a force between two points does not depend on the path followed (equivalently, the work round a closed path is zero), the force is conservative.', '{"0": "Gravity does negative work when an object is lifted.", "1": "Kinetic energy can change under a conservative force.", "2": "Conservative forces act on moving bodies."}'),
  ('PHY-0633', 'Work and energy', 'Conservative forces', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 73 (§4.3)', 'Which of the following is a non-conservative force?', '["Kinetic friction", "Gravitational force", "Electrostatic force", "Elastic spring force"]', 0, 'Kinetic friction always opposes motion, so its work depends on the length of the path. A longer path means more work, so it is non-conservative.', '{"1": "The book shows gravity''s work is path-independent.", "2": "The book lists it as conservative.", "3": "The book lists it as conservative."}'),
  ('PHY-0634', 'Work and energy', 'Conservative forces', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 72 (§4.3)', 'A 2 kg book is moved from the floor to a shelf 1.5 m higher by three different paths. Taking g = 10 m s⁻², the work done by gravity is:', '["+30 J for every path", "−30 J for every path", "−30 J only for the vertical path", "different for each path"]', 1, 'Gravity is conservative, so the work depends only on the change in height: W = −mgh = −2 × 10 × 1.5 = −30 J for each path.', '{"0": "The displacement is against gravity, so the work is negative.", "2": "Gravity is conservative, so all paths give the same work.", "3": "Work by gravity does not depend on the path."}'),
  ('PHY-0635', 'Work and energy', 'Conservative forces', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 73 (§4.3)', 'A ball is thrown up and caught again at the same height. Ignoring air resistance, the total work done by gravity during the round trip is:', '["2mgh", "zero", "−mgh", "mgh"]', 1, 'Gravity is conservative: the work over a closed path is zero (−mgh up, +mgh down).', '{"0": "Gravity does −mgh going up and +mgh coming down.", "2": "That is only the upward half.", "3": "That is only the downward half."}'),
  ('PHY-0636', 'Work and energy', 'Power', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 74 (§4.4)', 'Power is defined as:', '["the total work done", "force times displacement", "the capacity to do work", "the rate at which work is done"]', 3, 'Average power P = ΔW/Δt, the rate of doing work. Its SI unit is the watt (1 J s⁻¹).', '{"0": "Power tells how fast work is done, not how much.", "1": "That is work.", "2": "That is energy."}'),
  ('PHY-0637', 'Work and energy', 'Power', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 74 (§4.4)', 'Power can also be written as:', '["P = F·v", "P = F × v", "P = F/v", "P = ½Fv²"]', 0, 'P = ΔW/Δt = F·Δd/Δt = F·v, the scalar product of force and velocity.', '{"1": "Power is a scalar, so it uses the scalar product.", "2": "Power increases with both force and velocity.", "3": "This does not have the units of power."}'),
  ('PHY-0638', 'Work and energy', 'Power', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 74 (§4.4)', 'A car''s engine provides a forward force of 2000 N while the car moves at a steady 25 m s⁻¹. The power delivered is:', '["2.5 kW", "5 kW", "50 kW", "80 W"]', 2, 'P = Fv = 2000 × 25 = 50 000 W = 50 kW.', '{"0": "This drops the factor of 20.", "1": "This drops a factor of 10.", "3": "80 = 2000/25; power is F × v."}'),
  ('PHY-0639', 'Work and energy', 'Power', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 74 (Example 4.2)', 'A 50 kg girl climbs a staircase of vertical height 6.0 m in 10 s. Her power output is about (g = 9.8 m s⁻²):', '["30 W", "49 W", "294 W", "2940 W"]', 2, 'Work = mgh = 50 × 9.8 × 6.0 = 2940 J. P = W/t = 2940/10 = 294 W.', '{"0": "This leaves out g.", "1": "This divides by 60 instead of 10.", "3": "2940 J is the work done; it must be divided by 10 s."}'),
  ('PHY-0640', 'Work and energy', 'Power', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 74 (§4.4)', 'One kilowatt-hour equals:', '["3.6 × 10⁶ J", "3.6 × 10³ J", "6.0 × 10⁴ J", "1000 J"]', 0, '1 kW h = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J = 3.6 MJ.', '{"1": "3600 J is one watt for one hour.", "2": "1 kW for one minute.", "3": "1000 J is one kilowatt for one second."}'),
  ('PHY-0641', 'Work and energy', 'Power', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 74 (§4.4)', 'A 1.5 kW heater runs for 4 hours a day for 30 days. The energy used is:', '["6 kW h", "45 kW h", "180 kW h", "180 J"]', 2, 'Energy = power × time = 1.5 kW × 4 h × 30 = 180 kW h.', '{"0": "6 kW h is the use for one day.", "1": "This ignores the 4 hours per day.", "3": "The unit must be kW h, not J."}'),
  ('PHY-0642', 'Work and energy', 'Energy', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 74 (§4.5)', 'Energy is defined as:', '["the capacity of a body to do work", "the rate of doing work", "force per unit area", "mass times velocity"]', 0, 'Energy is a body''s capacity to do work; kinetic and potential energy are its two basic mechanical forms.', '{"1": "That is power.", "2": "That is pressure.", "3": "That is momentum."}'),
  ('PHY-0643', 'Work and energy', 'Kinetic energy', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 75 (§4.5)', 'A 4 kg ball moves at 5 m s⁻¹. Its kinetic energy is:', '["10 J", "20 J", "50 J", "100 J"]', 2, 'KE = ½mv² = ½ × 4 × 25 = 50 J.', '{"0": "10 J uses v instead of v².", "1": "20 kg m s⁻¹ is its momentum.", "3": "100 J leaves out the factor ½."}'),
  ('PHY-0644', 'Work and energy', 'Kinetic energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 75 (§4.5)', 'If a body''s speed is tripled, its kinetic energy becomes:', '["3 times as large", "6 times as large", "9 times as large", "27 times as large"]', 2, 'KE = ½mv²; tripling v multiplies v² by 9.', '{"0": "KE depends on v², not v.", "1": "Doubling the factor 3 does not give the change in v².", "3": "KE goes as v², not v³."}'),
  ('PHY-0645', 'Work and energy', 'Kinetic energy', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 75 (§4.5)', 'A light body and a heavy body have the same linear momentum. Which has more kinetic energy?', '["The heavy body", "The light body", "Both have equal kinetic energy", "It cannot be decided without knowing the speeds"]', 1, 'KE = ½mv² = p²/2m. For the same p, the smaller mass has the larger kinetic energy.', '{"0": "KE = p²/2m, so for equal p the larger mass gives smaller KE.", "2": "Equal momentum does not mean equal KE.", "3": "With equal p, KE = p²/2m depends only on m."}'),
  ('PHY-0646', 'Work and energy', 'Kinetic energy', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 75 (§4.5)', 'The momentum of a body is doubled while its mass stays the same. Its kinetic energy becomes:', '["twice as large", "unchanged", "half as large", "four times as large"]', 3, 'KE = p²/2m. Doubling p multiplies p² by 4, so KE becomes four times as large.', '{"0": "KE = p²/2m depends on p².", "1": "KE changes with momentum.", "2": "KE increases with momentum."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0647', 'Work and energy', 'Kinetic energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 75 (Example 4.3)', 'A 1000 kg car moving at 20 m s⁻¹ is braked to rest in 50 m. The average braking force is:', '["400 N", "2000 N", "4000 N", "8000 N"]', 2, 'KE = ½(1000)(20)² = 2.0 × 10⁵ J. This equals the work against the braking force: f × 50 = 2.0 × 10⁵, so f = 4000 N.', '{"0": "400 = 20000/50; the KE is 200 000 J, not 20 000 J.", "1": "This halves the correct force.", "3": "This leaves out the ½ in the kinetic energy."}'),
  ('PHY-0648', 'Work and energy', 'Potential energy', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 76 (§4.5)', 'The energy stored in a compressed spring is called:', '["kinetic energy", "gravitational potential energy", "chemical energy", "elastic potential energy"]', 3, 'Potential energy can be due to a body''s constrained state; the energy stored in a compressed or stretched spring is elastic potential energy.', '{"0": "The spring is not moving.", "1": "It is due to the spring''s compressed state, not its height.", "2": "It is a mechanical energy due to deformation."}'),
  ('PHY-0649', 'Work and energy', 'Absolute potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 76 (§4.5)', 'The book defines the absolute gravitational potential energy of an object at a point as the work done by the gravitational force in moving the object from that point to:', '["infinity, where the gravitational force becomes zero", "the surface of the Earth", "the centre of the Earth", "the highest point it can reach"]', 0, 'Absolute PE is the work done by gravity in displacing the object from that position to infinity, where the gravitational force is zero.', '{"1": "mgh is measured from a chosen level, but absolute PE is measured from infinity.", "2": "The reference point is infinity, not the centre.", "3": "The reference is fixed at infinity."}'),
  ('PHY-0650', 'Work and energy', 'Absolute potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 78 (§4.5)', 'The absolute gravitational potential energy of a mass m at distance r from the Earth''s centre is:', '["U = +GMm/r", "U = −GMm/r", "U = −GMm/r²", "U = mgr"]', 1, 'Adding the work over small steps from r to infinity gives U = −GMm/r. The minus sign shows the field is attractive.', '{"0": "The negative sign is needed because the field is attractive.", "2": "GMm/r² is the gravitational force, not the potential energy.", "3": "mgh is only valid close to the Earth''s surface."}'),
  ('PHY-0651', 'Work and energy', 'Absolute potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 78 (§4.5)', 'As a body is raised to a greater distance r from the Earth''s centre, its absolute potential energy U = −GMm/r:', '["decreases, becoming more negative", "stays constant", "increases, becoming less negative and approaching zero at infinity", "becomes positive"]', 2, 'U = −GMm/r is negative. As r increases, U becomes less negative, i.e. it increases, reaching zero at infinite distance.', '{"0": "As r grows, GMm/r gets smaller, so U is less negative.", "1": "U depends on r.", "3": "U stays negative and only reaches zero at infinity."}'),
  ('PHY-0652', 'Work and energy', 'Absolute potential energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 76 (§4.5)', 'The formula PE = mgh is valid only:', '["for any height above the Earth", "at the centre of the Earth", "for bodies in orbit", "near the Earth''s surface, where the gravitational force is nearly constant"]', 3, 'The relation mgh assumes a constant gravitational force, which is true only near the Earth''s surface; far away the force varies as the inverse square of distance.', '{"0": "At large distances g changes as 1/r².", "1": "The book uses it near the surface.", "2": "Orbiting bodies are at large distances where g is not constant."}'),
  ('PHY-0653', 'Work and energy', 'Escape velocity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 79 (§4.6)', 'Escape velocity is the:', '["initial velocity a body needs to go out of the Earth''s gravitational field", "velocity of a satellite in a low orbit", "maximum speed of a falling body in air", "speed of the Earth around the Sun"]', 0, 'The book defines escape velocity as the initial velocity with which an object goes out of the Earth''s gravitational field.', '{"1": "That is the orbital (critical) velocity, about 7.9 km s⁻¹.", "2": "That is terminal velocity.", "3": "That is an orbital speed, unrelated."}'),
  ('PHY-0654', 'Work and energy', 'Escape velocity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 79 (§4.6)', 'The escape velocity from the surface of a planet of mass M and radius R is:', '["√(GM/R)", "√(2GM/R)", "2GM/R", "√(GM/2R)"]', 1, 'Setting initial KE equal to the increase in PE: ½mv² = GMm/R, so v_esc = √(2GM/R) = √(2gR).', '{"0": "This is the orbital speed just above the surface.", "2": "This is v², not v.", "3": "The factor 2 belongs in the numerator."}'),
  ('PHY-0655', 'Work and energy', 'Escape velocity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 79 (§4.6)', 'The escape velocity from the Earth''s surface is about:', '["1.1 km s⁻¹", "3 × 10⁵ km s⁻¹", "7.9 km s⁻¹", "11 km s⁻¹"]', 3, 'v_esc = √(2gR) = √(2 × 9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶) ≈ 1.1 × 10⁴ m s⁻¹ ≈ 11 km s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This is ten times too small.", "1": "This is the speed of light.", "2": "7.9 km s⁻¹ is the speed for a low circular orbit."}'),
  ('PHY-0656', 'Work and energy', 'Escape velocity', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 79 (§4.6)', 'The escape velocity from the Earth''s surface is about √2 times the speed of a satellite in a very low orbit. Which statement explains this?', '["v_esc = 2v_orbit because the satellite must go round twice", "Escape velocity depends on the mass of the body, orbital speed does not", "v_esc = √(2GM/R) while v_orbit = √(GM/R)", "Orbital speed includes the Earth''s rotation"]', 2, 'v_esc/v_orbit = √(2GM/R)/√(GM/R) = √2. So 7.9 km s⁻¹ × 1.41 ≈ 11.2 km s⁻¹.', '{"0": "The factor is √2, not 2.", "1": "Neither depends on the body''s mass.", "3": "The formulas compared do not include rotation."}'),
  ('PHY-0657', 'Work and energy', 'Escape velocity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 79 (§4.6)', 'Does the escape velocity of a rocket depend on the mass of the rocket?', '["Yes; a heavier rocket needs a larger escape velocity.", "Yes; a lighter rocket needs a larger escape velocity.", "Only if it is launched at an angle", "No; the mass of the rocket cancels from ½mv² = GMm/R."]', 3, 'In ½mv² = GMm/R the rocket mass m cancels, so v_esc = √(2GM/R) depends only on the planet''s mass and radius.', '{"0": "m appears on both sides and cancels.", "1": "The rocket''s mass does not appear in √(2GM/R).", "2": "The escape speed does not depend on the rocket''s mass."}'),
  ('PHY-0658', 'Work and energy', 'Work–energy theorem', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 80 (§4.7)', 'The work–energy theorem states that the work done on a body by the net force equals:', '["the change in its kinetic energy", "the change in its potential energy", "its total energy", "its momentum"]', 0, 'From F d = ½mv_f² − ½mv_i², the work done on a body by a net force equals the change in its kinetic energy.', '{"1": "The theorem relates the net force to kinetic energy.", "2": "It equals the change in KE, not the total energy.", "3": "Momentum relates to impulse."}'),
  ('PHY-0659', 'Work and energy', 'Work–energy theorem', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 80 (§4.7)', 'A 2 kg body at rest is acted on by a net force of 10 N over 5 m. Its final speed is:', '["5 m s⁻¹", "7.1 m s⁻¹", "10 m s⁻¹", "50 m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'W = Fd = 10 × 5 = 50 J = ½mv² = ½(2)v². So v² = 50 and v ≈ 7.1 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This forgets the ½ in ½mv² (50 = 2v²).", "2": "10 = √100 treats the mass as 1 kg; the mass is 2 kg.", "3": "50 J is the work done; it must be converted to speed."}'),
  ('PHY-0660', 'Work and energy', 'Work–energy theorem', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 80 (§4.7)', 'A force reduces a ball''s speed from 14 m s⁻¹ to 6 m s⁻¹. The work done by this force on the ball is:', '["positive, because a force acted on the ball", "negative, because the kinetic energy decreases", "zero, because the ball is still moving", "impossible to tell without the mass"]', 1, 'By the work–energy theorem W = ΔKE. The KE falls, so W is negative: the force acted opposite to the motion.', '{"0": "Work is positive only if KE increases.", "2": "The KE has changed, so the work is not zero.", "3": "The sign follows from the change in KE alone."}'),
  ('PHY-0661', 'Work and energy', 'Interconversion of energy', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 81 (§4.8)', 'A body falls freely from height h with no air resistance. Halfway down, its kinetic energy is:', '["mgh", "0", "½mgh", "2mgh"]', 2, 'Total energy stays mgh. Halfway down, PE = mg(h/2) = ½mgh, so KE = mgh − ½mgh = ½mgh.', '{"0": "The full mgh is the KE just before it hits the ground.", "1": "It has gained speed by halfway.", "3": "The total energy is only mgh."}'),
  ('PHY-0662', 'Work and energy', 'Interconversion of energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 81 (§4.8)', 'A 0.5 kg stone falls freely from rest through 20 m (g = 10 m s⁻²). Just before landing its speed is:', '["10 m s⁻¹", "14 m s⁻¹", "20 m s⁻¹", "200 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'Loss of PE = gain of KE: mgh = ½mv², so v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 20) = √400 = 20 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This uses v² = gh instead of 2gh.", "1": "This uses v = √(gh) = √200 instead of √(2gh).", "3": "200 = 2gh, which is v², not v."}'),
  ('PHY-0663', 'Work and energy', 'Interconversion of energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 82 (§4.8)', 'When a body falls through air, the loss in its potential energy equals:', '["the gain in kinetic energy only", "the work done against friction only", "the gain in kinetic energy minus the work against friction", "the gain in kinetic energy plus the work done against friction"]', 3, 'With air friction f over height h: mgh = ½mv² + fh. Loss in PE = gain in KE + work against friction.', '{"0": "That is true only without friction.", "1": "Some of the PE also becomes KE.", "2": "The friction work must be added, not subtracted."}'),
  ('PHY-0664', 'Work and energy', 'Interconversion of energy', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 83 (Example 4.6)', 'A 2 kg object falls from rest through 20 m and hits the ground at 18 m s⁻¹ (g = 10 m s⁻²). The average air resistance is:', '["0 N", "3.8 N", "20 N", "76 J"]', 1, 'mgh = 400 J; ½mv² = ½(2)(18)² = 324 J. Work against air = 76 J = f × 20 m, so f = 3.8 N.', '{"0": "The object is slower than it would be in free fall, so air resistance acted.", "2": "20 N is the object''s weight.", "3": "76 J is the energy lost to air, not the force."}'),
  ('PHY-0665', 'Work and energy', 'Interconversion of energy', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 82 (§4.8)', 'A roller-coaster car starts from rest at the top of a 5.0 m high hill. Ignoring friction, its speed when it is 1.8 m above the ground is (g = 10 m s⁻²):', '["6 m s⁻¹", "8 m s⁻¹", "10 m s⁻¹", "64 m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'Drop in height = 5.0 − 1.8 = 3.2 m. ½mv² = mgΔh → v = √(2 × 10 × 3.2) = √64 = 8 m s⁻¹, whatever the mass.', '{"0": "6 m s⁻¹ uses a drop of 1.8 m instead of 3.2 m.", "2": "10 m s⁻¹ is its speed at ground level (a drop of 5.0 m).", "3": "64 is v², not v."}'),
  ('PHY-0666', 'Work and energy', 'Interconversion of energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 82 (§4.8)', 'Without friction, a body falling from rest at height h has total mechanical energy:', '["mgh at every point of its fall", "0 at the top and mgh at the bottom", "2mgh at the bottom", "mgh at the top only"]', 0, 'At every point PE + KE = mg(h − x) + mgx = mgh, so the total is constant: loss in PE equals gain in KE.', '{"1": "At the top it has PE = mgh.", "2": "Energy is not created during the fall.", "3": "It stays mgh all the way down."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0667', 'Work and energy', 'Conservative forces', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 4 Work, Energy and Power, p. 73 (§4.3)', 'A sledge is dragged from P to Q along two different paths over a rough horizontal surface. The second path is twice as long. The work done against kinetic friction along the second path is:', '["the same as along the first path", "twice as much as along the first path", "half as much", "zero along a closed path"]', 1, 'Kinetic friction does work equal to the friction force times the length of the path, so doubling the path doubles the work.', '{"0": "Friction is non-conservative; its work depends on path length.", "2": "A longer path means more work against friction.", "3": "For friction, work round a closed path is not zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0668', 'Physics of solids', 'Classification of solids', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 88 (§5.1)', 'In which type of solid are the atoms or molecules arranged in a regular pattern that repeats throughout the material?', '["Amorphous solids", "Glassy solids", "Crystalline solids", "Polymeric solids"]', 2, 'Crystalline solids have a regular, long-range ordered arrangement of atoms, molecules or ions, e.g. metals, ceramics, quartz and diamond.', '{"0": "Amorphous means without form; there is no regular arrangement.", "1": "Glassy solids are amorphous, with a disordered structure.", "3": "Polymers are intermediate between order and disorder."}'),
  ('PHY-0669', 'Physics of solids', 'Classification of solids', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 88 (§5.1)', 'Which of these has a definite melting point?', '["Ordinary glass", "Plastic", "Fused silica", "Quartz"]', 3, 'Crystalline solids such as quartz, calcite, sugar, mica and diamond change abruptly from order to disorder at a definite melting point.', '{"0": "Glass softens gradually and has no definite melting point.", "1": "The book lists plastic as amorphous, with no definite melting point.", "2": "Fused silica is amorphous."}'),
  ('PHY-0670', 'Physics of solids', 'Classification of solids', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 88 (§5.1)', 'Why does ordinary glass not have a sharp melting point?', '["Its atoms do not vibrate at all.", "It is a crystalline solid with very strong bonds.", "It melts only above 2000 °C.", "It is amorphous: it has no regular arrangement, so it softens gradually into a viscous liquid."]', 3, 'Amorphous (glassy) solids are like liquids with their disordered structure frozen in. On heating, glass gradually softens into a paste before becoming a very viscous liquid, so there is no definite melting point.', '{"0": "All solids'' atoms vibrate; this is not the reason.", "1": "Glass is not crystalline.", "2": "The book says glass becomes a very viscous liquid at about 800 °C, gradually."}'),
  ('PHY-0671', 'Physics of solids', 'Classification of solids', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 88 (§5.1)', 'In a crystalline solid, atoms are not static. According to the book:', '["each atom vibrates about a fixed point with an amplitude that increases with temperature", "atoms move freely through the whole crystal", "atoms stay completely still until the solid melts", "atomic vibrations decrease as temperature rises"]', 0, 'Each atom vibrates about a fixed point with an amplitude that increases with temperature; at the melting point the vibrations become large enough to break the structure.', '{"1": "That describes a liquid or a gas.", "2": "The atoms vibrate at all temperatures.", "3": "The amplitude increases with temperature."}'),
  ('PHY-0672', 'Physics of solids', 'Classification of solids', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 88 (§5.1)', 'Polymeric solids are described in the book as:', '["perfectly crystalline with long-range order", "completely disordered like liquids", "partially or poorly crystalline, with a structure between order and disorder", "made only of metal atoms"]', 2, 'Polymers have a structure intermediate between order and disorder and are classed as partially or poorly crystalline. Examples: polythene, polystyrene, nylon.', '{"0": "That describes crystalline solids.", "1": "That describes amorphous solids.", "3": "Polymers are mainly carbon chains with H, O, N and others."}'),
  ('PHY-0673', 'Physics of solids', 'Classification of solids', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 89 (§5.1)', 'Which of the following is a polymer?', '["Diamond", "Common salt", "Nylon", "Copper"]', 2, 'Polythene, polystyrene and nylon are the book''s examples of polymers formed by polymerisation into long-chain molecules.', '{"0": "Diamond is crystalline.", "1": "Sodium chloride is crystalline.", "3": "Copper is a crystalline metal."}'),
  ('PHY-0674', 'Physics of solids', 'Deformation', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 89 (§5.2)', 'The ability of a body to return to its original shape after the deforming force is removed is called:', '["plasticity", "viscosity", "ductility", "elasticity"]', 3, 'When the external force is removed, the atoms go back to their equilibrium positions and the body regains its shape; this ability is called elasticity.', '{"0": "Plasticity is permanent deformation.", "1": "Viscosity is the fluid friction between layers.", "2": "Ductility is the ability to be drawn into wires."}'),
  ('PHY-0675', 'Physics of solids', 'Stress', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 90 (§5.3)', 'Stress is defined as:', '["the force applied per unit area to produce a change in shape, volume or length", "the change in length per unit original length", "the force per unit extension", "the change in volume per unit volume"]', 0, 'Stress σ = F/A, measured in N m⁻² (pascal).', '{"1": "That is tensile strain.", "2": "That is the spring constant.", "3": "That is volumetric strain."}'),
  ('PHY-0676', 'Physics of solids', 'Stress', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 90 (§5.3)', 'A stress that changes the shape of a body is called:', '["tensile stress", "shear stress", "volume stress", "compressive strain"]', 1, 'The book: a stress that changes length is tensile stress, one that changes volume is volume stress, and one that changes shape is shear stress.', '{"0": "Tensile stress changes the length.", "2": "Volume stress changes the volume.", "3": "Strain is the deformation, not the stress."}'),
  ('PHY-0677', 'Physics of solids', 'Strain', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 90 (§5.3)', 'The SI unit of strain is:', '["N m⁻²", "m", "none, because strain is a ratio of two lengths", "N"]', 2, 'Strain = ΔL/L₀, a ratio of lengths, so it is dimensionless and has no unit.', '{"0": "N m⁻² is the unit of stress.", "1": "Strain is a fraction of the length, not a length.", "3": "N is the unit of force."}'),
  ('PHY-0678', 'Physics of solids', 'Stress and strain', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 90 (§5.3)', 'A wire 2.0 m long stretches by 1.0 mm under a load. The tensile strain is:', '["0.5", "2.0 × 10⁻³", "2.0 × 10³", "5.0 × 10⁻⁴"]', 3, 'Strain = ΔL/L₀ = (1.0 × 10⁻³ m)/(2.0 m) = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴.', '{"0": "0.5 = 1.0/2.0 without converting mm to m.", "1": "This uses ΔL = 4 mm; the extension is 1.0 mm.", "2": "This is L/ΔL, the inverse."}'),
  ('PHY-0679', 'Physics of solids', 'Shear strain', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 90 (§5.3)', 'For a small shear deformation, the shear strain γ = Δx/y is approximately equal to:', '["the shear angle θ in degrees", "the change in volume per unit volume", "zero, because shape changes do not count as strain", "the shear angle θ in radians"]', 3, 'γ = Δx/y = tan θ, and for small θ in radians tan θ ≈ θ, so γ ≈ θ.', '{"0": "For small angles tan θ ≈ θ only when θ is in radians.", "1": "That is volumetric strain.", "2": "Shear strain measures change of shape."}'),
  ('PHY-0680', 'Physics of solids', 'Young''s modulus', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 91 (§5.3)', 'Young''s modulus is defined as:', '["tensile stress divided by tensile strain", "tensile strain divided by tensile stress", "force divided by extension", "stress multiplied by strain"]', 0, 'Y = (F/A)/(ΔL/L₀) = tensile stress/tensile strain. Its unit is the same as stress, N m⁻².', '{"1": "This is the inverse.", "2": "That is the spring constant k.", "3": "The modulus is a ratio, not a product."}'),
  ('PHY-0681', 'Physics of solids', 'Young''s modulus', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 91 (§5.3)', 'A wire of cross-sectional area 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² and length 3.0 m stretches by 1.5 mm under a 100 N load. Its Young''s modulus is:', '["1.0 × 10¹¹ N m⁻²", "1.0 × 10⁸ N m⁻²", "2.5 × 10¹⁰ N m⁻²", "5.0 × 10⁷ N m⁻²"]', 0, 'Stress = 100/(2.0 × 10⁻⁶) = 5.0 × 10⁷ N m⁻². Strain = 1.5 × 10⁻³/3.0 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴. Y = 5.0 × 10⁷/5.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.0 × 10¹¹ N m⁻².', '{"1": "This uses a strain of 0.5 instead of 5.0 × 10⁻⁴.", "2": "This uses a strain of 2.0 × 10⁻³ (taking L as 0.75 m).", "3": "5.0 × 10⁷ N m⁻² is the stress; it must be divided by the strain."}'),
  ('PHY-0682', 'Physics of solids', 'Young''s modulus', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 91 (Table 5.1)', 'From the book''s table of elastic constants, which material has the largest Young''s modulus?', '["Steel", "Diamond", "Aluminium", "Lead"]', 1, 'Table 5.1 gives diamond the largest Young''s modulus (about 1120 × 10⁹ N m⁻²), far more than steel or tungsten.', '{"0": "Steel is about 200 × 10⁹ N m⁻², well below diamond.", "2": "Aluminium is about 70 × 10⁹ N m⁻².", "3": "Lead has one of the smallest values in the table."}'),
  ('PHY-0683', 'Physics of solids', 'Young''s modulus experiment', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 91 (§5.4)', 'In Searle''s apparatus, why are two wires of the same material and length used?', '["To double the extension produced", "Because one wire alone cannot carry a load", "The reference wire cancels effects such as temperature change and sagging of the support, so only the test wire''s extension is measured.", "To find Young''s modulus of two materials at once"]', 2, 'The reference wire, kept under constant load, hangs beside the test wire; the spirit level between them records only the extra extension of the test wire, so changes affecting both wires cancel.', '{"0": "Only the test wire is loaded to find the extension.", "1": "A single wire can carry the load.", "3": "Both wires are of the same material."}'),
  ('PHY-0684', 'Physics of solids', 'Young''s modulus experiment', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 92 (§5.4)', 'In the Searle''s experiment, the slope of the stress–strain graph gives:', '["the extension of the wire", "the elastic potential energy", "the breaking stress", "the Young''s modulus of the wire"]', 3, 'Within the proportional region the stress–strain graph is a straight line; its slope (stress/strain) is Young''s modulus.', '{"0": "Extension is found from the micrometer readings.", "1": "Energy is found from the area under a force–extension graph.", "2": "The breaking stress is beyond the straight-line region."}'),
  ('PHY-0685', 'Physics of solids', 'Elastic and plastic deformation', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 92 (§5.5)', 'Hooke''s law states that, up to the proportional limit:', '["strain is directly proportional to stress", "strain is inversely proportional to stress", "stress is independent of strain", "strain is proportional to the square of stress"]', 0, 'In region OA of the stress–strain curve, strain (deformation) is directly proportional to stress (load); this is Hooke''s law.', '{"1": "Larger stress gives larger strain.", "2": "The two are proportional.", "3": "The relation is linear."}'),
  ('PHY-0686', 'Physics of solids', 'Elastic and plastic deformation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 93 (§5.5)', 'If a wire is loaded beyond its elastic limit (yield point) and the load is removed:', '["it returns exactly to its original length", "it is permanently deformed and does not regain its original length", "it breaks immediately", "it becomes shorter than before"]', 1, 'Beyond the yield point the material behaves plastically: it is permanently changed and does not recover its original shape after the stress is removed.', '{"0": "That happens only below the elastic limit.", "2": "It breaks only at the fracture point D.", "3": "A stretched wire stays longer, not shorter."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0687', 'Physics of solids', 'Elastic and plastic deformation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 93 (§5.5)', 'The ultimate tensile strength (UTS) of a material is:', '["the stress at which Hooke''s law stops being obeyed", "the stress at the yield point", "the maximum stress it can withstand", "the stress at which it is first loaded"]', 2, 'Point C on the curve is the UTS, the maximum stress the material can withstand; it is regarded as the material''s nominal strength.', '{"0": "That is the proportional limit.", "1": "That is the elastic limit.", "3": "The UTS is the peak of the curve (point C)."}'),
  ('PHY-0688', 'Physics of solids', 'Ductile and brittle substances', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 93 (§5.5)', 'Which of these is a brittle substance?', '["Copper", "Lead", "Wrought iron", "Glass"]', 3, 'Brittle substances break just after the elastic limit is reached; glass and high-carbon steel are the book''s examples.', '{"0": "Copper is ductile.", "1": "Lead is ductile.", "2": "Wrought iron is ductile."}'),
  ('PHY-0689', 'Physics of solids', 'Ductile and brittle substances', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 93 (§5.5)', 'A ductile substance is one that:', '["undergoes plastic deformation until it breaks", "breaks just after the elastic limit", "has no elastic region", "cannot be stretched"]', 0, 'Substances that undergo plastic deformation until they break are ductile, e.g. lead, copper and wrought iron.', '{"1": "That describes a brittle substance.", "2": "Ductile materials have an elastic region first.", "3": "Ductile substances can be stretched a lot."}'),
  ('PHY-0690', 'Physics of solids', 'Strain energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 94 (§5.6)', 'The work done in stretching a spring obeying Hooke''s law by an extension x is:', '["kx²", "½kx²", "kx", "½kx"]', 1, 'The force rises uniformly from 0 to F = kx, so the average force is ½F. W = ½Fx = ½kx², the area under the force–extension graph.', '{"0": "The average force is ½F, so a factor ½ is needed.", "2": "kx is the force, not the work.", "3": "This has units of force, not energy."}'),
  ('PHY-0691', 'Physics of solids', 'Strain energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 94 (§5.6)', 'A spring of spring constant 400 N m⁻¹ is stretched by 5 cm within its elastic limit. The elastic potential energy stored is:', '["0.5 J", "1.0 J", "20 J", "5000 J"]', 0, 'U = ½kx² = ½ × 400 × (0.05)² = 200 × 0.0025 = 0.5 J.', '{"1": "1.0 J leaves out the ½.", "2": "20 N is the force kx, not the energy.", "3": "This uses x = 5 instead of 0.05 m."}'),
  ('PHY-0692', 'Physics of solids', 'Strain energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 94 (§5.6)', 'On a force–extension graph for a material within its limit of proportionality, the area under the line represents:', '["the Young''s modulus", "the spring constant", "the work done, which is stored as elastic potential energy", "the breaking force"]', 2, 'The area under a force–extension graph equals the work done to stretch the material, which equals the elastic PE stored in it (½Fx).', '{"0": "Young''s modulus is the slope of the stress–strain graph.", "1": "The spring constant is the slope of the force–extension graph.", "3": "The area is an energy, not a force."}'),
  ('PHY-0693', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Archimedes'' principle', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 94 (§5.7)', 'Archimedes'' principle states that a body totally or partially immersed in a fluid experiences an upthrust equal to:', '["its own weight", "the volume of the body", "the pressure at its bottom face", "the weight of the fluid it displaces"]', 3, 'The upthrust on an immersed body equals the weight of fluid it displaces: F = ρgV.', '{"0": "That is true only when the body floats.", "1": "Upthrust is a force, not a volume.", "2": "Upthrust is a force equal to the weight of fluid displaced."}'),
  ('PHY-0694', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Archimedes'' principle', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 95 (§5.7)', 'In the book''s derivation, the upthrust on a submerged cylinder arises because:', '["the liquid pressure on its bottom face is greater than on its top face", "the liquid pressure on its top face is greater", "the sides of the cylinder push it up", "the cylinder loses mass in the liquid"]', 0, 'P = ρgh grows with depth. The force on the bottom (ρgh₂A) is greater than on the top (ρgh₁A); the difference ρgAh = ρgV is the upthrust.', '{"1": "Pressure increases with depth, so the bottom face has more pressure.", "2": "Side forces cancel horizontally.", "3": "Its mass does not change."}'),
  ('PHY-0695', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Archimedes'' principle', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 95 (Example 5.2)', 'A metal block of volume 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ m³ is fully immersed in water (ρ = 1000 kg m⁻³, g = 10 m s⁻²). The upthrust on it is:', '["0.2 N", "2.0 N", "20 N", "2000 N"]', 1, 'Upthrust = ρgV = 1000 × 10 × 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.0 N, whatever the block is made of.', '{"0": "This uses g = 1 m s⁻², missing a factor of 10.", "2": "This is ten times too large.", "3": "This multiplies ρ and g without the volume."}'),
  ('PHY-0696', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Floatation', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 96 (§5.7)', 'A floating object:', '["displaces fluid whose volume equals its own volume", "displaces fluid whose weight equals its own weight", "has no upthrust acting on it", "weighs more than the upthrust on it"]', 1, 'Principle of floatation: a floating object displaces a fluid having weight equal to the weight of the object.', '{"0": "A floating object is only partly immersed, so it displaces less than its own volume.", "2": "Upthrust supports it.", "3": "Then it would sink."}'),
  ('PHY-0697', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Floatation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 96 (§5.7)', 'A submarine dives below the surface by:', '["emptying its tanks so its weight decreases", "increasing its volume", "filling its tanks with seawater so its weight becomes greater than the upthrust", "switching off its engines"]', 2, 'Filling the tanks with seawater increases the submarine''s weight; once its weight exceeds the upthrust, it sinks. Emptying the tanks lets it rise.', '{"0": "Emptying the tanks makes it rise.", "1": "Its hull volume stays the same.", "3": "Diving is controlled by the tanks."}'),
  ('PHY-0698', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Floatation', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 97 (Example 5.3)', 'A balloon of total weight 50 N (fabric plus helium) has a volume of 8 m³. Air has density 1.25 kg m⁻³ (g = 10 m s⁻²). The largest extra load it can lift is:', '["10 N", "50 N", "100 N", "150 N"]', 1, 'Upthrust = ρ_air gV = 1.25 × 10 × 8 = 100 N. Extra load = 100 − 50 = 50 N.', '{"0": "This uses g = 1 m s⁻² in part of the calculation.", "2": "100 N is the upthrust; the balloon''s own weight must be subtracted.", "3": "The weight is subtracted, not added."}'),
  ('PHY-0699', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Fluid flow', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 98 (§5.8)', 'In streamline (laminar) flow:', '["the particles move in eddies and whirlpools", "streamlines cross each other", "the velocity at each point changes abruptly", "every particle passing a point follows the same path as the particles that passed it earlier"]', 3, 'In laminar flow the motion is smooth and regular; each particle follows the path (streamline) of earlier particles, and streamlines cannot cross.', '{"0": "That is turbulent flow.", "1": "Streamlines never cross in steady flow.", "2": "That describes turbulent flow."}'),
  ('PHY-0700', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Fluid flow', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 98 (§5.8)', 'Fluid flow becomes turbulent when:', '["the flow speed rises above a certain value", "the fluid is at rest", "the flow speed is very small", "the fluid has zero viscosity"]', 0, 'Above a certain velocity the motion becomes unsteady and irregular, with eddies and whirlpools: turbulent flow.', '{"1": "A fluid at rest has no flow at all.", "2": "Slow flow is usually laminar.", "3": "Turbulence appears in real flows above a certain speed."}'),
  ('PHY-0701', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Ideal fluid', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 98 (§5.8)', 'Which of the following is NOT a property of an ideal fluid?', '["It is incompressible.", "It has a high viscosity.", "Its flow is steady.", "It is non-viscous."]', 1, 'An ideal fluid is non-viscous, incompressible and in steady flow. High viscosity is the opposite of an ideal fluid.', '{"0": "An ideal fluid has constant density.", "2": "Steady flow is one of the conditions.", "3": "An ideal fluid has no friction between layers."}'),
  ('PHY-0702', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Rate of flow', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 99 (§5.8)', 'Water flows at 2 m s⁻¹ through a pipe of cross-sectional area 5 × 10⁻⁴ m². The volume flow rate is:', '["1 × 10⁻³ m³ s⁻¹", "1 × 10⁻⁴ m³ s⁻¹", "2.5 × 10⁻⁴ m³ s⁻¹", "4 × 10³ m³ s⁻¹"]', 0, 'Rate of flow = Av = 5 × 10⁻⁴ × 2 = 1 × 10⁻³ m³ s⁻¹ (1 litre per second).', '{"1": "This is ten times too small.", "2": "This divides the area by the speed.", "3": "This divides the speed by the area."}'),
  ('PHY-0703', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Equation of continuity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 99 (§5.9)', 'For an incompressible fluid in steady flow, the equation of continuity is:', '["A₁/v₁ = A₂/v₂", "P₁A₁ = P₂A₂", "A₁v₁ = A₂v₂", "v₁ = v₂"]', 2, 'The volume per second entering equals that leaving: Av = constant.', '{"0": "The speed rises where the area falls, so it is the product that stays constant.", "1": "That is not the continuity equation.", "3": "Speeds differ when the areas differ."}'),
  ('PHY-0704', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Equation of continuity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 100 (§5.9)', 'The equation of continuity is a form of the principle of:', '["conservation of energy", "conservation of momentum", "conservation of charge", "conservation of mass"]', 3, 'The mass flowing in through A₁ in time Δt equals that flowing out through A₂ (ρA₁v₁ = ρA₂v₂), so the equation expresses conservation of mass.', '{"0": "Bernoulli''s equation comes from conservation of energy.", "1": "Continuity is about the mass of fluid flowing.", "2": "That applies to electric circuits."}'),
  ('PHY-0705', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Equation of continuity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 101 (§5.10)', 'Water flows at 1.5 m s⁻¹ in a pipe of diameter 4 cm. The pipe narrows to 2 cm. The speed in the narrow part is:', '["0.375 m s⁻¹", "3 m s⁻¹", "6 m s⁻¹", "12 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'Area ∝ d². A₁/A₂ = (4/2)² = 4. v₂ = v₁ × A₁/A₂ = 1.5 × 4 = 6 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "The speed increases in the narrow part.", "1": "Halving the diameter quarters the area, so the speed rises fourfold.", "3": "This uses the cube of the diameter ratio."}'),
  ('PHY-0706', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Equation of continuity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 101 (§5.10)', 'Squeezing the end of a garden hose makes the water come out faster because:', '["the cross-sectional area decreases, so the speed must increase to keep Av constant", "squeezing adds energy to the water", "the pressure from the tap increases", "the water becomes less dense"]', 0, 'By the continuity equation A₁v₁ = A₂v₂, reducing the area at the outlet increases the flow velocity.', '{"1": "The thumb does no work to speed the water up.", "2": "The tap pressure is unchanged.", "3": "Water is nearly incompressible."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0707', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Equation of continuity', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 100 (Example 5.4)', 'A hose with an outlet of cross-sectional area 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ m² delivers 12 kg of water in 30 s (ρ = 1000 kg m⁻³). The speed of the water at the outlet is:', '["0.4 m s⁻¹", "2.0 m s⁻¹", "4.0 × 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹", "20 m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'Mass flow = 12/30 = 0.4 kg s⁻¹ = ρAv. v = 0.4/(1000 × 2.0 × 10⁻⁴) = 0.4/0.2 = 2.0 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "0.4 kg s⁻¹ is the mass flow rate.", "2": "This uses the volume flow rate as a speed.", "3": "This is ten times too large."}'),
  ('PHY-0708', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Bernoulli''s equation', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 101 (§5.11)', 'Bernoulli''s equation states that for an ideal fluid in steady flow:', '["P + ρv + ρh is constant", "P + ½ρv² + ρgh is constant along a streamline", "Av is constant", "P is the same at all points"]', 1, 'The sum of pressure, KE per unit volume (½ρv²) and PE per unit volume (ρgh) remains constant.', '{"0": "The kinetic term is ½ρv² and the height term includes g.", "2": "That is the equation of continuity.", "3": "Pressure changes with speed and height."}'),
  ('PHY-0709', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Bernoulli''s equation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 103 (§5.11)', 'Bernoulli''s equation is derived from the principle of:', '["conservation of mass", "conservation of momentum", "conservation of energy", "Archimedes'' principle"]', 2, 'The work done by pressure forces equals the change in KE plus PE of the fluid. This energy balance gives Bernoulli''s equation.', '{"0": "That gives the equation of continuity.", "1": "The derivation uses work and energy, not momentum.", "3": "Archimedes'' principle is about upthrust."}'),
  ('PHY-0710', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Bernoulli''s effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 105 (§5.12)', 'In a horizontal pipe, where the fluid speed is high, the pressure is:', '["high", "the same as where the speed is low", "zero", "low"]', 3, 'For a horizontal pipe, P + ½ρv² = constant, so where v is large, P is small.', '{"0": "Faster flow means lower pressure in a horizontal pipe.", "1": "Bernoulli''s equation links speed and pressure.", "2": "The pressure falls but is not zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0711', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Uses of Bernoulli''s equation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 103 (§5.12)', 'The lift on an aeroplane wing arises mainly because:', '["air flows faster over the top of the wing, so the pressure there is lower than below", "air flows faster under the wing, giving more pressure on top", "the wing is lighter than air", "the engine pushes the wing upward"]', 0, 'The wing shape crowds the streamlines above it, so air moves faster above than below. By Bernoulli, the pressure is lower on top, and the pressure difference pushes the wing up.', '{"1": "The faster air is on top.", "2": "Wings are much denser than air.", "3": "The engine gives forward thrust, not lift."}'),
  ('PHY-0712', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Uses of Bernoulli''s equation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 103 (§5.12)', 'The curved path of a spinning ball through the air is called the:', '["Doppler effect", "Magnus effect", "Venturi effect", "Torricelli effect"]', 1, 'Spin makes air move faster on one side; the pressure difference produces a sideways force that bends the path, the Magnus effect.', '{"0": "The Doppler effect is about the change in frequency.", "2": "The Venturi effect is pressure drop in a constriction.", "3": "Torricelli''s theorem is about the efflux speed."}'),
  ('PHY-0713', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Uses of Bernoulli''s equation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 104 (§5.12)', 'How does a perfume atomizer (or paint sprayer) work?', '["The liquid is pumped up by the rubber bulb directly.", "The liquid evaporates and rises as vapour.", "Fast air over the top of a tube lowers the pressure there, so atmospheric pressure pushes the liquid up the tube.", "The air pushes the liquid down and out."]', 2, 'Squeezing the bulb drives air fast through a narrow opening, creating low pressure above the tube; atmospheric pressure on the liquid surface pushes it up into the stream.', '{"0": "The bulb blows air across the tube; it does not push the liquid.", "1": "The liquid rises as a liquid and is sprayed.", "3": "The liquid is pushed up the tube."}'),
  ('PHY-0714', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Venturi relation', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 105 (§5.12)', 'In a horizontal Venturi meter, the speed in the narrow part is much greater than in the wide part. Water (ρ = 1000 kg m⁻³) moves at 4 m s⁻¹ in the narrow part. The pressure difference P₁ − P₂ is about:', '["1.6 × 10⁴ Pa", "2 × 10³ Pa", "4 × 10³ Pa", "8 × 10³ Pa"]', 3, 'Venturi relation: P₁ − P₂ ≈ ½ρv₂² = ½ × 1000 × 16 = 8000 Pa.', '{"0": "This leaves out the ½ in ½ρv².", "1": "This uses ½ρv instead of ½ρv².", "2": "This uses ρv instead of ½ρv²."}'),
  ('PHY-0715', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Torricelli''s theorem', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 105 (§5.12)', 'Water flows out of a small hole 5 m below the water surface in a large open tank. The speed of efflux is (g = 10 m s⁻²):', '["5 m s⁻¹", "10 m s⁻¹", "50 m s⁻¹", "100 m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'Torricelli''s theorem: v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 5) = √100 = 10 m s⁻¹, the speed of a body falling freely through 5 m.', '{"0": "This uses v = √(gh/2).", "2": "This uses gh instead of √(2gh).", "3": "100 is v², not v."}'),
  ('PHY-0716', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Viscous drag', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 106 (§5.13)', 'The drag force on an object moving through a fluid:', '["decreases as the speed increases", "does not depend on speed", "acts in the direction of motion", "increases as the speed of the object increases"]', 3, 'An object moving through a fluid experiences a retarding (drag) force that increases with its speed.', '{"0": "Faster motion gives greater drag.", "1": "Drag depends on speed.", "2": "Drag is a retarding force."}'),
  ('PHY-0717', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Stokes'' law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 106 (§5.13)', 'Stokes'' law gives the drag force on a small sphere of radius r moving slowly at speed v through a fluid of viscosity η as:', '["F = 6πηrv", "F = 6πηr²v", "F = 6πηv/r", "F = ½ρv²A"]', 0, 'Stokes'' law: F = 6πηrv, valid for slow motion; at high speeds the force is no longer proportional to v.', '{"1": "The force is proportional to r, not r².", "2": "The force grows with r.", "3": "This is not Stokes'' law."}'),
  ('PHY-0718', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Terminal velocity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 107 (§5.14)', 'A raindrop reaches terminal velocity when:', '["its weight becomes zero", "the upward drag force becomes equal to its weight", "the drag force becomes zero", "its acceleration becomes equal to g"]', 1, 'As speed rises, drag rises until it equals the weight. The net force is then zero and the drop falls at a constant speed, the terminal velocity.', '{"0": "Its weight is unchanged.", "2": "Drag is greatest at terminal velocity.", "3": "At terminal velocity the acceleration is zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0719', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Terminal velocity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 107 (§5.14)', 'For a small sphere falling through a fluid, the terminal velocity v_t = 2gr²ρ/9η. If the radius of the drop is doubled, the terminal velocity becomes:', '["twice as large", "half as large", "four times as large", "eight times as large"]', 2, 'v_t ∝ r², so doubling r multiplies v_t by 4.', '{"0": "v_t ∝ r², not r.", "1": "Larger drops fall faster.", "3": "v_t ∝ r², not r³."}'),
  ('PHY-0720', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Terminal velocity', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 107 (Example 5.5)', 'A tiny water droplet of radius 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ m falls through air (η = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ kg m⁻¹ s⁻¹, ρ = 1000 kg m⁻³, g = 9.8 m s⁻²). Its terminal velocity is about:', '["0.0044 m s⁻¹", "0.022 m s⁻¹", "0.044 m s⁻¹", "4.4 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'v_t = 2gr²ρ/9η = 2 × 9.8 × (4.0 × 10⁻¹⁰) × 1000 / (9 × 2.0 × 10⁻⁵) = 7.84 × 10⁻⁶ / 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ ≈ 0.044 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This is ten times too small.", "1": "This drops the factor 2 in 2gr²ρ.", "3": "This is 100 times too large."}'),
  ('PHY-0721', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Real fluids', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 107 (§5.15)', 'Which statement about ideal and real fluids is correct?', '["Water is an example of an ideal fluid.", "Honey is less viscous than water.", "Real fluids have zero viscosity.", "Ideal fluids do not exist in practice; every real fluid has some viscosity."]', 3, 'An ideal fluid is non-viscous and incompressible; it cannot exist practically. Water, petrol, honey etc. are real (viscous) fluids.', '{"0": "Water is a real fluid with viscosity.", "1": "Honey is more viscous than water.", "2": "Real fluids possess viscosity."}'),
  ('PHY-0722', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Superfluids', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 108 (§5.16)', 'Which property is shown by a superfluid such as helium-4 at very low temperature?', '["It can creep up the walls and over the edge of its container because it has no viscosity.", "It becomes a solid with infinite viscosity.", "It can only flow through wide pipes.", "Its flow stops as soon as it is stirred."]', 0, 'Superfluids have zero viscosity, so they flow without friction or loss of KE, pass through very narrow gaps and even climb up and over the walls of containers.', '{"1": "A superfluid has zero viscosity.", "2": "Superfluids flow through extremely narrow spaces.", "3": "Once moving, it keeps flowing; stirred vortices keep spinning."}'),
  ('PHY-0723', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Superfluids', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 5 Solids and Fluid Dynamics, p. 108 (§5.16)', 'One application of superfluid helium-4 mentioned in the book is:', '["as a fuel in rockets", "as a coolant for high-field magnets", "to make glass", "as a lubricant in car engines"]', 1, 'The book says superfluid helium-4 serves as a coolant for high-field magnets, and helium-3 and helium-4 are used in advanced particle detectors.', '{"0": "The book does not list that use.", "2": "Glass is not made from helium.", "3": "Superfluid helium only exists near absolute zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0724', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory of gases', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 113 (§6.1)', 'Which is one of the assumptions of the kinetic theory of gases?', '["Gas molecules attract each other strongly at all distances.", "The volume of the molecules is a large part of the gas volume.", "Gas molecules are at rest unless heated.", "The collisions between gas molecules and with the walls are perfectly elastic."]', 3, 'The kinetic theory assumes perfectly elastic collisions (no net loss of kinetic energy), negligible molecular volume, no intermolecular forces, and constant random motion.', '{"0": "The theory assumes no intermolecular forces except during collisions.", "1": "Molecular volume is assumed negligible.", "2": "Molecules are in constant random motion."}'),
  ('PHY-0725', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory of gases', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 113 (§6.1)', 'According to the kinetic theory, the pressure of a gas on the walls of its container is due to:', '["collisions of the gas molecules with the walls", "the weight of the gas pressing down", "attraction between molecules and the walls", "the volume of the molecules"]', 0, 'The force exerted by the molecules when they collide with the walls produces the pressure of the gas.', '{"1": "Gas pressure acts equally on all walls, not just the bottom.", "2": "The theory assumes no such forces.", "3": "Molecular volume is assumed negligible."}'),
  ('PHY-0726', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory of gases', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 113 (§6.1)', 'The average kinetic energy of the molecules of a gas is directly proportional to its:', '["absolute temperature", "volume", "Celsius temperature", "number of molecules"]', 0, 'The kinetic theory states that the average KE of gas particles is directly proportional to the absolute temperature: ⟨½mv²⟩ = (3/2)k_BT.', '{"1": "Volume does not set the average molecular kinetic energy.", "2": "It is proportional to the kelvin temperature, not °C.", "3": "The average KE per molecule does not depend on how many there are."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0727', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory of gases', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 114 (§6.1)', 'A real gas behaves most like an ideal gas at:', '["high pressure and low temperature", "high pressure and high temperature", "low pressure and low temperature", "low pressure and high temperature"]', 3, 'At low pressure and high temperature the gas has low density, the molecules are far apart, and intermolecular forces are negligible, so it approaches ideal behaviour.', '{"0": "Under these conditions intermolecular forces and molecular volume matter most.", "1": "High pressure brings molecules close together.", "2": "Low temperature makes intermolecular attraction important."}'),
  ('PHY-0728', 'Thermodynamics', 'Ideal gas equation', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 114 (§6.1)', 'In the ideal gas equation PV = nRT, n stands for:', '["the number of molecules", "the density of the gas", "the molar mass", "the number of moles of the gas"]', 3, 'In PV = nRT, n is the amount of substance (number of moles), R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ and T is the absolute temperature.', '{"0": "The number of molecules N appears in PV = Nk_BT.", "1": "Density is ρ = PM/RT.", "2": "Molar mass is M, with n = m/M."}'),
  ('PHY-0729', 'Thermodynamics', 'Ideal gas equation', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 115 (§6.1)', 'The Boltzmann constant k_B is related to the gas constant R and Avogadro''s number N_A by:', '["k_B = R/N_A", "k_B = R × N_A", "k_B = N_A/R", "k_B = R/T"]', 0, 'k_B = R/N_A = 8.31/6.02 × 10²³ ≈ 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹; it is the gas constant per molecule.', '{"1": "That would give a huge number, not 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.", "2": "This is the inverse.", "3": "R/T is not a constant."}'),
  ('PHY-0730', 'Thermodynamics', 'Ideal gas equation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 115 (§6.1)', 'The ideal gas equation written in terms of the number of molecules N is:', '["PV = NRT", "PV = Nk_BT", "PV = nk_BT", "P = Nk_BTV"]', 1, 'Since n = N/N_A and R = N_A k_B, PV = nRT becomes PV = Nk_BT.', '{"0": "With N molecules, the constant is k_B, not R.", "2": "With n moles, the constant is R.", "3": "Volume multiplies P, it does not appear with N on the right."}'),
  ('PHY-0731', 'Thermodynamics', 'Ideal gas equation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 115 (Example 6.1)', 'Two moles of an ideal gas are held at 300 K in a volume of 0.050 m³ (R = 8.3 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹). The pressure is about:', '["1.0 × 10⁵ Pa", "2.0 × 10⁵ Pa", "5.0 × 10⁴ Pa", "5.0 × 10³ Pa"]', 0, 'P = nRT/V = 2 × 8.3 × 300/0.050 = 4980/0.050 ≈ 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa.', '{"1": "This doubles the correct pressure.", "2": "This uses one mole instead of two.", "3": "This divides by 1 m³ instead of 0.050 m³ (nRT = 4980 J)."}'),
  ('PHY-0732', 'Thermodynamics', 'Gas laws', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 116 (§6.1)', 'Boyle''s law states that for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature:', '["V/T = constant", "PV = constant", "P/T = constant", "P + V = constant"]', 1, 'Boyle''s law: P ∝ 1/V at constant T, so PV = constant, i.e. P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.', '{"0": "That is Charles'' law (constant pressure).", "2": "That is the pressure law (constant volume).", "3": "Pressure and volume are inversely proportional, so their product is constant."}'),
  ('PHY-0733', 'Thermodynamics', 'Gas laws', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 116 (§6.1)', 'A gas occupies 6 L at 100 kPa. It is compressed at constant temperature to 2 L. The new pressure is:', '["33 kPa", "200 kPa", "300 kPa", "600 kPa"]', 2, 'P₁V₁ = P₂V₂ → 100 × 6 = P₂ × 2 → P₂ = 300 kPa.', '{"0": "Pressure rises when volume falls at constant temperature.", "1": "P₂ = P₁V₁/V₂ = 600/2, not 100 × 2.", "3": "600 is P₁V₁ in kPa L, not a pressure."}'),
  ('PHY-0734', 'Thermodynamics', 'Gas laws', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 116 (§6.1)', 'A gas at constant pressure is heated from 27 °C to 127 °C. Its volume increases by a factor of:', '["2", "3/4", "4/3", "127/27"]', 2, 'V ∝ T (in kelvin). T₁ = 300 K, T₂ = 400 K, so V₂/V₁ = 400/300 = 4/3.', '{"0": "Doubling would need T to go from 300 K to 600 K.", "1": "Volume increases when temperature rises at constant pressure.", "3": "Charles'' law uses absolute (kelvin) temperatures."}'),
  ('PHY-0735', 'Thermodynamics', 'Gas laws', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 117 (§6.1)', 'A sealed rigid container of gas is heated from 300 K to 450 K. Its pressure changes from 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa to:', '["1.3 × 10⁵ Pa", "2.0 × 10⁵ Pa", "3.0 × 10⁵ Pa", "4.5 × 10⁵ Pa"]', 2, 'At constant volume P ∝ T: P₂ = P₁ × T₂/T₁ = 2.0 × 10⁵ × 450/300 = 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa.', '{"0": "Pressure rises, not falls, when a gas is heated at constant volume.", "1": "Pressure changes with temperature at constant volume.", "3": "This takes P ∝ T but uses 450/200; the ratio is 450/300."}'),
  ('PHY-0736', 'Thermodynamics', 'Gas laws', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 116 (§6.1)', 'On a graph of V against T (in kelvin) for a gas at constant pressure, the line:', '["is a hyperbola", "is horizontal", "is straight and passes through the origin (0 K)", "cuts the T-axis at 273 K"]', 2, 'Charles'' law V ∝ T gives a straight line through the origin on a V–T(K) graph; on a °C scale it meets the axis at −273 °C.', '{"0": "A hyperbola is the shape of a P–V graph at constant temperature.", "1": "Volume changes with temperature.", "3": "Extrapolated, it meets zero volume at 0 K (−273 °C)."}'),
  ('PHY-0737', 'Thermodynamics', 'Kinetic theory and gas laws', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 116 (§6.1)', 'In terms of molecules, why does the pressure of a gas rise when it is heated at constant volume?', '["Molecules become larger and take up more space.", "More molecules are produced.", "Molecules attract the walls more strongly.", "Molecules move faster, so they hit the walls more often and with greater force."]', 3, 'Higher temperature means higher average KE and speed. Collisions with the walls become more frequent and more forceful, so the pressure rises.', '{"0": "Molecular size does not change on heating.", "1": "The number of molecules in a sealed container stays the same.", "2": "The theory assumes no such attraction."}'),
  ('PHY-0738', 'Thermodynamics', 'Thermal equilibrium', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 117 (§6.1)', 'Two bodies in contact are in thermal equilibrium when:', '["they are at the same temperature, so there is no net heat flow between them", "they contain the same amount of heat", "they have the same mass", "one is hotter than the other"]', 0, 'When two bodies reach the same temperature there is no driving force for heat transfer, e.g. a spoon and the coffee it stands in.', '{"1": "Equilibrium depends on temperature, not on heat content.", "2": "Mass does not decide heat flow.", "3": "Then heat would flow from the hotter to the colder."}'),
  ('PHY-0739', 'Thermodynamics', 'Internal energy', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 117 (§6.2)', 'The internal energy of a substance is:', '["the kinetic energy of the whole body moving", "the sum of the kinetic and potential energies of its molecules", "only the potential energy of its molecules", "the heat it contains"]', 1, 'Internal energy is the sum of all forms of molecular energy (kinetic and potential) of a substance.', '{"0": "Internal energy is about molecular energies, not bulk motion.", "2": "Molecular kinetic energy is also included.", "3": "Heat is energy in transfer; internal energy is stored."}'),
  ('PHY-0740', 'Thermodynamics', 'Internal energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 117 (§6.2)', 'For an ideal gas, the internal energy depends only on its:', '["volume", "pressure", "temperature", "shape of the container"]', 2, 'Ideal-gas molecules have no intermolecular forces, so no potential energy; U is the translational KE of the molecules, which is proportional to temperature.', '{"0": "Ideal gas molecules exert no forces on each other, so volume does not affect U.", "1": "Pressure alone does not fix U.", "3": "The container shape does not affect U."}'),
  ('PHY-0741', 'Thermodynamics', 'Internal energy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 118 (§6.2)', 'Internal energy is called a function of state. This means it:', '["depends on how the change is carried out", "is always zero for a gas", "cannot change during any process", "depends only on the initial and final states, not on the path taken"]', 3, 'Like gravitational PE, internal energy depends only on the state of the system; only its change ΔU matters.', '{"0": "That describes heat and work, not internal energy.", "1": "A gas has internal energy.", "2": "Internal energy changes in many processes."}'),
  ('PHY-0742', 'Thermodynamics', 'Average kinetic energy', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 117 (§6.2)', 'The average translational kinetic energy of a gas molecule at 300 K is (k_B = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹):', '["1.2 × 10⁻²⁰ J", "3.7 × 10³ J", "4.1 × 10⁻²¹ J", "6.2 × 10⁻²¹ J"]', 3, '⟨KE⟩ = (3/2)k_BT = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 ≈ 6.2 × 10⁻²¹ J.', '{"0": "This uses 3k_BT instead of (3/2)k_BT.", "1": "This uses R instead of k_B for a single molecule.", "2": "4.1 × 10⁻²¹ J is k_BT; the factor 3/2 is missing."}'),
  ('PHY-0743', 'Thermodynamics', 'Heat and work', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 118 (§6.3)', 'By the convention used in the book, work done BY a system on its surroundings is taken as:', '["positive", "negative", "zero", "always equal to the heat supplied"]', 0, 'Following the steam-engine idea of "heat in, work out", heat added to the system and work done by the system are positive.', '{"1": "Work done on the system is negative.", "2": "Work done by an expanding system is not zero.", "3": "That is only true in special processes."}'),
  ('PHY-0744', 'Thermodynamics', 'Heat and work', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 118 (§6.3)', 'A gas expands at a constant pressure of 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa from 1.0 × 10⁻³ m³ to 3.0 × 10⁻³ m³. The work done by the gas is:', '["200 J", "400 J", "600 J", "800 J"]', 1, 'W = PΔV = 2.0 × 10⁵ × (3.0 − 1.0) × 10⁻³ = 2.0 × 10⁵ × 2.0 × 10⁻³ = 400 J.', '{"0": "This uses only 1.0 × 10⁻³ m³ for ΔV.", "2": "This uses the final volume, not the change.", "3": "This doubles the volume change; ΔV = 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³."}'),
  ('PHY-0745', 'Thermodynamics', 'Heat and work', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 118 (§6.3)', 'On a P–V diagram, the work done by a gas during a change is given by:', '["the slope of the curve", "the area under the curve between the initial and final volumes", "the product of the final pressure and final volume", "the length of the curve"]', 1, 'W = PΔV for small steps; adding them gives the area under the P–V curve.', '{"0": "The slope is ΔP/ΔV, not work.", "2": "PV at one point is not the work done.", "3": "Length on the graph has no physical meaning here."}'),
  ('PHY-0746', 'Thermodynamics', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 119 (§6.4)', 'The first law of thermodynamics, with the book''s sign convention, is written as:', '["Q = ΔU − W", "W = Q + ΔU", "Q = ΔU + W", "ΔU = Q × W"]', 2, 'Heat Q added to a system appears as an increase in internal energy ΔU plus work W done by the system on its surroundings.', '{"0": "With W taken as work done by the system, Q = ΔU + W.", "1": "Heat supplied is shared between ΔU and W.", "3": "The terms add; they are not multiplied."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0747', 'Thermodynamics', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 120 (Example 6.2)', 'A gas absorbs 500 J of heat and does 200 J of work on its surroundings. Its internal energy changes by:', '["+700 J", "−300 J", "+200 J", "+300 J"]', 3, 'ΔU = Q − W = 500 − 200 = +300 J.', '{"0": "Work done by the gas reduces the energy left as internal energy.", "1": "More heat is added than work done, so U increases.", "2": "200 J is the work done."}'),
  ('PHY-0748', 'Thermodynamics', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 119 (§6.4)', 'When a bicycle pump with a blocked outlet is pushed in quickly, the air inside becomes hot. This is because:', '["work done on the air increases its internal energy", "heat flows into the air from the surroundings", "the air''s mass increases", "friction in the pump cools the air"]', 0, 'Pushing the piston quickly does work on the trapped air; with little time for heat to escape, this work raises the internal energy and so the temperature.', '{"1": "The process is quick, so little heat flows in.", "2": "The mass of trapped air is constant.", "3": "Friction would heat, not cool, and the main cause is the work on the gas."}'),
  ('PHY-0749', 'Thermodynamics', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 120 (§6.4)', 'Applying the first law to the human body, the book explains that:', '["the body creates energy when it works", "work done by the body decreases its internal energy, which is replaced by the food we eat", "the body''s internal energy increases whenever it does work", "metabolism violates the first law"]', 1, 'From ΔU = Q − W, work done by the body lowers its internal energy; the energy is restored from the food eaten (metabolism).', '{"0": "Energy is not created.", "2": "Doing work uses up internal energy.", "3": "Metabolism obeys the first law."}'),
  ('PHY-0750', 'Thermodynamics', 'Isothermal process', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 120 (§6.4)', 'In an isothermal expansion of an ideal gas:', '["Q = 0, so the gas cools", "W = 0, so all heat raises the temperature", "ΔU = 0, so all the heat absorbed is used to do work", "the temperature rises"]', 2, 'At constant temperature the internal energy of an ideal gas is unchanged (ΔU = 0), so the first law gives Q = W.', '{"0": "Q = 0 describes an adiabatic process.", "1": "In an expansion the gas does work.", "3": "Isothermal means constant temperature."}'),
  ('PHY-0751', 'Thermodynamics', 'Isothermal process', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 121 (§6.4)', 'Why must an isothermal change be carried out slowly?', '["Fast changes break the gas molecules.", "Slow changes increase the internal energy.", "Boyle''s law applies only to slow sound waves.", "Heat must have time to flow in or out so the temperature can stay constant."]', 3, 'Heat transfer takes time, so to keep the temperature constant the expansion or compression must be slow.', '{"0": "Speed does not break molecules.", "1": "Internal energy stays constant in an isothermal process.", "2": "Boyle''s law applies at constant temperature, however that is achieved."}'),
  ('PHY-0752', 'Thermodynamics', 'Adiabatic process', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 121 (§6.4)', 'In an adiabatic expansion of a gas:', '["the gas does work at the expense of its internal energy, so its temperature falls", "the temperature stays constant", "heat enters the gas to keep its temperature constant", "the internal energy increases"]', 0, 'For an adiabatic process Q = 0, so W = −ΔU. When the gas expands and does work, its internal energy and temperature fall.', '{"1": "That is an isothermal process.", "2": "No heat is exchanged in an adiabatic process.", "3": "In an expansion the gas loses internal energy."}'),
  ('PHY-0753', 'Thermodynamics', 'Adiabatic process', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 121 (§6.4)', 'Which of these is an example of an adiabatic process given in the book?', '["Slow melting of ice in a glass", "The rapid escape of air from a burst tyre", "Slow compression of a gas in a cylinder kept in a water bath", "Water boiling in an open pan"]', 1, 'Adiabatic changes happen when a gas expands or is compressed rapidly; the book''s examples include a burst tyre, sound waves passing through air, and cloud formation.', '{"0": "This involves steady heat transfer.", "2": "This is close to isothermal.", "3": "Heat is supplied continuously."}'),
  ('PHY-0754', 'Thermodynamics', 'Adiabatic process', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 121 (§6.4)', 'For an adiabatic process in an ideal gas, the relation between pressure and volume is:', '["PV = constant", "P/V = constant", "PVᵞ = constant, where γ = C_p/C_v", "V/T = constant"]', 2, 'Since the temperature does not stay constant, an adiabatic change follows PVᵞ = constant, with γ the ratio of molar specific heats at constant pressure and constant volume.', '{"0": "That is the isothermal (Boyle''s law) relation.", "1": "No standard process gives this.", "3": "That is Charles'' law."}'),
  ('PHY-0755', 'Thermodynamics', 'Adiabatic process', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 121 (§6.4)', 'On a P–V diagram, why is an adiabat steeper than an isotherm through the same point?', '["Because PV is constant on an adiabat", "Because heat is added during an adiabatic change", "Because γ is less than 1", "In an adiabatic compression the temperature also rises, so the pressure rises faster than in an isothermal one."]', 3, 'PVᵞ = constant with γ > 1 makes P change faster with V than PV = constant. Physically, adiabatic compression heats the gas, adding to the pressure rise.', '{"0": "PV is constant on an isotherm.", "1": "No heat is exchanged.", "2": "γ = C_p/C_v is greater than 1."}'),
  ('PHY-0756', 'Thermodynamics', 'Reversible and irreversible processes', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 121 (§6.5)', 'A reversible process is one that:', '["can be retraced in exactly the reverse order without producing any change in the surroundings", "happens very suddenly", "involves friction", "can only go forward"]', 0, 'In a reversible process, the substance passes through the same stages in reverse, with heat and work effects reversed, leaving no change in the surroundings.', '{"1": "Sudden changes are irreversible.", "2": "Processes involving friction are irreversible.", "3": "That describes an irreversible process."}'),
  ('PHY-0757', 'Thermodynamics', 'Reversible and irreversible processes', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 122 (§6.5)', 'Which of these is an example of an irreversible process?', '["Slow evaporation and condensation of a liquid", "An explosion", "Very slow compression of a gas in a cylinder", "Slow liquefaction of a gas"]', 1, 'Sudden changes and changes involving friction or heat dissipation are irreversible. An explosion is a highly irreversible process.', '{"0": "The book calls these practically reversible.", "2": "The book gives this as nearly reversible.", "3": "This is practically reversible."}'),
  ('PHY-0758', 'Thermodynamics', 'Heat engine', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 122 (§6.6)', 'A heat engine works by:', '["converting all the heat it absorbs into work", "taking heat from a cold body and giving it to a hot body", "taking heat from a high-temperature reservoir, converting part of it into work and rejecting the rest to a low-temperature reservoir", "creating heat from work"]', 2, 'In a cycle, the engine absorbs Q₁ from the hot source, does work W and expels Q₂ to the cold sink: W = Q₁ − Q₂.', '{"0": "The second law says this is impossible.", "1": "That is a refrigerator.", "3": "That is not the principle of a heat engine."}'),
  ('PHY-0759', 'Thermodynamics', 'Second law of thermodynamics', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 123 (§6.7)', 'Kelvin''s statement of the second law says it is impossible to:', '["convert work into heat", "transfer heat from a hot body to a cold body", "build any heat engine", "convert heat taken from a single reservoir entirely into work in a cycle"]', 3, 'No process can convert heat from a single reservoir entirely into work without some other change. Two reservoirs at different temperatures are needed.', '{"0": "Work can be converted completely into heat (e.g. friction).", "1": "This happens naturally.", "2": "Heat engines work with two reservoirs."}'),
  ('PHY-0760', 'Thermodynamics', 'Second law of thermodynamics', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 123 (§6.7)', 'Why can''t a ship''s engine run using only the heat stored in the ocean?', '["There is no reservoir at a lower temperature to which the engine can reject heat.", "The ocean contains too little heat energy.", "Sea water is too heavy.", "The first law forbids it."]', 0, 'A heat engine needs a hot source and a colder sink. The ocean and atmosphere hold huge amounts of heat, but with no colder reservoir available, it cannot be turned into useful work.', '{"1": "The ocean contains a huge amount of heat.", "2": "Weight is not the reason.", "3": "The first law would allow it; the second law forbids it."}'),
  ('PHY-0761', 'Thermodynamics', 'Second law of thermodynamics', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 123 (§6.7)', 'According to the book, roughly what percentage of the available heat energy does a petrol engine convert into work?', '["About 75%", "About 25%", "100%", "About 5%"]', 1, 'The book says petrol engines convert roughly 25% and diesel engines 35–40% of the available heat into work.', '{"0": "That is far above real petrol engine efficiencies.", "2": "The second law forbids 100% conversion.", "3": "Petrol engines do much better than this."}'),
  ('PHY-0762', 'Thermodynamics', 'Carnot engine', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 123 (§6.8)', 'The Carnot cycle consists of:', '["four isothermal processes", "four adiabatic processes", "two isothermal and two adiabatic processes", "two isobaric and two isochoric processes"]', 2, 'Isothermal expansion at T₁, adiabatic expansion to T₂, isothermal compression at T₂, adiabatic compression back to T₁.', '{"0": "The cycle needs adiabatic steps to change temperature.", "1": "The cycle needs isothermal steps to exchange heat.", "3": "These are not the Carnot steps."}'),
  ('PHY-0763', 'Thermodynamics', 'Carnot engine', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 124 (§6.8)', 'The efficiency of a Carnot engine working between temperatures T₁ (hot) and T₂ (cold) is:', '["1 − T₁/T₂", "T₂/T₁", "(T₁ + T₂)/T₁", "1 − T₂/T₁"]', 3, 'η = (Q₁ − Q₂)/Q₁ = 1 − Q₂/Q₁ = 1 − T₂/T₁, with temperatures in kelvin.', '{"0": "This would be negative since T₁ > T₂.", "1": "T₂/T₁ is the fraction of heat rejected.", "2": "This is greater than 1."}'),
  ('PHY-0764', 'Thermodynamics', 'Carnot engine', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 125 (Example 6.3)', 'A Carnot engine works between 527 °C and 127 °C. Its efficiency is:', '["33%", "50%", "76%", "100%"]', 1, 'T₁ = 800 K, T₂ = 400 K. η = 1 − 400/800 = 0.5 = 50%.', '{"0": "This divides the temperature difference by T₁ + T₂ instead of by T₁.", "2": "This uses Celsius temperatures (1 − 127/527).", "3": "100% needs T₂ = 0 K."}'),
  ('PHY-0765', 'Thermodynamics', 'Carnot engine', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 124 (§6.8)', 'The efficiency of a Carnot engine can be 100% only if:', '["the cold reservoir is at absolute zero", "the hot reservoir is at absolute zero", "the working substance is an ideal gas", "the engine runs very fast"]', 0, 'η = 1 − T₂/T₁ = 1 only if T₂ = 0 K. Such reservoirs are not available, so efficiency is always less than 100%.', '{"1": "The hot reservoir must be hotter than the cold one.", "2": "Efficiency does not depend on the working substance.", "3": "Speed does not raise the Carnot limit."}'),
  ('PHY-0766', 'Thermodynamics', 'Carnot''s theorem', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 125 (§6.8)', 'Carnot''s theorem states that:', '["all real engines are as efficient as a Carnot engine", "no heat engine can be more efficient than a Carnot engine working between the same two temperatures", "a Carnot engine''s efficiency depends on its working substance", "the efficiency of a Carnot engine is always 100%"]', 1, 'No engine can beat a Carnot engine between the same two temperatures, and all Carnot engines between them have the same efficiency, whatever the working substance.', '{"0": "Real engines lose energy to friction and heat losses.", "2": "All Carnot engines between the same temperatures have the same efficiency.", "3": "It is 1 − T₂/T₁, always less than 1."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0767', 'Thermodynamics', 'Carnot engine', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 125 (§6.8)', 'In most practical engines the cold reservoir is near room temperature. The efficiency can then be raised mainly by:', '["lowering the temperature of the hot reservoir", "using a different working substance", "raising the temperature of the hot reservoir", "increasing the mass of the engine"]', 2, 'With T₂ fixed near room temperature, η = 1 − T₂/T₁ increases only if T₁ is increased.', '{"0": "That reduces T₁ − T₂ and lowers efficiency.", "1": "Carnot efficiency does not depend on the substance.", "3": "Mass does not enter the efficiency formula."}'),
  ('PHY-0768', 'Thermodynamics', 'Refrigerator', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 125 (§6.9)', 'A refrigerator is:', '["a device that makes heat disappear", "a heat engine that converts heat into work", "a device that needs no work to cool its contents", "a heat engine operating in reverse, using work to move heat from a cold to a hot reservoir"]', 3, 'A refrigerator absorbs heat Q₂ from a cold reservoir and, with work W done on it, rejects Q₁ = Q₂ + W to a hot reservoir.', '{"0": "Heat is moved, not destroyed.", "1": "A refrigerator uses work, it does not produce it.", "2": "Heat does not flow from cold to hot without work."}'),
  ('PHY-0769', 'Thermodynamics', 'Refrigerator', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 126 (§6.9)', 'A refrigerator removes 600 J of heat from its freezer while 150 J of work is done on it. Its coefficient of performance is:', '["0.25", "4", "5", "450"]', 1, 'COP = Q₂/W = 600/150 = 4.', '{"0": "This is W/Q₂, the inverse.", "2": "This uses Q₁ = 750 J instead of Q₂.", "3": "This is Q₂ − W, not a ratio."}'),
  ('PHY-0770', 'Thermodynamics', 'Refrigerator', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 126 (Example 6.4)', 'An ideal refrigerator keeps its freezer at −13 °C (260 K) and rejects heat to a room at 26 °C (299 K). Its coefficient of performance is about:', '["0.13", "0.87", "6.7", "7.7"]', 2, 'COP = T₂/(T₁ − T₂) = 260/(299 − 260) = 260/39 ≈ 6.7.', '{"0": "This is (T₁ − T₂)/T₁.", "1": "0.87 = 260/299, which is T₂/T₁, not the COP.", "3": "This uses T₁ instead of T₂ in the numerator."}'),
  ('PHY-0771', 'Thermodynamics', 'Entropy', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 126 (§6.10)', 'When a system absorbs heat ΔQ reversibly at absolute temperature T, its change in entropy is:', '["ΔS = ΔQ/T", "ΔS = ΔQ × T", "ΔS = T/ΔQ", "ΔS = ΔQ − T"]', 0, 'Entropy was introduced by Clausius; for a reversible process ΔS = ΔQ/T, in J K⁻¹.', '{"1": "Entropy change is heat divided by temperature.", "2": "This is the inverse.", "3": "Heat and temperature cannot be subtracted."}'),
  ('PHY-0772', 'Thermodynamics', 'Entropy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 127 (§6.10)', 'Heat Q flows from a hot reservoir at T₁ to a cold reservoir at T₂. The total entropy of the two reservoirs:', '["decreases, because the hot reservoir loses heat", "increases, because Q/T₂ is greater than Q/T₁", "stays the same", "becomes zero"]', 1, 'The hot reservoir loses Q/T₁ and the cold one gains Q/T₂. Since T₁ > T₂, Q/T₂ > Q/T₁, so the net entropy change is positive.', '{"0": "The cold reservoir gains more entropy than the hot one loses.", "2": "That happens only in a reversible process.", "3": "Entropy does not become zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0773', 'Thermodynamics', 'Entropy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 127 (§6.10)', 'An increase in entropy is linked by the book to:', '["an increase in the energy available for work", "a decrease in molecular randomness", "an increase in disorder and the degradation of energy into a less useful form", "the destruction of energy"]', 2, 'Natural processes go towards greater disorder. Each entropy increase loses some chance to convert heat into work, so energy is degraded to a less orderly form.', '{"0": "Increasing entropy means less energy is available for work.", "1": "Entropy measures disorder; it rises with randomness.", "3": "Energy is conserved; it is degraded, not destroyed."}'),
  ('PHY-0774', 'Thermodynamics', 'Entropy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 127 (§6.10)', 'For a reversible process the entropy of the system plus surroundings:', '["increases", "decreases", "becomes infinite", "remains constant"]', 3, 'Only processes in which entropy increases or remains constant can occur; reversible processes keep it constant, irreversible ones increase it.', '{"0": "Entropy increases in irreversible processes.", "1": "The total entropy never decreases.", "2": "Entropy changes are finite."}'),
  ('PHY-0775', 'Thermodynamics', 'Entropy', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 128 (Example 6.5)', '2.0 kg of ice at 0 °C melts into water at 0 °C (latent heat of fusion 3.36 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹). The change in entropy is about:', '["+2.46 × 10³ J K⁻¹", "+1.23 × 10³ J K⁻¹", "−2.46 × 10³ J K⁻¹", "+6.72 × 10⁵ J K⁻¹"]', 0, 'ΔQ = mL = 2.0 × 3.36 × 10⁵ = 6.72 × 10⁵ J. ΔS = ΔQ/T = 6.72 × 10⁵/273 ≈ 2.46 × 10³ J K⁻¹.', '{"1": "This is for 1.0 kg of ice.", "2": "Melting absorbs heat, so the entropy increases.", "3": "This is the heat absorbed, not the entropy change."}'),
  ('PHY-0776', 'Thermodynamics', 'Entropy', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 6 Heat and Thermodynamics, p. 128 (§6.10)', 'According to the second law, the entropy of the universe:', '["always decreases", "always increases in natural processes", "stays at zero", "first increases then decreases"]', 1, 'When all systems are taken together as the universe, the entropy always increases; a local decrease is at the expense of a larger increase elsewhere.', '{"0": "Local decreases are paid for by larger increases elsewhere.", "2": "Entropy is not zero.", "3": "Taken as a whole, the universe''s entropy keeps increasing."}'),
  ('PHY-0777', 'Waves', 'Waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 132 (§7.1)', 'A wave travelling through a medium is best described as a regular disturbance that:', '["carries the particles of the medium along with it from the source to the receiver", "transfers matter but not energy", "can only travel through a solid", "carries energy outward from its source while the particles of the medium only vibrate about their mean positions"]', 3, 'A wave is a regular disturbance that carries energy away from the source; in a medium the particles move up and down or to and fro about their equilibrium positions.', '{"0": "The particles oscillate about fixed mean positions; there is no net transport of matter.", "1": "A wave transfers energy; it does not transport matter.", "2": "Mechanical waves travel through solids, liquids and gases, and EM waves need no medium at all."}'),
  ('PHY-0778', 'Waves', 'Wave terms', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 133 (§7.1)', 'The wavelength of a wave is the distance between:', '["two consecutive points on the wave that are in phase", "a crest and the next trough", "the equilibrium position and a crest", "the source and the point where the wave dies out"]', 0, 'Wavelength λ is the distance between two consecutive similar points (in phase), such as two neighbouring crests.', '{"1": "That distance is half a wavelength.", "2": "That is the amplitude.", "3": "That is the range of the wave, not its wavelength."}'),
  ('PHY-0779', 'Waves', 'Wave speed', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 133 (§7.1)', 'A sound wave has a frequency of 250 Hz and a wavelength of 1.36 m. Its speed is:', '["184 m s⁻¹", "251 m s⁻¹", "340 m s⁻¹", "3400 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'v = fλ = 250 Hz × 1.36 m = 340 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This divides f by λ instead of multiplying them.", "1": "Frequency and wavelength are multiplied, not added.", "3": "Check the arithmetic: 250 × 1.36 = 340."}'),
  ('PHY-0780', 'Waves', 'Wave terms', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 133 (§7.1)', 'A wave source completes 600 oscillations in 2 minutes. The period of the wave is:', '["0.0033 s", "0.2 s", "5 s", "300 s"]', 1, 'f = 600/120 s = 5 Hz, so T = 1/f = 0.2 s.', '{"0": "This takes the time as 2 s instead of 120 s.", "2": "5 is the frequency in hertz; the period is its reciprocal.", "3": "This divides the oscillations by the minutes; it is neither f nor T."}'),
  ('PHY-0781', 'Waves', 'Types of waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 134 (§7.1)', 'Which of these waves can travel through a vacuum?', '["Seismic waves", "Sound waves", "Ripples on a pond", "X-rays"]', 3, 'X-rays are electromagnetic waves, which do not need a medium and can travel through vacuum. The others are mechanical waves.', '{"0": "Seismic waves are mechanical and need the Earth''s material to travel.", "1": "Sound is mechanical and needs a solid, liquid or gas.", "2": "Water waves are mechanical waves in water."}'),
  ('PHY-0782', 'Waves', 'Types of waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 134 (§7.1)', 'The de Broglie (matter) waves associated with moving electrons belong to the class of:', '["mechanical waves", "surface waves", "sound waves", "quantum waves"]', 3, 'The book groups waves associated with particles such as electrons and photons (matter or de Broglie waves) as quantum waves.', '{"0": "Mechanical waves are vibrations of a material medium.", "1": "Surface waves travel along an interface between two media.", "2": "Sound is a longitudinal mechanical wave."}'),
  ('PHY-0783', 'Waves', 'Transverse and longitudinal waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 134 (§7.1)', 'In a transverse wave, the particles of the medium vibrate:', '["at right angles to the direction in which the wave''s energy travels", "parallel to the direction in which the energy travels", "only forwards, moving steadily away from the source", "not at all, since only energy moves"]', 0, 'In a transverse wave the particle vibrations are perpendicular to the direction of energy travel; in a longitudinal wave they are along it.', '{"1": "That describes a longitudinal wave.", "2": "Particles oscillate about their mean positions; they are not carried along.", "3": "In a mechanical wave the particles do vibrate; it is their net position that does not change."}'),
  ('PHY-0784', 'Waves', 'Transverse and longitudinal waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 134 (§7.1)', 'Sound waves travelling through air are:', '["longitudinal mechanical waves", "transverse mechanical waves", "transverse electromagnetic waves", "longitudinal electromagnetic waves"]', 0, 'Sound needs a medium (mechanical) and its particles vibrate back and forth along the direction of propagation (longitudinal).', '{"1": "Air particles vibrate along the direction of travel, in compressions and rarefactions.", "2": "Sound needs a medium, so it is not electromagnetic.", "3": "EM waves are transverse and need no medium; sound is mechanical."}'),
  ('PHY-0785', 'Waves', 'Superposition', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 135 (§7.2)', 'Two waves with amplitudes 5 mm and 3 mm meet at a point exactly out of phase. The amplitude of the resultant displacement there is:', '["0 mm", "2 mm", "5.8 mm", "8 mm"]', 1, 'Out of phase, y = y₁ + (−y₂) = 5 mm − 3 mm = 2 mm.', '{"0": "Complete cancellation needs equal amplitudes.", "2": "Displacements add algebraically, not as perpendicular vectors.", "3": "That is the in-phase result."}'),
  ('PHY-0786', 'Waves', 'Superposition', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 135 (§7.2)', 'According to the textbook, the principle of superposition applies to:', '["electromagnetic waves only", "waves of exactly the same frequency only", "linear, small-amplitude waves", "waves travelling in the same direction only"]', 2, 'The book notes that the principle of superposition holds for linear or small-amplitude waves, whatever their type, frequency or direction.', '{"0": "It applies to mechanical waves such as sound and string waves too.", "1": "Beats come from superposing waves of different frequencies.", "3": "Stationary waves come from superposing waves travelling in opposite directions."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0787', 'Waves', 'Superposition', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 135 (§7.2)', 'Two waves of slightly different frequencies travelling in the same direction are superposed. The result is:', '["stationary waves", "steady interference", "diffraction", "beats"]', 3, 'Superposition of waves with slightly different frequencies moving the same way gives a periodic rise and fall of loudness: beats.', '{"0": "These need equal frequencies travelling in opposite directions.", "1": "Interference needs the same frequency and the same direction.", "2": "Diffraction is the bending of waves round an obstacle or through a gap."}'),
  ('PHY-0788', 'Waves', 'Noise-cancelling headphones', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 136 (§7.2)', 'In noise-cancelling headphones, the "anti-noise" signal produced by the processing unit has:', '["the same amplitude and the same phase as the ambient noise", "twice the frequency of the ambient noise", "a much larger amplitude than the noise, in the same phase", "the same amplitude as the ambient noise but opposite phase"]', 3, 'The anti-noise is the exact opposite of the ambient noise in amplitude and phase, so by superposition the two cancel.', '{"0": "That would double the noise, not cancel it.", "1": "Waves of different frequency cannot cancel steadily.", "2": "That would make the noise louder."}'),
  ('PHY-0789', 'Waves', 'Noise-cancelling headphones', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 136 (§7.2)', 'In noise-cancelling headphones, which part picks up the background noise in the first place?', '["The microphone", "The processing unit", "The ear cushion", "The music source"]', 0, 'One or more microphones capture the ambient noise and send it to the amplifier and processing unit.', '{"1": "It generates the anti-noise signal from what the microphone captures.", "2": "Padding blocks some sound passively but does not capture it.", "3": "The desired audio is mixed in later; it does not detect noise."}'),
  ('PHY-0790', 'Waves', 'Interference', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 136 (§7.3)', 'Two loudspeakers driven by the same audio generator act as coherent sources because they:', '["are placed close to each other", "emit sound of high intensity", "emit waves of the same frequency with a constant phase difference", "emit waves of different frequencies that are in phase at the start"]', 2, 'Driven by one generator, the speakers vibrate in phase at the same frequency, keeping a constant phase difference: they are coherent.', '{"0": "Distance between sources does not make them coherent.", "1": "Loudness has nothing to do with coherence.", "3": "Different frequencies cannot keep a constant phase relation."}'),
  ('PHY-0791', 'Waves', 'Interference', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 136 (§7.3)', 'Which pair of sources would produce a steady (sustained) interference pattern of sound?', '["Two separate tuning forks of 256 Hz and 260 Hz", "Two speakers each playing a different song", "Two speakers connected to the same audio generator", "A speaker and a person humming nearby"]', 2, 'Interference needs coherent sources of the same frequency. Two speakers fed from one generator are such sources.', '{"0": "Different frequencies produce beats, not a steady pattern.", "1": "The waves have no fixed frequency or phase relation.", "3": "Independent sources are not coherent."}'),
  ('PHY-0792', 'Waves', 'Interference', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 137 (§7.3)', 'Waves from two in-phase coherent sources reach a point with a path difference of 2.5λ. At that point the interference is:', '["constructive", "constructive, with amplitude 2.5 times that of one wave", "absent, because the paths are different", "destructive"]', 3, '2.5λ = (2n + 1)λ/2 with n = 2, the condition for destructive interference.', '{"0": "Constructive interference needs a whole number of wavelengths.", "1": "The amplitude does not scale with the path difference.", "2": "Interference happens at every point where the waves overlap."}'),
  ('PHY-0793', 'Waves', 'Interference', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 137 (§7.3)', 'Two in-phase sources emit sound of frequency 680 Hz; the speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹. The smallest path difference that gives destructive interference is:', '["0.25 m", "0.5 m", "1.0 m", "2.0 m"]', 0, 'λ = v/f = 340/680 = 0.5 m. The smallest destructive path difference is λ/2 = 0.25 m.', '{"1": "That is one whole wavelength, which gives constructive interference.", "2": "That is two wavelengths, constructive again.", "3": "This divides f by v instead of v by f."}'),
  ('PHY-0794', 'Waves', 'Interference', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 137 (§7.3)', 'Two in-phase loudspeakers are placed so that a microphone is 1.0 m farther from one than from the other. Taking the speed of sound as 340 m s⁻¹, the lowest frequency at which the microphone detects a minimum is:', '["85 Hz", "170 Hz", "340 Hz", "680 Hz"]', 1, 'The first minimum needs ΔS = λ/2, so λ = 2.0 m and f = v/λ = 340/2 = 170 Hz.', '{"0": "This uses a path difference of λ/4.", "2": "At 340 Hz the path difference is one whole wavelength, giving a maximum.", "3": "At 680 Hz the path difference is 2λ, also a maximum."}'),
  ('PHY-0795', 'Waves', 'Stationary waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 139 (§7.4)', 'In a stationary wave, the distance between a node and the next antinode is:', '["λ/4", "λ/2", "λ", "2λ"]', 0, 'Nodes are λ/2 apart and antinodes lie midway between them, so a node and the next antinode are λ/4 apart.', '{"1": "That is the node-to-node (or antinode-to-antinode) distance.", "2": "A full wavelength spans two loops.", "3": "That spans four loops."}'),
  ('PHY-0796', 'Waves', 'Stationary waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 140 (§7.4)', 'Which statement about energy in a stationary wave is correct?', '["Energy travels along the medium at the wave speed.", "Energy is greatest at the nodes.", "Energy cannot flow past the nodes, so it stays in the segments between them.", "The wave carries no energy at all."]', 2, 'Nodes are always at rest, so energy cannot flow past them; it remains "standing" between nodes, alternating between KE and PE.', '{"0": "That describes a progressive wave.", "1": "Nodes are permanently at rest.", "3": "Energy is present, alternating between kinetic and potential forms."}'),
  ('PHY-0797', 'Waves', 'Stationary waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 140 (§7.4)', 'On a string vibrating in a stationary wave of frequency 400 Hz, neighbouring antinodes are 0.25 m apart. The speed of the waves on the string is:', '["50 m s⁻¹", "100 m s⁻¹", "200 m s⁻¹", "400 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'Antinodes are λ/2 apart, so λ = 0.50 m and v = fλ = 400 × 0.50 = 200 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This uses λ = 0.125 m.", "1": "This takes the antinode spacing as the full wavelength.", "3": "This uses λ = 1.0 m."}'),
  ('PHY-0798', 'Waves', 'Stretched string', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 141 (§7.5)', 'A string 0.80 m long is fixed at both ends, and waves travel on it at 240 m s⁻¹. Its fundamental frequency is:', '["75 Hz", "150 Hz", "192 Hz", "300 Hz"]', 1, 'For the fundamental, λ = 2l = 1.6 m, so f₁ = v/2l = 240/1.6 = 150 Hz.', '{"0": "This uses f = v/4l, which is for a pipe closed at one end.", "2": "v and l are not multiplied.", "3": "This uses f = v/l; the fundamental has λ = 2l."}'),
  ('PHY-0799', 'Waves', 'Stretched string', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 142 (§7.5)', 'The tension in a stretched string is made four times larger while its length and mass per unit length stay the same. Its fundamental frequency:', '["halves", "stays the same", "becomes four times larger", "doubles"]', 3, 'v = √(F/m), so v doubles when F is ×4; f₁ = v/2l doubles too.', '{"0": "Greater tension raises the wave speed and frequency.", "1": "The wave speed depends on tension.", "2": "Frequency varies as the square root of tension."}'),
  ('PHY-0800', 'Waves', 'Stretched string', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 142 (§7.5)', 'A string under a tension of 64 N has a mass per unit length of 0.01 kg m⁻¹. The speed of transverse waves on it is:', '["8 m s⁻¹", "80 m s⁻¹", "640 m s⁻¹", "6400 m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'v = √(F/m) = √(64/0.01) = √6400 = 80 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "Check the arithmetic: 64/0.01 = 6400, and √6400 = 80.", "2": "This multiplies instead of taking the square root.", "3": "This forgets the square root."}'),
  ('PHY-0801', 'Waves', 'Stretched string', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 142 (§7.5)', 'A string 1.5 m long vibrates in 3 loops at a frequency of 150 Hz. Its fundamental frequency is:', '["25 Hz", "50 Hz", "150 Hz", "450 Hz"]', 1, 'With n loops, fₙ = nf₁, so f₁ = 150/3 = 50 Hz.', '{"0": "This would be the case for 6 loops.", "2": "This is the 3rd harmonic, the frequency given.", "3": "This multiplies by the number of loops instead of dividing."}'),
  ('PHY-0802', 'Waves', 'Stretched string', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 143 (§7.5)', 'A string fixed at both ends has a fundamental frequency of 60 Hz. Which of these can NOT be one of its natural frequencies?', '["120 Hz", "150 Hz", "180 Hz", "240 Hz"]', 1, 'A stretched string vibrates only at fₙ = nf₁ (quantised frequencies). 150 Hz is 2.5 × 60, not a whole-number multiple.', '{"0": "120 = 2 × 60, the 2nd harmonic.", "2": "180 = 3 × 60, the 3rd harmonic.", "3": "240 = 4 × 60, the 4th harmonic."}'),
  ('PHY-0803', 'Waves', 'Air columns', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 145 (§7.6)', 'A pipe that is closed at one end can produce:', '["only the odd harmonics (f₁, 3f₁, 5f₁, …)", "only the even harmonics (2f₁, 4f₁, …)", "all harmonics (f₁, 2f₁, 3f₁, …)", "only the fundamental"]', 0, 'With a node at the closed end and an antinode at the open end, fₙ = nv/4l with n = 1, 3, 5, …: only odd harmonics.', '{"1": "Even harmonics are missing in a closed pipe.", "2": "That is true of a pipe open at both ends.", "3": "Higher odd harmonics are also possible."}'),
  ('PHY-0804', 'Waves', 'Air columns', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 144 (§7.6)', 'An organ pipe 0.85 m long is open at both ends. If the speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹, its fundamental frequency is:', '["100 Hz", "200 Hz", "400 Hz", "800 Hz"]', 1, 'For an open pipe, f₁ = v/2l = 340/(2 × 0.85) = 200 Hz.', '{"0": "That would be the fundamental if the pipe were closed at one end.", "2": "This uses λ = l instead of λ = 2l.", "3": "This uses λ = l/2."}'),
  ('PHY-0805', 'Waves', 'Air columns', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 145 (§7.6)', 'A pipe 0.50 m long is closed at one end. The speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹. The lowest frequency above the fundamental at which it resonates is:', '["170 Hz", "340 Hz", "510 Hz", "680 Hz"]', 2, 'f₁ = v/4l = 340/2.0 = 170 Hz. A closed pipe gives only odd harmonics, so the next is 3f₁ = 510 Hz.', '{"0": "This is the fundamental itself.", "1": "That is 2f₁; even harmonics are absent in a closed pipe.", "3": "That is 4f₁, also an even harmonic."}'),
  ('PHY-0806', 'Waves', 'Air columns', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 146 (§7.6)', 'Two pipes have the same length; one is open at both ends and the other is closed at one end. The fundamental frequency of the open pipe is:', '["twice that of the closed pipe", "half that of the closed pipe", "equal to that of the closed pipe", "four times that of the closed pipe"]', 0, 'Open: f₁ = v/2l; closed: f₁ = v/4l. So the open pipe''s fundamental is twice as high.', '{"1": "The open pipe''s fundamental has the shorter wavelength (2l against 4l).", "2": "The two pipes have different end conditions.", "3": "The ratio is (v/2l)/(v/4l) = 2."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0807', 'Waves', 'Air columns', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 146 (§7.6)', 'An open pipe is said to be richer in harmonics than a closed pipe because it:', '["produces only odd harmonics", "has a lower fundamental frequency", "produces both odd and even harmonics", "has no nodes inside it"]', 2, 'An open pipe gives all harmonics (n = 1, 2, 3, …), while a closed pipe gives only odd ones, so the open pipe''s sound is richer in harmonics.', '{"0": "That is the closed pipe.", "1": "For the same length its fundamental is higher, and that is not what \"richer\" means.", "3": "Stationary waves in an open pipe do have nodes."}'),
  ('PHY-0808', 'Waves', 'Air columns', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 145 (§7.6)', 'For stationary sound waves in a pipe, there is always:', '["an antinode at a closed end and a node at an open end", "a node at both ends of any pipe", "an antinode at both ends of any pipe", "a displacement node at a closed end and an antinode at an open end"]', 3, 'Air at a closed end cannot move (node), while air at an open end moves freely (antinode).', '{"0": "Air cannot move at a closed end, so it must be a node.", "1": "An open end is free, so it is an antinode.", "2": "A closed end always has a node."}'),
  ('PHY-0809', 'Waves', 'Microwave stationary waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 149 (§7.7)', 'In the microwave experiment, a probe moved between the transmitter and a metal reflector shows successive maxima 1.5 cm apart. The wavelength of the microwaves is:', '["0.75 cm", "1.5 cm", "3.0 cm", "6.0 cm"]', 2, 'Successive maxima (antinodes) are λ/2 apart, so λ = 2 × 1.5 cm = 3.0 cm.', '{"0": "This halves the spacing instead of doubling it.", "1": "Adjacent antinodes are half a wavelength apart.", "3": "This takes the spacing as λ/4."}'),
  ('PHY-0810', 'Waves', 'Microwave stationary waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 149 (§7.7)', 'In the microwave experiment, the stationary wave between the transmitter and the metal plate is formed by:', '["interference between two separate transmitters", "diffraction of microwaves round the probe", "refraction of microwaves in the metal plate", "superposition of the incident waves and the waves reflected from the plate"]', 3, 'Waves reflected by the metal plate travel back and superpose with the incident waves, forming nodes and antinodes.', '{"0": "Only one transmitter is used.", "1": "The pattern comes from reflection and superposition.", "2": "Microwaves are reflected by metal, not refracted through it."}'),
  ('PHY-0811', 'Waves', 'Diffraction', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 150 (§7.8)', 'Waves passing through a gap spread out most when the width of the gap is:', '["comparable to or smaller than the wavelength", "many times larger than the wavelength", "unrelated to the wavelength, since every gap diffracts equally", "equal to the amplitude of the wave"]', 0, 'Diffraction is most noticeable when the gap is about the same size as, or smaller than, the wavelength.', '{"1": "Wide gaps give little spreading.", "2": "The amount of diffraction depends on gap width compared with λ.", "3": "Amplitude does not affect diffraction."}'),
  ('PHY-0812', 'Waves', 'Diffraction', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 150 (§7.8)', 'In a ripple tank, a dipper makes 300 ripples in 15 s and the ripples are 1.2 cm apart. The speed of the ripples is:', '["0.06 cm s⁻¹", "12.5 cm s⁻¹", "24 cm s⁻¹", "250 cm s⁻¹"]', 2, 'f = 300/15 s = 20 Hz, so v = fλ = 20 × 1.2 cm = 24 cm s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This divides λ by f instead of multiplying them.", "1": "This divides time by spacing.", "3": "This divides the number of ripples by the spacing."}'),
  ('PHY-0813', 'Waves', 'Diffraction', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 151 (§7.8)', 'X-rays are diffracted by crystals because:', '["X-rays have a very long wavelength", "crystals absorb all X-rays that strike them", "the spacing between atoms in a crystal is comparable to the wavelength of X-rays", "X-rays are mechanical waves"]', 2, 'Atomic spacing in crystals (about 10⁻¹⁰ m) is similar to X-ray wavelengths, so the regular rows of atoms diffract the X-rays.', '{"0": "X-ray wavelengths are very short, about 10⁻¹⁰ m.", "1": "Absorption cannot produce a diffraction pattern.", "3": "X-rays are electromagnetic."}'),
  ('PHY-0814', 'Waves', 'Diffraction', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 150 (§7.8)', 'We can hear a person talking behind a doorway but cannot see them because:', '["sound travels faster than light", "light cannot pass through a gap", "sound wavelengths are comparable to the doorway width, while light wavelengths are far smaller", "sound is a transverse wave"]', 2, 'Sound (λ about 1 m) diffracts strongly around a door-sized gap; light (λ about 5 × 10⁻⁷ m) barely diffracts.', '{"0": "Light is about a million times faster than sound.", "1": "Light passes through the doorway but spreads very little.", "3": "Sound in air is longitudinal; that is not the reason."}'),
  ('PHY-0815', 'Waves', 'Beats', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 151 (§7.9)', 'Two tuning forks of 512 Hz and 508 Hz are sounded together. The number of beats heard per second is:', '["2", "4", "8", "510"]', 1, 'The beat frequency equals the difference: 512 − 508 = 4 beats per second.', '{"0": "This halves the difference.", "2": "This doubles the difference.", "3": "That is the average frequency, not the beat frequency."}'),
  ('PHY-0816', 'Waves', 'Beats', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 152 (§7.9)', 'Two notes produce 5 beats per second. The time between two successive maxima of loudness is:', '["0.1 s", "0.2 s", "2.5 s", "5 s"]', 1, 'One beat is one rise and fall of loudness, so the beat period is 1/5 = 0.2 s.', '{"0": "This takes two maxima per beat.", "2": "This halves the beat frequency.", "3": "5 is the beat frequency, not the beat period."}'),
  ('PHY-0817', 'Waves', 'Beats', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 153 (§7.9)', 'Fork A (384 Hz) gives 6 beats per second with fork B. When B is loaded with a little wax, the beats fall to 2 per second. The original frequency of B was:', '["372 Hz", "378 Hz", "384 Hz", "390 Hz"]', 3, 'B is 384 ± 6 Hz. Wax lowers B''s frequency; the beats fall only if B was above A, so B = 390 Hz.', '{"0": "That would be A minus twice the beat frequency.", "1": "Wax lowers B''s frequency; from 378 Hz the beats would increase, not fall.", "2": "Equal frequencies would give no beats."}'),
  ('PHY-0818', 'Waves', 'Beats', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 153 (§7.9)', 'A practical use of beats is to:', '["measure the speed of a moving car", "find the wavelength of X-rays", "cancel background noise in headphones", "tune a musical instrument against a standard tuning fork"]', 3, 'Beats disappear when two frequencies become equal, so a musician adjusts the instrument until no beats are heard with the standard.', '{"0": "That uses the Doppler effect.", "1": "That uses diffraction by crystals.", "2": "That uses destructive superposition of anti-noise."}'),
  ('PHY-0819', 'Waves', 'Beats', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 151 (§7.9)', 'When two sounds produce beats, the loudness at a fixed point:', '["rises and falls periodically", "stays constant but louder than either sound", "is always zero", "doubles every second"]', 0, 'Beats are a periodic rise and fall in loudness caused by superposing two waves of slightly different frequencies.', '{"1": "That is steady constructive interference.", "2": "That is permanent cancellation.", "3": "Loudness alternates; it does not keep growing."}'),
  ('PHY-0820', 'Waves', 'Intensity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 154 (§7.10)', 'The SI unit of the intensity of a wave is:', '["W m⁻¹", "J m⁻²", "W m⁻²", "W"]', 2, 'Intensity I = P/A, power per unit area, measured in watts per square metre.', '{"0": "Intensity is power per unit area, not per unit length.", "1": "Intensity involves power (J s⁻¹), not just energy.", "3": "That is the unit of power."}'),
  ('PHY-0821', 'Waves', 'Intensity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 155 (§7.10)', 'A point source radiates sound uniformly in all directions with a power of 100 W. The intensity 2.0 m from the source is about:', '["1.99 W m⁻²", "3.98 W m⁻²", "7.96 W m⁻²", "25 W m⁻²"]', 0, 'I = P/4πr² = 100/(4π × 4.0) = 100/50.3 ≈ 1.99 W m⁻².', '{"1": "This uses 2πr² (a hemisphere) instead of 4πr².", "2": "This uses πr² instead of 4πr².", "3": "This divides by r² only."}'),
  ('PHY-0822', 'Waves', 'Intensity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 155 (§7.10)', 'The intensity of sound from a point source is 0.8 W m⁻² at a distance of 4 m. At 8 m from the source it is:', '["0.1 W m⁻²", "0.2 W m⁻²", "0.4 W m⁻²", "1.6 W m⁻²"]', 1, 'I ∝ 1/r². Doubling r divides I by 4: 0.8/4 = 0.2 W m⁻².', '{"0": "Intensity goes as 1/r², not 1/r³.", "2": "Intensity goes as 1/r², not 1/r.", "3": "Intensity falls, not rises, with distance."}'),
  ('PHY-0823', 'Waves', 'Doppler effect', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 156 (§7.11)', 'As a train approaches a stationary listener on a platform while sounding its whistle, the listener hears:', '["a lower pitch than the whistle actually emits", "exactly the emitted pitch", "no change in pitch but a higher speed of sound", "a higher pitch than the whistle actually emits"]', 3, 'Waves are crowded together in front of an approaching source, so more waves reach the listener per second: the apparent frequency (pitch) rises.', '{"0": "That happens as the train moves away.", "1": "Relative motion changes the received frequency.", "2": "The speed of sound in still air does not change."}'),
  ('PHY-0824', 'Waves', 'Doppler effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 157 (§7.11)', 'When a sound source moves towards a stationary listener, which quantity of the sound reaching the listener stays unchanged?', '["The speed of sound in the (still) air", "The wavelength", "The frequency", "Both wavelength and frequency"]', 0, 'The motion of the source shortens λ to (v − u_s)/f and raises the frequency, but the speed v of sound in still air is unchanged.', '{"1": "The wavelength in front of the source becomes shorter.", "2": "The apparent frequency becomes higher.", "3": "Both change; only the speed stays the same."}'),
  ('PHY-0825', 'Waves', 'Doppler effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 158 (§7.11)', 'An ambulance siren of frequency 680 Hz approaches a stationary observer at 20 m s⁻¹. If the speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹, the frequency heard is about:', '["642 Hz", "680 Hz", "700 Hz", "723 Hz"]', 3, 'f′ = vf/(v − u_s) = 340 × 680/(340 − 20) = 722.5 Hz ≈ 723 Hz.', '{"0": "This is the formula for a receding source.", "1": "This ignores the Doppler effect.", "2": "Speeds are not added to frequencies."}'),
  ('PHY-0826', 'Waves', 'Doppler effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 158 (§7.11)', 'A train whistle of frequency 500 Hz moves away from a stationary observer at 40 m s⁻¹. If sound travels at 360 m s⁻¹, the frequency heard is:', '["444 Hz", "450 Hz", "500 Hz", "563 Hz"]', 1, 'For a receding source f′ = vf/(v + u_s) = 360 × 500/400 = 450 Hz.', '{"0": "This uses f(1 − u/v), which is not the book''s formula for a moving source.", "2": "This ignores the Doppler effect.", "3": "This is the formula for an approaching source."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0827', 'Waves', 'Doppler effect', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 158 (§7.11)', 'A stationary listener hears a sound 1.25 times its true frequency as the source approaches. If the speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹, the speed of the source is:', '["68 m s⁻¹", "85 m s⁻¹", "272 m s⁻¹", "425 m s⁻¹"]', 0, 'v/(v − u_s) = 1.25 gives v − u_s = 0.8v, so u_s = 0.2 × 340 = 68 m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "This takes u_s = 0.25v.", "2": "This is v − u_s, not u_s.", "3": "A source faster than sound would not fit this formula."}'),
  ('PHY-0828', 'Waves', 'Doppler effect', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 157 (§7.11)', 'A source emits sound of frequency f and moves towards a stationary listener at speed u_s; the speed of sound is v. The wavelength reaching the listener is:', '["(v − u_s)/f", "(v + u_s)/f", "v/f", "vf/(v − u_s)"]', 0, 'In each period the source moves u_s/f towards the listener, shortening the wavelength to λ′ = (v − u_s)/f.', '{"1": "That is the longer wavelength behind the source.", "2": "That is the wavelength when the source is at rest.", "3": "That is the apparent frequency, not the wavelength."}'),
  ('PHY-0829', 'Waves', 'Doppler applications', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 160 (§7.12)', 'The acronym SONAR stands for:', '["Sonic Oscillation And Reflection", "Sound Navigation And Ranging", "Sound Of Natural Acoustic Resonance", "Signal Operated Navigation And Radar"]', 1, 'SONAR = Sound Navigation And Ranging, an underwater echo-ranging system that can use the Doppler shift to find a target''s speed.', '{"0": "Not the meaning of the acronym.", "2": "Not the meaning of the acronym.", "3": "Radar uses radio waves; sonar uses sound."}'),
  ('PHY-0830', 'Waves', 'Doppler applications', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 161 (§7.12)', 'A radar speed trap finds the speed of a car by measuring:', '["the time for sound to echo back from the car", "the diffraction of light around the car", "the Doppler shift in the frequency of microwaves reflected from the car", "the beats between two engines"]', 2, 'Microwave bursts reflected from a moving car are Doppler shifted; the size of the shift gives the car''s speed.', '{"0": "Speed traps use microwaves, not sound echoes.", "1": "Diffraction is not used.", "3": "Car speed is found from the Doppler shift."}'),
  ('PHY-0831', 'Waves', 'Doppler applications', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 162 (§7.12)', 'A spectral line measured at 400 nm in the laboratory is observed at 440 nm in the light from a distant galaxy. Using the textbook''s relation f′ = cf/(c + u), the galaxy''s speed is (c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹):', '["2.7 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹, towards the Earth", "3.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹, away from the Earth", "3.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹, towards the Earth", "3.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, away from the Earth"]', 1, 'λ′/λ = (c + u)/c, so u = c(440/400 − 1) = 0.1c = 3.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹. The red shift means it is moving away.', '{"0": "The shift to longer wavelength (red shift) means the galaxy recedes.", "2": "A longer observed wavelength means recession, not approach.", "3": "That is the speed of light itself."}'),
  ('PHY-0832', 'Waves', 'Doppler applications', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 161 (§7.12)', 'The light from a star shows a blue shift. This means the star is:', '["moving away from the Earth", "at rest relative to the Earth", "getting hotter", "moving towards the Earth"]', 3, 'An approaching star''s light arrives with shorter wavelengths, shifting its spectrum towards the blue end.', '{"0": "Receding stars show a red shift.", "1": "A star at rest shows no shift.", "2": "The Doppler shift depends on relative motion, not temperature."}'),
  ('PHY-0833', 'Waves', 'Doppler applications', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 162 (§7.12)', 'In medicine, Doppler echocardiography is used to:', '["measure the speed of blood flow and detect heart-valve problems", "take X-ray images of bones", "measure body temperature", "measure the electrical activity of the heart"]', 0, 'Doppler echocardiography measures blood-flow velocity and detects cardiac abnormalities such as valve stenosis or regurgitation.', '{"1": "X-ray imaging does not use the Doppler shift.", "2": "That needs a thermometer or IR sensor.", "3": "That is an ECG."}'),
  ('PHY-0834', 'Waves', 'Hearing range', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 7 Waves and Vibrations, p. 158 (§7.11)', 'According to the hearing ranges given in the textbook, a 40 kHz whistle can be heard by:', '["a human, but not a dog", "a dog, but not a human", "both a human and a dog", "neither a human nor a dog"]', 1, 'The book gives about 20–20,000 Hz for humans and 15–50,000 Hz for dogs, so 40 kHz is audible to a dog only.', '{"0": "Humans hear only up to about 20 kHz.", "2": "40 kHz is above the human limit of about 20 kHz.", "3": "Dogs hear up to about 50 kHz."}'),
  ('PHY-0835', 'Physical optics', 'Polarization', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 167 (§8.1)', 'Interference and diffraction show that light is a wave. The phenomenon that shows light waves are transverse is:', '["reflection", "refraction", "diffraction", "polarization"]', 3, 'Only transverse waves can be polarized, so the polarization of light shows that light waves are transverse.', '{"0": "Both transverse and longitudinal waves can be reflected.", "1": "Refraction happens for both kinds of wave.", "2": "Diffraction shows wave nature but not whether the wave is transverse."}'),
  ('PHY-0836', 'Physical optics', 'Polarization', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 168 (§8.1)', 'Which of these waves can NOT be polarized?', '["Radio waves", "Visible light", "Waves on a stretched string", "Sound waves in air"]', 3, 'Polarization is shown only by transverse waves. Sound in air is longitudinal, so it cannot be polarized.', '{"0": "Radio waves are transverse EM waves and can be polarized.", "1": "Light is transverse and can be polarized.", "2": "These are transverse mechanical waves and can be polarized."}'),
  ('PHY-0837', 'Physical optics', 'Polarization', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 168 (§8.1)', 'Which of these gives unpolarized light?', '["An incandescent light bulb", "Light that has passed through a polaroid sheet", "Light reflected from glass at the polarizing angle", "Light emerging from an analyzer"]', 0, 'Ordinary sources such as sunlight, incandescent and fluorescent bulbs and candle flames have vibrations in all planes: unpolarized light.', '{"1": "A polaroid transmits only one plane of vibration, so its output is polarized.", "2": "Such light is (almost) plane polarized.", "3": "An analyzer is also a polarizer; its output is polarized."}'),
  ('PHY-0838', 'Physical optics', 'Polarization', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 168 (§8.1)', 'For a polarized transverse wave, the plane of polarization is the plane that contains:', '["the direction of propagation only", "all possible directions of vibration", "the electric and magnetic field vectors only", "the direction of vibration and the direction of propagation"]', 3, 'The textbook defines the plane of polarization as the plane containing the direction of vibration of the particles (or E-field) and the direction of propagation.', '{"0": "A single direction does not define a plane.", "1": "That describes unpolarized light.", "2": "The plane is defined with the direction of travel included."}'),
  ('PHY-0839', 'Physical optics', 'Polarization', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 168 (§8.1)', 'Which of these is NOT one of the textbook''s methods of polarizing unpolarized light?', '["Passing it straight through a sheet of ordinary window glass", "Passing it through a polaroid sheet", "Using a polarizing beam splitter", "Passing it through a calcite crystal"]', 0, 'The book lists polarizing filters (polaroid), polarizing beam splitters and certain crystals such as calcite or quartz. Ordinary glass at normal incidence does not polarize light.', '{"1": "This is the most common method.", "2": "This is listed as a method.", "3": "Certain crystals such as calcite and quartz polarize light."}'),
  ('PHY-0840', 'Physical optics', 'Types of polarization', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 169 (§8.2)', 'If the electric field vector of a light wave rotates in a circle (clockwise or anticlockwise) as the wave travels, the light is:', '["linearly polarized", "elliptically polarized", "circularly polarized", "unpolarized"]', 2, 'When E rotates circularly, right- or left-handed, the light is circularly polarized.', '{"0": "In linear polarization E oscillates in a single plane.", "1": "In elliptical polarization E traces an ellipse.", "3": "Unpolarized light has randomly oriented vibrations."}'),
  ('PHY-0841', 'Physical optics', 'Types of polarization', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 169 (§8.2)', 'Light in which the two components Ex and Ey of the electric field are unequal, or differ in phase by an arbitrary angle, so that E traces an ellipse, is called:', '["linearly polarized", "circularly polarized", "unpolarized", "elliptically polarized"]', 3, 'Elliptical polarization is a combination of linear and circular polarization; E traces an elliptical path.', '{"0": "E would stay in one plane.", "1": "Circular polarization needs equal components 90° out of phase.", "2": "Unpolarized light has no fixed pattern of E."}'),
  ('PHY-0842', 'Physical optics', 'Production and detection', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 169 (§8.3)', 'Unpolarized light passes through a polarizer and then an analyzer. The analyzer is turned slowly through one complete rotation (360°). How many times does the transmitted light disappear?', '["1", "2", "3", "4"]', 1, 'Light is extinguished whenever the axes are perpendicular: at 90° and at 270°, which is twice per revolution.', '{"0": "The axes are perpendicular twice in each full turn.", "2": "Count the angles 90° and 270° only.", "3": "The light is brightest (not extinguished) at 0° and 180°."}'),
  ('PHY-0843', 'Physical optics', 'Production and detection', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 170 (§8.3)', 'If light waves were longitudinal, what would be seen when the axes of a polarizer and an analyzer are made perpendicular?', '["The light would still pass through the analyzer.", "The light would be extinguished.", "The light would change colour.", "The light would be reflected back by the analyzer."]', 0, 'Longitudinal vibrations have no direction across the beam for a polaroid to block, so they would never disappear. The fact that light does disappear proves it is transverse.', '{"1": "That is what actually happens, because light is transverse.", "2": "Polaroids do not change the colour of light in this way.", "3": "Crossed polaroids absorb, not reflect."}'),
  ('PHY-0844', 'Physical optics', 'Brewster''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 171 (§8.4)', 'Light strikes glass of refractive index 1.6 from air. The polarizing (Brewster) angle is about:', '["32.0°", "38.7°", "51.3°", "58.0°"]', 3, 'tan θp = n = 1.6, so θp = tan⁻¹ 1.6 ≈ 58.0°.', '{"0": "That is the angle of refraction at the polarizing angle.", "1": "That is sin⁻¹(1/1.6), the critical angle.", "2": "That is cos⁻¹(1/1.6)."}'),
  ('PHY-0845', 'Physical optics', 'Brewster''s law', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 171 (§8.4)', 'When unpolarized light strikes a transparent surface at the polarizing angle, the reflected and refracted rays are:', '["perpendicular to each other", "parallel to each other", "at 45° to each other", "the same ray, because no light is refracted"]', 0, 'At the polarizing angle the reflected and refracted beams are at right angles, so θp + θr = 90°, which leads to tan θp = n.', '{"1": "They travel in different media in different directions.", "2": "The angle between them is 90°.", "3": "Most of the light is still refracted."}'),
  ('PHY-0846', 'Physical optics', 'Brewster''s law', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 171 (§8.4)', 'Light falls on water (n = 1.33) from air at the polarizing angle. The angle of refraction in the water is about:', '["36.9°", "45.0°", "48.8°", "53.1°"]', 0, 'θp = tan⁻¹ 1.33 ≈ 53.1°. At this angle θr = 90° − θp ≈ 36.9°.', '{"1": "The polarizing angle is not 45°; tan θp = n.", "2": "That is the critical angle sin⁻¹(1/1.33).", "3": "That is the polarizing angle, the angle of incidence."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0847', 'Physical optics', 'Brewster''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 171 (§8.4)', 'Reflected light from a medium is found to be plane polarized when the angle of incidence is 56°. The refractive index of the medium is about:', '["0.56", "0.83", "1.48", "1.79"]', 2, 'Brewster''s law: n = tan θp = tan 56° ≈ 1.48.', '{"0": "That is cos 56°.", "1": "That is sin 56°.", "3": "That is 1/cos 56°."}'),
  ('PHY-0848', 'Physical optics', 'Polarization by reflection', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 170 (§8.4)', 'Light reflected from the smooth surface of water is:', '["always completely unpolarized", "partially polarized parallel to the surface", "polarized at right angles to the surface", "turned into circularly polarized light"]', 1, 'Light reflected from water is partially polarized parallel to the surface. Polaroid sunglasses block this component to cut glare.', '{"0": "The book notes that reflected light is partly polarized.", "2": "The vibrations are mostly parallel to the surface.", "3": "Reflection from water gives (partial) plane polarization."}'),
  ('PHY-0849', 'Physical optics', 'Malus''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 172 (§8.5)', 'Plane polarized light of intensity 48 W m⁻² falls on an analyzer whose axis is at 60° to the plane of polarization. The transmitted intensity is:', '["6 W m⁻²", "12 W m⁻²", "24 W m⁻²", "36 W m⁻²"]', 1, 'I = I₀ cos²θ = 48 × (0.5)² = 12 W m⁻².', '{"0": "This takes cos²60° as 0.125; cos²60° = 0.25.", "2": "This uses cos θ instead of cos²θ.", "3": "This uses sin²θ instead of cos²θ."}'),
  ('PHY-0850', 'Physical optics', 'Malus''s law', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 172 (§8.5)', 'Unpolarized light of intensity 60 W m⁻² passes through a polarizer and then an analyzer whose axis is at 30° to the polarizer''s axis. The final intensity is:', '["15 W m⁻²", "22.5 W m⁻²", "30 W m⁻²", "45 W m⁻²"]', 1, 'The polarizer passes half: 30 W m⁻². Then I = 30 cos²30° = 30 × 0.75 = 22.5 W m⁻².', '{"0": "This uses sin²30° instead of cos²30°.", "2": "That is the intensity after the first polarizer only.", "3": "This forgets that the first polarizer halves unpolarized light."}'),
  ('PHY-0851', 'Physical optics', 'Malus''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 173 (§8.5)', 'Plane polarized light of amplitude 12 units reaches an analyzer whose axis is at 60° to the plane of polarization. The amplitude of the transmitted light is:', '["3 units", "6 units", "10.4 units", "12 units"]', 1, 'Only the component A₀ cos θ passes: 12 × cos 60° = 6 units.', '{"0": "This uses cos²θ, which applies to intensity, not amplitude.", "2": "This uses sin θ, the component absorbed.", "3": "Some of the amplitude is absorbed."}'),
  ('PHY-0852', 'Physical optics', 'Malus''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 173 (§8.5)', 'At what angle between the axes of the polarizer and analyzer is the intensity of polarized light reduced to 75% of its value?', '["30°", "41.4°", "45°", "60°"]', 0, 'cos²θ = 0.75 gives cos θ = 0.866, so θ = 30°.', '{"1": "That is cos⁻¹ 0.75; it forgets the square.", "2": "At 45° the intensity is 50%.", "3": "At 60° the intensity is 25%."}'),
  ('PHY-0853', 'Physical optics', 'Malus''s law', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 173 (§8.5)', 'Unpolarized light of intensity I₀ passes through two crossed polaroids, so no light emerges. A third polaroid is placed between them with its axis at 45° to each. The intensity that now emerges is:', '["zero", "I₀/4", "I₀/8", "I₀/2"]', 2, 'After the first: I₀/2. After the middle: (I₀/2)cos²45° = I₀/4. After the last: (I₀/4)cos²45° = I₀/8.', '{"0": "The middle polaroid gives the light a component along the last axis.", "1": "This forgets that the first polaroid halves the unpolarized light.", "3": "That is the intensity after the first polaroid only."}'),
  ('PHY-0854', 'Physical optics', 'Polaroids', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 172 (§8.5)', 'Which material is given in the textbook as one that confines light vibrations to a single plane (a polaroid material)?', '["Rock salt", "Ordinary window glass", "Copper", "Tourmaline"]', 3, 'Crystals such as tourmaline and calcite have high directionality and pass vibrations in one plane only.', '{"0": "Rock salt has no such directionality.", "1": "Ordinary glass transmits all planes of vibration.", "2": "A metal is opaque to light."}'),
  ('PHY-0855', 'Physical optics', 'Optical activity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 173 (§8.5)', 'Which of these is optically active?', '["A sugar solution", "Distilled water", "Air", "A flat glass plate"]', 0, 'Sugar solutions (and quartz, cinnabar, insulin, collagen) rotate the plane of polarization: they are optically active.', '{"1": "Water does not rotate the plane of polarization.", "2": "Air is not optically active.", "3": "Ordinary glass does not rotate the plane of polarization."}'),
  ('PHY-0856', 'Physical optics', 'Optical activity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 173 (§8.5)', 'The angle through which an optically active substance rotates the plane of polarization does NOT depend on:', '["the concentration of the solution", "the intensity of the light", "the distance the light travels through the substance", "the wavelength of the light"]', 1, 'The book lists the type of substance, its concentration, the path length and the wavelength. Intensity is not one of them.', '{"0": "Rotation increases with concentration.", "2": "A longer path gives more rotation.", "3": "Rotation depends on wavelength."}'),
  ('PHY-0857', 'Physical optics', 'Optical activity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 174 (§8.5)', 'Optical activity is used in practice to:', '["measure the speed of light", "find the refractive index of glass", "find the concentration of sugar in a solution", "measure the frequency of sound"]', 2, 'The rotation depends on concentration, so measuring it gives the concentration of an optically active solution such as sugar.', '{"0": "Optical activity does not measure light speed.", "1": "Brewster''s law can, not optical activity.", "3": "Sound cannot be polarized."}'),
  ('PHY-0858', 'Physical optics', 'Optical activity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 173 (§8.5)', 'Optical activity in a substance is caused by:', '["the high temperature of the substance", "free electrons in the substance", "the colour of the substance", "the asymmetric (for example helical) shape of its molecules"]', 3, 'The book explains that optical activity arises from asymmetric molecules, such as those with a helical shape.', '{"0": "Temperature is not the cause.", "1": "Free electrons make a metal conducting, not optically active.", "2": "Colourless sugar solutions are optically active."}'),
  ('PHY-0859', 'Physical optics', 'Applications of polarization', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 175 (§8.5)', 'In sky photography, a polarizing filter on the camera improves the image mainly by:', '["reducing the glare and haze caused by scattering of light in the atmosphere", "increasing the total amount of light reaching the film", "making the clouds move more slowly", "changing the wavelength of the light"]', 0, 'Scattered skylight is partly polarized; the polarizer cuts this glare and haze and increases contrast.', '{"1": "A filter removes some light.", "2": "A filter has no effect on the scene itself.", "3": "Polarizers do not change wavelength."}'),
  ('PHY-0860', 'Physical optics', 'Applications of polarization', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 176 (§8.5)', 'Analyzing the stress in a transparent plastic part by shining polarized light through it and studying the fringes formed is called:', '["photoelectric effect", "photoelasticity", "holography", "optical activity"]', 1, 'Stress changes how the material affects polarized light, giving fringe patterns. This technique is photoelasticity.', '{"0": "That is the emission of electrons by light.", "2": "Holography records 3D images by interference of laser light.", "3": "Optical activity is the rotation of the plane of polarization by certain substances."}'),
  ('PHY-0861', 'Physical optics', 'Applications of polarization', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 175 (§8.5)', 'Which of these devices depends on the polarization of light?', '["An incandescent bulb", "A magnifying glass", "An LCD screen", "A glass prism spectrometer"]', 2, 'LCD screens, like polarized sunglasses and optical communication systems, rely on polarization.', '{"0": "It emits unpolarized light.", "1": "It works by refraction.", "3": "It works by dispersion."}'),
  ('PHY-0862', 'Gravitational waves', 'Gravitational waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 176 (§8.6)', 'Gravitational waves travel through space at:', '["the speed of sound", "a speed much lower than that of light", "an infinite speed, arriving instantly", "the speed of light"]', 3, 'Gravitational waves are ripples in space-time that travel at the speed of light.', '{"0": "They are not mechanical waves.", "1": "The book states they travel at the speed of light.", "2": "They take time to arrive (1.3 billion years for the first event detected)."}'),
  ('PHY-0863', 'Gravitational waves', 'Gravitational waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 176 (§8.6)', 'According to Einstein''s general theory of relativity, gravity is:', '["a curvature of space-time caused by massive bodies", "an electric force between masses", "a force that acts only on Earth", "a kind of sound wave in space"]', 0, 'General relativity describes gravity as the curvature of space-time produced by mass, like a heavy object denting a mattress.', '{"1": "Gravity is not electrical.", "2": "Gravity acts throughout the universe.", "3": "Sound cannot travel through empty space."}'),
  ('PHY-0864', 'Gravitational waves', 'Gravitational waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 180 (§8.7)', 'Gravitational waves were first observed directly by LIGO in:', '["1916", "2015", "2016", "2017"]', 1, 'LIGO first observed gravitational waves on 14 September 2015; the result was announced in 2016.', '{"0": "That is when Einstein predicted them.", "2": "The discovery was announced in 2016; the detection was in 2015.", "3": "That is the year of the Nobel Prize and Virgo''s first detection."}'),
  ('PHY-0865', 'Gravitational waves', 'Types of gravitational waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 177 (§8.6)', 'Gravitational waves of constant frequency and amplitude, produced by a single massive object such as a neutron star spinning at a constant rate, are called:', '["burst gravitational waves", "continuous gravitational waves", "stochastic gravitational waves", "compact binary inspiral waves"]', 1, 'A steadily spinning massive object gives continuous gravitational waves of constant frequency and amplitude.', '{"0": "Bursts come from violent events such as supernovas.", "2": "These are weak random signals from many sources.", "3": "These come from two objects orbiting each other."}'),
  ('PHY-0866', 'Gravitational waves', 'Types of gravitational waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 177 (§8.6)', 'Which type of gravitational wave is the most difficult to detect?', '["Burst waves", "Continuous waves", "Stochastic waves", "Compact binary inspiral waves"]', 2, 'Stochastic waves are weak, random signals formed by superposition of many weak sources, so they are the hardest to detect.', '{"0": "These come from violent single events and are stronger.", "1": "These have constant frequency and amplitude.", "3": "These were the first to be detected."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0867', 'Gravitational waves', 'Types of gravitational waves', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 177 (§8.6)', 'Burst gravitational waves are produced by:', '["a neutron star spinning steadily", "many distant weak sources together", "vehicles and aeroplanes on Earth", "violent events such as supernovas and gamma-ray bursts"]', 3, 'The book lists supernovas, gamma-ray bursts and cosmic strings as sources of burst gravitational waves.', '{"0": "That gives continuous waves.", "1": "That gives stochastic waves.", "2": "These produce waves far too weak to detect."}'),
  ('PHY-0868', 'Gravitational waves', 'Gravitational waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 177 (§8.6)', 'Accelerating cars and aeroplanes produce gravitational waves, yet none is detected. This is because:', '["their masses and accelerations are far too small to produce detectable waves", "they do not accelerate", "only stars can produce gravitational waves", "the atmosphere absorbs gravitational waves"]', 0, 'Every accelerating object produces gravitational waves, but on Earth masses and accelerations are far too small for the waves to be detected.', '{"1": "They do accelerate; the book says every accelerating object produces gravitational waves.", "2": "Any accelerating mass produces them.", "3": "Gravitational waves pass even through the whole Earth."}'),
  ('PHY-0869', 'Gravitational waves', 'Space-time distortion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 179 (§8.6)', 'As a gravitational wave passes through a body, the body:', '["is heated strongly", "is stretched and compressed periodically in directions perpendicular to the wave''s travel", "is pushed steadily along the direction of the wave", "becomes electrically charged"]', 1, 'A passing gravitational wave causes periodic stretching and compressing ("tidal forces") at right angles to its direction of travel.', '{"0": "Gravitational waves are not a heating effect.", "2": "The effect is an oscillating stretch and squeeze, not a push.", "3": "Gravitational waves do not charge matter."}'),
  ('PHY-0870', 'Gravitational waves', 'Gravitational waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 181 (§8.7)', 'A gravitational wave has a wavelength of 6000 km. Its frequency is (c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹):', '["0.02 Hz", "5 Hz", "50 Hz", "500 Hz"]', 2, 'f = c/λ = (3 × 10⁸)/(6 × 10⁶) = 50 Hz.', '{"0": "This divides λ by c.", "1": "Check the conversion: 6000 km = 6 × 10⁶ m.", "3": "Check the powers of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-0871', 'Gravitational waves', 'Gravitational waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 181 (§8.7)', 'A gravitational wave has a frequency of 250 Hz. Its wavelength is (c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹):', '["12 km", "120 km", "1200 km", "12000 km"]', 2, 'λ = c/f = (3 × 10⁸)/250 = 1.2 × 10⁶ m = 1200 km.', '{"0": "Check the powers of ten: 3 × 10⁸/250 = 1.2 × 10⁶ m.", "1": "1.2 × 10⁶ m is 1200 km, not 120 km.", "3": "Check the powers of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-0872', 'Gravitational waves', 'Interferometer', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 179 (§8.7)', 'In the name LIGO, the letters stand for:', '["Light Intensity Gravity Oscillator", "Large Ionized Gas Observatory", "Laser Interferometer Gravitational-wave Observatory", "Laser Imaging of Galactic Objects"]', 2, 'LIGO is the Laser Interferometer Gravitational-wave Observatory in the USA.', '{"0": "Not the meaning of the acronym.", "1": "Not the meaning of the acronym.", "3": "Not the meaning of the acronym."}'),
  ('PHY-0873', 'Gravitational waves', 'Interferometer', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 181 (§8.7)', 'Each arm of the LIGO interferometer is about:', '["0.4 km long", "3 km long", "4 km long", "40 km long"]', 2, 'LIGO has 4 km arms; the Virgo interferometer has 3 km arms.', '{"0": "This is ten times too short.", "1": "That is the arm length of Virgo in Italy.", "3": "This is ten times too long."}'),
  ('PHY-0874', 'Gravitational waves', 'Interferometer', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 180 (§8.7)', 'In a gravitational-wave interferometer, the beam splitter:', '["produces the laser light", "measures the returning beams", "reflects used light back into the instrument", "divides the laser beam into two perpendicular beams"]', 3, 'The beam splitter divides the laser beam into two beams sent down the two perpendicular arms.', '{"0": "The laser does that.", "1": "The photodetector does that.", "2": "That is the power recycling mirror."}'),
  ('PHY-0875', 'Gravitational waves', 'Interferometer', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 180 (§8.7)', 'In a LIGO interferometer, the purpose of the Fabry–Perot cavity (two mirrors facing each other in each arm) is to:', '["increase the effective path length of the light", "split the beam into two", "produce a stable laser beam", "detect the interference pattern"]', 0, 'The light bounces many times between the two mirrors of the Fabry–Perot cavity, increasing the path length and so the sensitivity.', '{"1": "That is the beam splitter.", "2": "That is the laser.", "3": "That is the photodetector."}'),
  ('PHY-0876', 'Gravitational waves', 'Interferometer', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 180 (§8.7)', 'An interferometer senses a passing gravitational wave because the wave:', '["changes the colour of the laser light", "changes the arm lengths slightly, shifting the phase between the two beams and altering the interference pattern", "makes the laser switch off", "produces a sound that the photodetector hears"]', 1, 'The wave changes the distance between the mirrors by a tiny amount; the returning beams then recombine with a phase shift, changing the pattern at the photodetector.', '{"0": "The wave changes distances, not the colour of the source.", "2": "The laser keeps running.", "3": "The photodetector measures light, not sound."}'),
  ('PHY-0877', 'Gravitational waves', 'Interferometer', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 180 (§8.7)', 'In a gravitational-wave interferometer, the power recycling mirror:', '["divides the beam into two arms", "measures the phase shift", "sends light that has already travelled through the instrument back into it", "stretches the arms when a wave arrives"]', 2, 'The power recycling mirror continually reflects laser light that has already been through the instrument back in, hence the name.', '{"0": "That is the beam splitter.", "1": "That is the photodetector.", "3": "Mirrors do not cause the stretching."}'),
  ('PHY-0878', 'Gravitational waves', 'Binary systems', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 177 (§8.6)', 'In the study of gravitational waves, a binary system is:', '["a single star spinning on its axis", "a computer that stores data in 0s and 1s", "two planets that never interact", "two compact objects, such as black holes or neutron stars, orbiting each other"]', 3, 'A binary system consists of two compact objects (black holes, neutron stars or white dwarfs) orbiting each other and emitting gravitational waves.', '{"0": "A binary system has two objects.", "1": "That is a different meaning of \"binary\".", "2": "The objects orbit each other and interact strongly."}'),
  ('PHY-0879', 'Gravitational waves', 'Binary systems', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 178 (§8.6)', 'An orbit with an eccentricity of 0.4 is:', '["elliptical", "circular", "parabolic", "hyperbolic"]', 0, 'For 0 < e < 1 the orbit is an ellipse.', '{"1": "A circular orbit has e = 0.", "2": "A parabolic path has e = 1.", "3": "A hyperbolic path has e > 1."}'),
  ('PHY-0880', 'Gravitational waves', 'Gravitational waves', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 8 Physical Optics and Gravitational Waves, p. 181 (§8.7)', 'The 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics for the observation of gravitational waves went to:', '["Albert Einstein", "Rainer Weiss, Barry Barish and Kip Thorne", "Nergis Mavalvala alone", "Stephen Hawking and Roger Penrose"]', 1, 'Rainer Weiss, Barry C. Barish and Kip S. Thorne received the 2017 Nobel Prize for their work on observing gravitational waves.', '{"0": "Einstein predicted them in 1916 and died in 1955; Nobel Prizes are not given after death.", "2": "She was a member of the LIGO team but not a laureate.", "3": "Neither received the prize for gravitational-wave detection."}'),
  ('PHY-0881', 'Electrostatics', 'Coulomb''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 187 (§9.1)', 'Two point charges in air repel each other with a force of 0.8 N. Keeping the separation the same, they are placed in a medium of relative permittivity 4. The force becomes:', '["0.05 N", "0.2 N", "0.8 N", "3.2 N"]', 1, 'In a medium F = F_air/εr = 0.8/4 = 0.2 N.', '{"0": "This divides by εr², but the force is divided by εr once.", "2": "A dielectric always reduces the electrostatic force.", "3": "A dielectric reduces the force; it does not increase it."}'),
  ('PHY-0882', 'Electrostatics', 'Coulomb''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 186 (§9.1)', 'Two point charges of 2 μC and 5 μC are 0.3 m apart in air. The force between them is (k = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻²):', '["0.3 N", "1.0 N", "10 N", "100 N"]', 1, 'F = kq₁q₂/r² = (9 × 10⁹)(2 × 10⁻⁶)(5 × 10⁻⁶)/(0.3)² = 0.09/0.09 = 1.0 N.', '{"0": "This divides by r instead of r².", "2": "Check the powers of ten: 2 μC × 5 μC = 10 × 10⁻¹² C².", "3": "Check the powers of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-0883', 'Electrostatics', 'Coulomb''s law', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 186 (§9.1)', 'The SI unit of the permittivity of free space, ε₀, is:', '["N m² C⁻²", "N C⁻¹", "C m⁻²", "C² N⁻¹ m⁻²"]', 3, 'From F = q₁q₂/4πε₀r², ε₀ = q₁q₂/(4πFr²), with unit C²/(N m²) = C² N⁻¹ m⁻² (value 8.85 × 10⁻¹² in SI).', '{"0": "That is the unit of k = 1/4πε₀.", "1": "That is the unit of electric field.", "2": "That is the unit of surface charge density."}'),
  ('PHY-0884', 'Electrostatics', 'Coulomb''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 187 (§9.1)', 'A charge of +4q and a charge of +q are placed a short distance apart. Compared with the force on the +q charge, the force on the +4q charge is:', '["equal in size and opposite in direction", "four times as large", "one quarter as large", "zero, because the larger charge is not affected by the smaller one"]', 0, 'Coulomb''s force is mutual: F₁₂ = −F₂₁. Both charges feel the same magnitude kq₁q₂/r² in opposite directions.', '{"1": "Coulomb forces are mutual; both feel the same magnitude.", "2": "The force on each depends on the product q₁q₂, the same for both.", "3": "Each charge exerts a force on the other."}'),
  ('PHY-0885', 'Electrostatics', 'Coulomb''s law', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 187 (§9.1)', 'From Table 9.1 of the textbook, the material whose relative permittivity is closest to 1 is:', '["distilled water", "glass", "mica", "air"]', 3, 'Air has εr ≈ 1.0006, so Coulomb''s law in air is practically the same as in vacuum.', '{"0": "Water has a very large relative permittivity (about 80).", "1": "Glass has εr of several units.", "2": "Mica has εr of several units."}'),
  ('PHY-0886', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 197 (§9.5)', 'The unit V m⁻¹ of electric field strength is equivalent to:', '["N C⁻¹", "N m C⁻¹", "J C⁻¹", "C N⁻¹"]', 0, '1 V m⁻¹ = 1 J C⁻¹ m⁻¹ = 1 N m C⁻¹ m⁻¹ = 1 N C⁻¹.', '{"1": "Check: V/m = (J/C)/m = (N m)/(C m) = N/C.", "2": "That is the volt itself.", "3": "This is upside down."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0887', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 189 (§9.2)', 'The electric field 0.3 m from a point charge of 4 nC in air is (k = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻²):', '["40 N C⁻¹", "120 N C⁻¹", "400 N C⁻¹", "1200 N C⁻¹"]', 2, 'E = kq/r² = (9 × 10⁹ × 4 × 10⁻⁹)/(0.3)² = 36/0.09 = 400 N C⁻¹.', '{"0": "Check the powers of ten.", "1": "This divides by r instead of r².", "3": "This divides by 0.03 instead of 0.09."}'),
  ('PHY-0888', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 189 (§9.2)', 'The distance from a point charge is tripled. The electric field strength becomes:', '["one-third of its original value", "three times its original value", "nine times its original value", "one-ninth of its original value"]', 3, 'E = kq/r², so tripling r divides E by 3² = 9.', '{"0": "E varies as 1/r², not 1/r.", "1": "The field weakens with distance.", "2": "The field weakens with distance."}'),
  ('PHY-0889', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 189 (§9.2)', 'Charges of +9 μC and +1 μC are 4 m apart. At what distance from the +9 μC charge, on the line between them, is the electric field zero?', '["1 m", "2 m", "3 m", "3.6 m"]', 2, '9/d² = 1/(4 − d)² gives 3/d = 1/(4 − d), so 12 − 3d = d and d = 3 m.', '{"0": "That is the distance from the +1 μC charge.", "1": "At the midpoint the larger charge''s field is nine times stronger.", "3": "This uses a 9 : 1 ratio of distances instead of 3 : 1."}'),
  ('PHY-0890', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field lines', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 190 (§9.2)', 'In a diagram of electric field lines, the field is strongest where:', '["the lines are closest together", "the lines are farthest apart", "the lines are curved", "the lines cross each other"]', 0, 'The number of lines per unit area (perpendicular) is proportional to E, so closely spaced lines mean a strong field.', '{"1": "Widely spaced lines show a weak field.", "2": "Curvature shows direction changes, not strength.", "3": "Field lines never cross."}'),
  ('PHY-0891', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field lines', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 191 (§9.2)', 'For two identical positive point charges, the point midway between them is:', '["the point of strongest field", "a point where field lines cross", "a point where the potential is zero", "a neutral point where the electric field is zero"]', 3, 'At the midpoint the equal and opposite fields of the two like charges cancel, giving a zero-field spot (neutral zone).', '{"0": "The two fields cancel at the midpoint.", "1": "Field lines never cross.", "2": "Both charges give positive potential there; it is the field that is zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0892', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field lines', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 191 (§9.2)', 'A uniform electric field is shown by field lines that are:', '["parallel and equally spaced", "radial, spreading out from a point", "closed loops", "crossing at regular intervals"]', 0, 'In the middle region between parallel plates the lines are parallel and evenly spaced, so E has the same size and direction everywhere.', '{"1": "That is the field of a point charge, which weakens with distance.", "2": "Electric field lines begin on positive and end on negative charges.", "3": "Field lines never cross."}'),
  ('PHY-0893', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric flux', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 192 (§9.3)', 'A flat area of 0.5 m² is placed in a uniform field of 200 N C⁻¹, with its normal (vector area) at 60° to the field. The electric flux through it is:', '["0 N m² C⁻¹", "50 N m² C⁻¹", "86.6 N m² C⁻¹", "100 N m² C⁻¹"]', 1, 'Φ = EA cos θ = 200 × 0.5 × cos 60° = 50 N m² C⁻¹.', '{"0": "Flux is zero only when the area is parallel to the field.", "2": "This uses sin 60° instead of cos 60°.", "3": "That is the maximum flux, when the normal is along E."}'),
  ('PHY-0894', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric flux', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 192 (§9.3)', 'The electric flux through a flat surface in a uniform field is zero when:', '["the surface is perpendicular to the field lines", "the field is very strong", "the surface is parallel to the field lines", "the area is very large"]', 2, 'If the surface is parallel to E, no field lines cross it (θ = 90° between E and the vector area), so Φ = 0.', '{"0": "Then the flux is maximum, EA.", "1": "A strong field gives more flux, unless the surface is parallel.", "3": "Area alone does not make the flux zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0895', 'Electrostatics', 'Gauss''s law', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 193 (§9.4)', 'A closed surface encloses charges of +3 μC and −1 μC; a charge of +5 μC lies outside it. The total electric flux through the surface is (ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C² N⁻¹ m⁻²):', '["1.13 × 10⁵ N m² C⁻¹", "2.26 × 10⁵ N m² C⁻¹", "4.52 × 10⁵ N m² C⁻¹", "7.91 × 10⁵ N m² C⁻¹"]', 1, 'Φ = Q_enclosed/ε₀ = (3 − 1) × 10⁻⁶/8.85 × 10⁻¹² ≈ 2.26 × 10⁵ N m² C⁻¹.', '{"0": "This uses 1 μC.", "2": "This adds the magnitudes (3 + 1) instead of the algebraic sum.", "3": "Charges outside the surface do not contribute to the net flux."}'),
  ('PHY-0896', 'Electrostatics', 'Gauss''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 193 (§9.3)', 'A point charge sits at the centre of a spherical surface. If the radius of the sphere is doubled, the total electric flux through it:', '["doubles", "halves", "falls to one quarter", "stays the same"]', 3, 'Total flux through a closed surface is q/ε₀, independent of its shape or size.', '{"0": "The flux depends only on the charge enclosed (and medium).", "1": "The bigger area exactly makes up for the weaker field.", "2": "E falls as 1/r², but the area rises as r², so the flux is unchanged."}'),
  ('PHY-0897', 'Electrostatics', 'Gauss''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 194 (§9.4)', 'A conducting sphere of radius 10 cm carries a charge of 2 nC. The electric field at a point 30 cm from its centre is (k = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻²):', '["60 N C⁻¹", "200 N C⁻¹", "450 N C⁻¹", "1800 N C⁻¹"]', 1, 'Outside the sphere E = kq/r² as if all the charge were at the centre: (9 × 10⁹ × 2 × 10⁻⁹)/(0.3)² = 18/0.09 = 200 N C⁻¹.', '{"0": "This divides by r, not r².", "2": "This measures 20 cm from the surface; r is measured from the centre.", "3": "This uses the radius of the sphere (0.1 m), not the distance of the point."}'),
  ('PHY-0898', 'Electrostatics', 'Shielding', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 201 (§9.9)', 'People inside a car are usually safe during a lightning storm because:', '["the metal body acts as a Faraday cage, so the charge flows over its outer surface to the ground", "rubber tyres make the car a perfect insulator", "the glass windows absorb the charge", "lightning never strikes moving objects"]', 0, 'The metal chassis behaves like a Faraday cage: the charge stays on and travels over its outer surface, and the field inside stays zero.', '{"1": "The protection comes from the metal shell, not the tyres.", "2": "Glass is an insulator and does not carry the charge.", "3": "Lightning can strike cars and aeroplanes."}'),
  ('PHY-0899', 'Electrostatics', 'Shielding', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 200 (§9.9)', 'Under electrostatic conditions, the electric field just outside the surface of a charged conductor is:', '["perpendicular to the surface", "parallel to the surface", "zero everywhere", "at 45° to the surface"]', 0, 'If E had a component along the surface, free electrons would flow. At equilibrium there is no flow, so E is perpendicular to the surface.', '{"1": "A parallel component would move the free electrons, which does not happen at equilibrium.", "2": "The field is zero inside the conductor, not just outside it.", "3": "Any parallel component would make charges move."}'),
  ('PHY-0900', 'Electrostatics', 'Shielding', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 200 (§9.9)', 'At equilibrium under electrostatic conditions, any excess charge on a conductor:', '["spreads evenly through its volume", "collects at its centre", "resides on its outer surface", "leaks away completely"]', 2, 'Excess charges repel each other and rush to the surface; the field inside the conductor becomes zero.', '{"0": "Mutual repulsion drives the excess charge to the surface.", "1": "Like charges repel and move as far apart as possible.", "3": "An isolated conductor keeps its charge."}'),
  ('PHY-0901', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 195 (§9.5)', '6 J of work is done in moving a charge of +2 C from point A to point B while keeping it in equilibrium. The potential difference V_B − V_A is:', '["0.33 V", "3 V", "8 V", "12 V"]', 1, 'ΔV = W/q = 6 J/2 C = 3 V.', '{"0": "This divides q by W.", "2": "Work and charge are not added.", "3": "This multiplies W by q."}'),
  ('PHY-0902', 'Electrostatics', 'Potential gradient', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 197 (§9.5)', 'Two parallel plates 2 cm apart are connected to a 300 V supply. The electric field between them is:', '["6 V m⁻¹", "150 V m⁻¹", "1500 V m⁻¹", "15000 V m⁻¹"]', 3, 'E = ΔV/Δd = 300 V/0.02 m = 1.5 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹.', '{"0": "This divides d by V.", "1": "This uses d in cm without converting to metres.", "2": "This uses d = 0.2 m instead of 0.02 m."}'),
  ('PHY-0903', 'Electrostatics', 'Potential gradient', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 197 (§9.5)', 'The negative sign in E = −ΔV/Δd shows that the electric field points:', '["in the direction in which the potential increases", "at right angles to the direction of decreasing potential", "towards negative charges only when the potential is zero", "in the direction in which the potential decreases"]', 3, 'Electric intensity equals the negative of the potential gradient: E points along the direction of decreasing potential.', '{"0": "E points from high to low potential.", "1": "E is along the steepest fall in potential.", "2": "The sign has nothing to do with zero potential."}'),
  ('PHY-0904', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 196 (§9.5)', 'Which of these quantities is a scalar?', '["Electric field intensity", "Electrostatic force", "Vector area", "Electric potential"]', 3, 'Potential is work per unit charge; both W and q are scalars, so potential is a scalar.', '{"0": "E has direction; it is a vector.", "1": "Force is a vector.", "2": "As its name says, it is a vector."}'),
  ('PHY-0905', 'Electrostatics', 'Electron volt', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 198 (§9.6)', 'An alpha particle (charge +2e) is accelerated through a potential difference of 500 V. The energy it gains is:', '["1.0 × 10³ J", "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ J", "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ J", "8.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ J"]', 1, 'ΔK.E. = qΔV = 2e × 500 V = 1000 eV = 1000 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ J.', '{"0": "The energy is 1000 eV, not 1000 J.", "2": "This uses charge 4e.", "3": "This uses charge e, not 2e."}'),
  ('PHY-0906', 'Electrostatics', 'Motion of charged particles', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 199 (§9.7)', 'An electron starts from rest and is accelerated through a potential difference of 50 V. Its final speed is about (e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, m = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg):', '["1.8 × 10¹³ m s⁻¹", "3.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹", "4.2 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹", "4.2 × 10³ m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'eV = ½mv², so v = √(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 50/9.1 × 10⁻³¹) = √(1.76 × 10¹³) ≈ 4.2 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "That is v², not v.", "1": "This leaves out the factor 2 in v = √(2eV/m).", "3": "Check the powers of ten."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0907', 'Electrostatics', 'Motion of charged particles', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 199 (§9.7)', 'The acceleration of an electron in a uniform electric field of 2000 N C⁻¹ is about (e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, m = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg):', '["1.8 × 10¹¹ m s⁻²", "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ m s⁻²", "3.5 × 10¹⁴ m s⁻²", "5.7 × 10⁻¹⁵ m s⁻²"]', 2, 'a = eE/m = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2000)/(9.1 × 10⁻³¹) ≈ 3.5 × 10¹⁴ m s⁻².', '{"0": "That is e/m alone, without multiplying by E.", "1": "That is the force in newtons, not the acceleration.", "3": "This divides m by eE."}'),
  ('PHY-0908', 'Electrostatics', 'Path of a charged particle', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 199 (§9.8)', 'A charged particle enters a uniform electric field at right angles to the field lines. Its path inside the field is:', '["a parabola", "a straight line", "a circle", "a spiral"]', 0, 'The velocity component along the plates stays constant while the perpendicular component is uniformly accelerated, like a horizontally thrown projectile: the path is parabolic.', '{"1": "The force across its motion bends the path.", "2": "A constant force in one direction gives projectile-like motion, not a circle.", "3": "The force is constant, not changing direction."}'),
  ('PHY-0909', 'Electrostatics', 'Path of a charged particle', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 200 (§9.8)', 'A proton and an electron enter the same uniform field between parallel plates with the same speed, at right angles to the field. Compared with the proton, the electron is deflected:', '["towards the same plate and by the same amount", "towards the opposite plate by the same amount", "towards the opposite plate and by a much larger amount", "towards the same plate, by a smaller amount"]', 2, 'The forces are equal in size but opposite in direction. Since a = eE/m and the electron''s mass is about 1/1836 of the proton''s, its deflection is far larger.', '{"0": "The charges have opposite signs, and the masses differ greatly.", "1": "The electron''s much smaller mass gives a much larger acceleration.", "3": "Opposite charges deflect in opposite directions."}'),
  ('PHY-0910', 'Current electricity', 'Electric current', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 201 (§9.10)', 'A charge of 90 C passes through a cross-section of a wire in 1 minute. The current is:', '["0.67 A", "1.5 A", "90 A", "5400 A"]', 1, 'I = Q/t = 90 C/60 s = 1.5 A.', '{"0": "This divides t by Q.", "2": "This uses t = 1 s.", "3": "This multiplies Q by t."}'),
  ('PHY-0911', 'Current electricity', 'Electric current', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 201 (§9.10)', 'A bulb lights almost at once when switched on, even though the drift velocity of electrons is very small. This is because:', '["electrons from the switch travel to the bulb at the speed of light", "the bulb stores electrons, ready to release them", "all the free electrons in the circuit start drifting at once, and the disturbance travels along the wire almost instantly", "the drift velocity becomes very large at the moment of switching"]', 2, 'Free electrons are already present throughout the wire; switching on makes them all drift together, so the current is set up very rapidly.', '{"0": "Individual electrons drift slowly.", "1": "A bulb does not store charge.", "3": "The drift velocity stays small."}'),
  ('PHY-0912', 'Current electricity', 'Conventional current', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 202 (§9.10)', 'Conventional current in the external circuit flows:', '["from the negative terminal to the positive terminal", "from lower to higher potential", "in both directions at once", "from the positive terminal to the negative terminal, from higher to lower potential"]', 3, 'Conventional current is the hypothetical flow of positive charge, from higher to lower potential (+ to −) in the external circuit.', '{"0": "That is the direction of electron flow.", "1": "Conventional current flows from higher to lower potential outside the cell.", "2": "In a DC circuit the current has one direction."}'),
  ('PHY-0913', 'Current electricity', 'Current through a conductor', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 203 (§9.11)', 'A wire has cross-sectional area 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² and 8.0 × 10²⁸ free electrons per m³. When it carries 1.28 A, the drift velocity is (e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C):', '["1.0 × 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹", "1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m s⁻¹", "1.0 × 10⁻² m s⁻¹", "1.0 × 10² m s⁻¹"]', 0, 'v = I/nAe = 1.28/(8.0 × 10²⁸ × 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 1.28/12800 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "Check the powers of ten in nAe.", "2": "Check the powers of ten in nAe.", "3": "Drift velocities are very small."}'),
  ('PHY-0914', 'Current electricity', 'Current through a conductor', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 203 (§9.11)', 'A thick and a thin wire of the same metal are joined in series and carry the same current. The thin wire has half the cross-sectional area. The drift velocity of electrons in the thin wire is:', '["twice that in the thick wire", "half that in the thick wire", "equal to that in the thick wire", "four times that in the thick wire"]', 0, 'I = nAvq is the same in both, so v ∝ 1/A. Half the area gives twice the drift velocity.', '{"1": "For the same current, a smaller area needs a faster drift.", "2": "I = nAvq with n and q fixed, so v ∝ 1/A.", "3": "v is inversely proportional to A, not A²."}'),
  ('PHY-0915', 'Current electricity', 'Ohm''s law', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 203 (§9.12)', 'Ohm''s law (V ∝ I) holds for a conductor only if:', '["the current is alternating", "the conductor is a semiconductor", "its physical state, such as its temperature, does not change", "the voltage is less than 1 V"]', 2, 'The current is directly proportional to the potential difference provided there is no change in the physical state (e.g. temperature) of the conductor.', '{"0": "Ohm''s law is stated for steady (direct) current here.", "1": "Semiconductors often do not obey Ohm''s law.", "3": "There is no such voltage limit."}'),
  ('PHY-0916', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 204 (§9.13)', 'A copper wire is 10 m long with a cross-sectional area of 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m². Taking ρ = 1.69 × 10⁻⁸ Ω m, its resistance is:', '["0.0169 Ω", "0.169 Ω", "1.69 Ω", "16.9 Ω"]', 1, 'R = ρL/A = (1.69 × 10⁻⁸ × 10)/(1.0 × 10⁻⁶) = 0.169 Ω.', '{"0": "Check the powers of ten.", "2": "Check the powers of ten.", "3": "Check the powers of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-0917', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 204 (§9.13)', 'Wire A is twice as long as wire B and has twice its diameter; both are of the same metal. The ratio R_A : R_B is:', '["1 : 2", "1 : 1", "1 : 4", "2 : 1"]', 0, 'R = ρL/A, and A ∝ d². So R_A/R_B = (2L/4A)/(L/A) = 1/2.', '{"1": "Doubling the diameter multiplies the area by 4, not 2.", "2": "This ignores A''s greater length.", "3": "This ignores the larger area of A."}'),
  ('PHY-0918', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 204 (§9.13)', 'A metal wire is cut into two equal halves. The resistivity of each half is:', '["half that of the original wire", "twice that of the original wire", "one quarter that of the original wire", "the same as that of the original wire"]', 3, 'Resistance belongs to a particular wire; resistivity is a property of the material, so it stays the same.', '{"0": "The resistance halves; the resistivity does not.", "1": "Resistivity is a property of the material.", "2": "Resistivity does not depend on dimensions."}'),
  ('PHY-0919', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 204 (§9.13)', 'The SI unit of electrical conductivity (σ = 1/ρ) is:', '["Ω⁻¹ m⁻¹", "Ω m", "Ω⁻¹", "Ω m⁻¹"]', 0, 'σ = 1/ρ, so its unit is (Ω m)⁻¹ = Ω⁻¹ m⁻¹ (mho m⁻¹).', '{"1": "That is the unit of resistivity.", "2": "That is the unit of conductance.", "3": "This is not the reciprocal of Ω m."}'),
  ('PHY-0920', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity and temperature', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 204 (§9.13)', 'The resistance of a metal wire rises with temperature mainly because:', '["the lattice atoms vibrate with larger amplitude, so free electrons collide with them more often", "the number of free electrons falls sharply", "the wire gets shorter and thinner", "the electrons move faster and get through more easily"]', 0, 'Hotter atoms vibrate more, increasing their collision cross-section; more frequent collisions mean greater resistance.', '{"1": "In metals the number of free electrons stays almost constant.", "2": "Thermal expansion is a very small effect and would not raise R this way.", "3": "That would lower, not raise, the resistance."}'),
  ('PHY-0921', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity and temperature', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 205 (§9.13)', 'A wire has a resistance of 50 Ω at 0 °C. Its temperature coefficient of resistance is 0.004 K⁻¹. Its resistance at 100 °C is:', '["20 Ω", "50.4 Ω", "70 Ω", "120 Ω"]', 2, 'R_t = R₀(1 + αt) = 50(1 + 0.004 × 100) = 50 × 1.4 = 70 Ω.', '{"0": "That is only the increase in resistance.", "1": "This forgets to multiply by the temperature change.", "3": "This adds the rise in °C to R₀."}'),
  ('PHY-0922', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity and temperature', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 205 (§9.13)', 'A wire has a resistance of 40 Ω at 0 °C and 46 Ω at 50 °C. Its temperature coefficient of resistance is:', '["0.12 K⁻¹", "0.15 K⁻¹", "2.6 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹", "3.0 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹"]', 3, 'α = (R_t − R₀)/(R₀t) = 6/(40 × 50) = 3.0 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹.', '{"0": "This forgets to divide by R₀.", "1": "This forgets to divide by t.", "2": "This divides by R_t (46 Ω) instead of R₀."}'),
  ('PHY-0923', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity and temperature', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 205 (§9.13)', 'Which of these materials has a negative temperature coefficient of resistance?', '["Copper", "Tungsten", "Silicon", "Nichrome"]', 2, 'Semiconductors such as silicon and germanium have resistances that decrease as temperature rises.', '{"0": "Metals have positive coefficients.", "1": "Metals have positive coefficients.", "3": "Nichrome has a small positive coefficient."}'),
  ('PHY-0924', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity and temperature', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 204 (§9.13)', 'According to Table 9.2 of the textbook, the resistance of which material changes least with temperature?', '["Tungsten", "Iron", "Copper", "Constantan"]', 3, 'Constantan has a temperature coefficient of only about 0.00001 K⁻¹, far smaller than other metals.', '{"0": "Its coefficient is about 0.0045 K⁻¹.", "1": "Its coefficient is about 0.005 K⁻¹.", "2": "Its coefficient is about 0.0039 K⁻¹."}'),
  ('PHY-0925', 'Current electricity', 'Electrical power', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 206 (§9.14)', 'A heater is rated 1100 W, 220 V. Its resistance when working is:', '["0.2 Ω", "5 Ω", "44 Ω", "4840 Ω"]', 2, 'R = V²/P = 220²/1100 = 48400/1100 = 44 Ω.', '{"0": "This divides V by P.", "1": "That is the current in amperes, P/V.", "3": "This forgets to divide V² by P."}'),
  ('PHY-0926', 'Current electricity', 'Electrical power', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 206 (§9.14)', 'The current through a fixed resistor is doubled. The power dissipated in it becomes:', '["twice as large", "half as large", "unchanged", "four times as large"]', 3, 'P = I²R; doubling I multiplies P by 2² = 4.', '{"0": "P = I²R, so power varies as the square of the current.", "1": "More current gives more power.", "2": "P depends on I."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0927', 'Current electricity', 'Electromotive force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 207 (§9.15)', 'Electromotive force (emf) is measured in volts because it is:', '["the energy supplied by the source to each unit charge (J C⁻¹)", "a force on charges, measured in newtons", "the charge stored in the cell", "the power of the cell"]', 0, 'Emf E = W/Q, the energy given to unit charge by the source. Its unit is J C⁻¹ = volt.', '{"1": "Despite its name, emf is not a force.", "2": "Stored charge is measured in coulombs or ampere-hours.", "3": "Power is measured in watts."}'),
  ('PHY-0928', 'Current electricity', 'Electromotive force', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 207 (§9.15)', 'A cell of emf 6 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω is connected to a 2.5 Ω resistor. The terminal potential difference is:', '["1 V", "5 V", "6 V", "7 V"]', 1, 'I = E/(R + r) = 6/3 = 2 A, so V = IR = 2 × 2.5 = 5 V (or E − Ir = 6 − 1).', '{"0": "That is the voltage lost inside the cell.", "2": "That is the emf, the terminal voltage on open circuit.", "3": "The terminal voltage cannot exceed the emf when the cell supplies current."}'),
  ('PHY-0929', 'Current electricity', 'Electromotive force', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 208 (§9.15)', 'A battery reads 9.0 V on open circuit. When a 4 Ω resistor is connected, its terminal voltage drops to 8.0 V. Its internal resistance is:', '["0.25 Ω", "0.5 Ω", "2 Ω", "4.5 Ω"]', 1, 'I = V/R = 8/4 = 2 A. E = V + Ir gives 9 = 8 + 2r, so r = 0.5 Ω.', '{"0": "This divides the lost volts by 4 A.", "2": "That is the current in amperes.", "3": "This divides 9 V by 2 A."}'),
  ('PHY-0930', 'Current electricity', 'Electromotive force', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 207 (§9.15)', 'The terminal potential difference of a cell is equal to its emf when:', '["the cell supplies a very large current", "the external resistance is zero", "no current is drawn from the cell", "the internal resistance is very large"]', 2, 'V = E − Ir, so V = E only when I = 0, i.e. on open circuit.', '{"0": "Then Ir is large and V is much less than E.", "1": "Then the terminal voltage is zero.", "3": "A large r makes V smaller when current flows."}'),
  ('PHY-0931', 'Current electricity', 'Kirchhoff''s rules', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 208 (§9.16)', 'At a junction, currents of 3 A and 5 A flow in, and currents of 2 A and I flow out. The current I is:', '["4 A", "6 A", "8 A", "10 A"]', 1, 'By Kirchhoff''s first rule, currents in = currents out: 3 + 5 = 2 + I, so I = 6 A.', '{"0": "Check: total in = 8 A.", "2": "That is the total in; 2 A of it already leaves.", "3": "This adds all three currents."}'),
  ('PHY-0932', 'Current electricity', 'Kirchhoff''s rules', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 209 (§9.16)', 'Kirchhoff''s first (point) rule is a statement of the law of conservation of:', '["energy", "momentum", "charge", "mass"]', 2, 'With no source or sink of charge at a point, the charge flowing in must equal the charge flowing out.', '{"0": "That is the basis of the second rule.", "1": "Momentum is not involved.", "3": "The rule is about charge flow, not mass."}'),
  ('PHY-0933', 'Current electricity', 'Kirchhoff''s rules', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 209 (§9.16)', 'A single loop contains a 12 V cell and a 4 V cell connected so that they oppose each other, with a total resistance of 4 Ω. The current in the loop is:', '["1 A", "2 A", "3 A", "4 A"]', 1, 'By Kirchhoff''s second rule: 12 − 4 − I(4) = 0, so I = 8/4 = 2 A.', '{"0": "This uses only the 4 V cell.", "2": "This uses only the 12 V cell.", "3": "This adds the emfs; they oppose each other."}'),
  ('PHY-0934', 'Current electricity', 'Wheatstone bridge', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 213 (§9.17)', 'In a balanced Wheatstone bridge ABCD, the galvanometer is between B and D and the battery between A and C. AB = 10 Ω, BC = 20 Ω and CD = 30 Ω. The resistance of DA is:', '["6.7 Ω", "15 Ω", "20 Ω", "60 Ω"]', 1, 'At balance B and D are at the same potential, so AB/BC = AD/DC: 10/20 = X/30, X = 15 Ω.', '{"0": "This inverts the ratio.", "2": "Check the ratio: AB/BC must equal AD/DC.", "3": "This uses BC/AB instead of AB/BC."}'),
  ('PHY-0935', 'Current electricity', 'Wheatstone bridge', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 212 (§9.17)', 'When a Wheatstone bridge is balanced:', '["maximum current flows through the galvanometer", "the battery supplies no current", "all four resistances must be equal", "no current flows through the galvanometer, because its two ends are at the same potential"]', 3, 'At balance the two ends of the galvanometer are at the same potential, so it shows no deflection.', '{"0": "Balance means zero galvanometer current.", "1": "Current still flows through the four arms.", "2": "Only the ratios have to be equal."}'),
  ('PHY-0936', 'Current electricity', 'Potentiometer', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 214 (§9.18)', 'A potentiometer wire 100 cm long has a steady 2.0 V across it. A cell balances at 75 cm from the end A. The emf of the cell is:', '["0.5 V", "1.5 V", "2.0 V", "2.7 V"]', 1, 'E_x = E(ℓ/L) = 2.0 × 75/100 = 1.5 V.', '{"0": "That corresponds to the remaining 25 cm.", "2": "That is the full voltage across the wire.", "3": "This divides by 0.75 instead of multiplying."}'),
  ('PHY-0937', 'Current electricity', 'Potentiometer', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 214 (§9.18)', 'Two cells are balanced on a potentiometer wire at 60 cm and 40 cm. If the first cell has an emf of 1.5 V, the second has an emf of:', '["0.6 V", "1.0 V", "1.5 V", "2.25 V"]', 1, 'E₁/E₂ = ℓ₁/ℓ₂, so E₂ = 1.5 × 40/60 = 1.0 V.', '{"0": "This uses ℓ₂/ℓ₁ squared.", "2": "Different balancing lengths mean different emfs.", "3": "This inverts the ratio."}'),
  ('PHY-0938', 'Current electricity', 'Potentiometer', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 214 (§9.18)', 'A potentiometer measures the emf of a cell more accurately than an ordinary voltmeter because:', '["at the balance point it draws no current from the cell", "it has a very small resistance", "it uses a more powerful cell", "it measures current, not voltage"]', 0, 'At balance no current flows from the cell being tested, so its full emf is measured, with no drop across its internal resistance.', '{"1": "Its accuracy comes from the null condition, not from low resistance.", "2": "The driver cell''s size is not the reason.", "3": "It compares potential differences."}'),
  ('PHY-0939', 'Current electricity', 'Galvanometer', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 214 (§9.19)', 'An advantage of using a galvanometer in a null method is that:', '["the galvanometer measures large currents directly", "the circuit needs no battery", "the galvanometer''s own resistance does not affect the measurement", "the galvanometer must have zero resistance"]', 2, 'At the null point no current flows through the galvanometer, so its internal resistance plays no part, giving accurate results.', '{"0": "In a null method it shows zero current.", "1": "A source is still needed.", "3": "Its resistance does not matter at the null point."}'),
  ('PHY-0940', 'Current electricity', 'Thermistors', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 215 (§9.20)', 'Most thermistors are made of semiconductor ceramics. As their temperature rises, their resistance:', '["increases", "stays constant", "first rises and then becomes zero", "decreases"]', 3, 'Most thermistors have a negative temperature coefficient: heating gives the charge carriers more energy, lowering the resistance.', '{"0": "That is true of metals and of PTC thermistors, not of most thermistors.", "1": "Thermistors are designed to be temperature-sensitive.", "2": "Thermistors do not behave like this."}'),
  ('PHY-0941', 'Current electricity', 'Thermistors', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 215 (§9.20)', 'Thermistors are made by heating, under high pressure, mixtures of:', '["oxides of metals such as manganese, nickel and cobalt", "pure copper and silver", "cadmium sulphide deposited on an insulating plate", "carbon fibres in concrete"]', 0, 'Thermistors are semiconductor ceramics made from mixed metallic oxides (manganese, nickel, cobalt, copper, iron), pressed and baked.', '{"1": "These are metals with positive temperature coefficients.", "2": "That is how an LDR is made.", "3": "Those are used to monitor bridges."}'),
  ('PHY-0942', 'Current electricity', 'Thermistors', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 216 (§9.20)', 'Which of these is an application of thermistors given in the textbook?', '["A coolant temperature sensor in a car engine", "An automatic street light that switches on at dusk", "A camera exposure control", "Finding an unknown emf by a null method"]', 0, 'Thermistors convert temperature changes into voltage changes, for example in engine coolant sensors and digital thermometers. They also limit inrush current.', '{"1": "That uses an LDR.", "2": "That uses an LDR.", "3": "That uses a potentiometer."}'),
  ('PHY-0943', 'Current electricity', 'Light dependent resistor', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 216 (§9.21)', 'The resistance of a light dependent resistor (LDR):', '["increases as the light intensity increases", "does not depend on light", "decreases as the light intensity increases", "decreases as its temperature falls"]', 2, 'Light photons free electrons in the semiconductor (e.g. cadmium sulphide), so brighter light gives lower resistance.', '{"0": "More light frees more electrons, lowering the resistance.", "1": "Then it would not be an LDR.", "3": "The LDR is designed to respond to light."}'),
  ('PHY-0944', 'Current electricity', 'Light dependent resistor', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 217 (§9.21)', 'An LDR and a 100 kΩ resistor form a voltage divider. When the LDR is moved from darkness into bright light, the voltage across the LDR:', '["rises, because the LDR''s resistance becomes much higher", "stays the same, because the supply is unchanged", "becomes equal to the supply voltage", "falls, because the LDR''s resistance becomes much lower"]', 3, 'In a divider, the larger resistance takes the larger share of the voltage. In light the LDR''s resistance becomes low, so its voltage falls.', '{"0": "Light lowers the LDR''s resistance.", "1": "The share of the voltage depends on the resistances.", "2": "That happens only if the LDR''s resistance becomes very large."}'),
  ('PHY-0945', 'Current electricity', 'Applications', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 9 Electrostatics and Current Electricity, p. 218 (§9.21)', 'Carbon fibres embedded in a concrete bridge carry a small current. Over time, the measured resistance of the fibre network increases. This suggests that:', '["the bridge is becoming stronger", "the temperature has dropped", "the fibres have become better conductors", "the concrete is cracking and the fibres are separating"]', 3, 'A steady resistance means the bridge is sound; a rising resistance means the fibres are being damaged by cracks.', '{"0": "Stronger concrete would not raise the resistance.", "1": "The book links rising resistance to damage, not cooling.", "2": "Better conductors would lower the resistance."}'),
  ('PHY-0946', 'Electromagnetism', 'Introduction', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 222 (Introduction)', 'The link between electricity and magnetism was first discovered in 1820, when it was found that a current produces a magnetic field. This was discovered by:', '["Michael Faraday", "Heinrich Lenz", "Charles de Coulomb", "Hans Christian Oersted"]', 3, 'In 1820 Oersted found that a current-carrying wire deflects a compass needle, showing that electricity and magnetism are related.', '{"0": "Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction later.", "1": "Lenz gave the rule for the direction of induced current (1834).", "2": "Coulomb measured electrostatic forces."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0947', 'Electromagnetism', 'Introduction', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 222 (Introduction)', 'Lodestone, the natural mineral that led to the invention of the compass, is the iron oxide:', '["haematite, Fe₂O₃", "iron pyrite, FeS₂", "rust, Fe(OH)₃", "magnetite, Fe₃O₄"]', 3, 'Lodestone is magnetite (Fe₃O₄), which attracts iron and aligns with the Earth''s magnetic poles.', '{"0": "Lodestone is magnetite, not haematite.", "1": "Pyrite is a sulphide, not lodestone.", "2": "Rust is not the magnetic mineral lodestone."}'),
  ('PHY-0948', 'Electromagnetism', 'Force on a current-carrying conductor', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 224 (§10.1)', 'A wire 0.5 m long carries 4 A in a uniform field of 0.2 T. The wire makes an angle of 30° with the field. The force on it is:', '["0.1 N", "0.2 N", "0.35 N", "0.4 N"]', 1, 'F = BIL sin θ = 0.2 × 4 × 0.5 × sin 30° = 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2 N.', '{"0": "This halves the force twice.", "2": "This uses cos 30° instead of sin 30°.", "3": "That is the force when the wire is perpendicular to the field."}'),
  ('PHY-0949', 'Electromagnetism', 'Force on a current-carrying conductor', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 224 (§10.1)', 'A straight wire 0.3 m long carries 5 A at right angles to a uniform magnetic field and experiences a force of 0.6 N. The magnetic flux density is:', '["0.09 T", "0.4 T", "0.9 T", "2.5 T"]', 1, 'B = F/(IL) = 0.6/(5 × 0.3) = 0.4 T.', '{"0": "This multiplies F by L and divides by I; the correct form is B = F/IL.", "2": "Check the arithmetic: IL = 1.5 A m.", "3": "This divides IL by F."}'),
  ('PHY-0950', 'Electromagnetism', 'Force on a current-carrying conductor', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 223 (§10.1)', 'In Fleming''s left-hand rule, with the first finger along the magnetic field and the second finger along the current, the thumb gives the direction of the:', '["force on the conductor", "induced current", "magnetic field of the wire", "electric field in the wire"]', 0, 'Fleming''s left-hand rule: First finger = Field, seCond finger = Current, thuMb = force (Motion).', '{"1": "Fleming''s left-hand rule is for the force on a current, not induction.", "2": "That is given by the right-hand grip rule.", "3": "The rule does not give the electric field."}'),
  ('PHY-0951', 'Electromagnetism', 'Force on a current-carrying conductor', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 223 (§10.1)', 'A current-carrying wire lies across a uniform magnetic field. The wire''s own field strengthens the external field on one side of the wire and weakens it on the other. The wire is pushed:', '["towards the side where the resultant field is stronger", "along its own length", "towards the side where the resultant field is weaker", "along the external field lines"]', 2, 'The fields reinforce on one side and oppose on the other, so the conductor moves towards the weaker-field side; the force is perpendicular to both the wire and B.', '{"0": "The conductor moves away from the stronger field region.", "1": "The force is perpendicular to the conductor.", "3": "The force is perpendicular to the field."}'),
  ('PHY-0952', 'Electromagnetism', 'Force on a current-carrying conductor', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 224 (§10.1)', 'The force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field is:', '["parallel to the magnetic field", "parallel to the conductor", "along the bisector of the conductor and the field", "perpendicular to both the conductor and the magnetic field"]', 3, 'F = I(L × B), so the force is at right angles to both L and B.', '{"0": "F = IL × B is perpendicular to B.", "1": "F is perpendicular to L.", "2": "A cross product is perpendicular to both vectors."}'),
  ('PHY-0953', 'Electromagnetism', 'Magnetic flux', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 225 (§10.2)', 'A flat loop of area 0.04 m² is in a uniform field of 0.5 T, with the normal to the loop at 60° to the field. The magnetic flux through the loop is:', '["0 Wb", "0.01 Wb", "0.017 Wb", "0.02 Wb"]', 1, 'Φ = BA cos θ = 0.5 × 0.04 × cos 60° = 0.02 × 0.5 = 0.01 Wb.', '{"0": "The flux is zero only when the field is parallel to the plane of the loop.", "2": "This uses sin 60° instead of cos 60°.", "3": "That is the maximum flux, with the normal along B."}'),
  ('PHY-0954', 'Electromagnetism', 'Magnetic flux', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 225 (§10.2)', 'The magnetic flux through a flat coil in a uniform field is zero when:', '["the field is perpendicular to the plane of the coil", "the field is very weak but not zero", "the coil has many turns", "the field is parallel to the plane of the coil"]', 3, 'When B lies in the plane, the angle between B and the normal (vector area) is 90°, so Φ = BA cos 90° = 0.', '{"0": "Then the flux is maximum.", "1": "A weak field still gives some flux unless it is parallel to the plane.", "2": "The number of turns does not make the flux zero."}'),
  ('PHY-0955', 'Electromagnetism', 'Magnetic flux', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 226 (§10.2)', 'The weber, the SI unit of magnetic flux, is equal to:', '["N m A⁻¹", "N A⁻¹ m⁻¹", "N A m⁻¹", "N m⁻²"]', 0, 'Φ = BA, so 1 Wb = 1 T × 1 m² = (N A⁻¹ m⁻¹)(m²) = N m A⁻¹.', '{"1": "That is the tesla, the unit of flux density.", "2": "The ampere is in the denominator.", "3": "That is the pascal."}'),
  ('PHY-0956', 'Electromagnetism', 'Magnetic flux', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 225 (§10.2)', 'Magnetic flux is a scalar quantity because it is:', '["the cross product of B and A", "always positive", "the dot (scalar) product of B and the vector area A", "measured in tesla"]', 2, 'Φ = B · A = BA cos θ. A dot product of two vectors is a scalar.', '{"0": "A cross product gives a vector.", "1": "Being positive does not make a quantity scalar.", "3": "Flux is measured in webers; the tesla is the unit of B."}'),
  ('PHY-0957', 'Electromagnetism', 'Magnetic flux linkage', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 227 (§10.3)', 'A coil of 200 turns has a flux of 3 mWb through each turn. The magnetic flux linkage is:', '["0.015 Wb-turns", "0.6 Wb-turns", "6.0 Wb-turns", "66.7 Wb-turns"]', 1, 'Flux linkage = NΦ = 200 × 3 × 10⁻³ Wb = 0.6 Wb-turns.', '{"0": "This divides the flux by the turns.", "2": "Check the conversion: 3 mWb = 3 × 10⁻³ Wb.", "3": "This divides the turns by the flux."}'),
  ('PHY-0958', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 228 (§10.4)', 'A proton moves at 2 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ at right angles to a magnetic field of 0.5 T. The magnetic force on it is (e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C):', '["1.6 × 10⁻¹³ N", "3.2 × 10⁻¹³ N", "6.4 × 10⁻¹³ N", "8.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ N"]', 0, 'F = qvB sin 90° = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2 × 10⁶ × 0.5 = 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ N.', '{"1": "This leaves out B = 0.5 T.", "2": "This divides by B instead of multiplying.", "3": "This halves the force; sin 90° = 1."}'),
  ('PHY-0959', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 228 (§10.4)', 'A charged particle moving at 30° to a uniform magnetic field experiences a force F. If it moved with the same speed at 90° to the field, the force would be:', '["F/2", "F", "2F", "√3F"]', 2, 'F = qvB sin θ. Since sin 90°/sin 30° = 1/0.5 = 2, the force doubles.', '{"0": "The force is larger at 90°, since sin 90° > sin 30°.", "1": "The force depends on the angle.", "3": "That uses cos 30°/sin 30°; the ratio is sin 90°/sin 30° = 2."}'),
  ('PHY-0960', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 229 (§10.4)', 'An electron moves at 2.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ at right angles to a uniform field of 0.01 T. The radius of its circular path is about (m = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg, e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C):', '["1.1 × 10⁻³ m", "1.1 × 10⁻⁴ m", "1.1 × 10⁻² m", "1.1 m"]', 0, 'r = mv/(eB) = (9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 2.0 × 10⁶)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.01) = 1.82 × 10⁻²⁴/1.6 × 10⁻²¹ ≈ 1.1 × 10⁻³ m.', '{"1": "Check the powers of ten.", "2": "Check the powers of ten.", "3": "This is far too large; check B = 10⁻² T."}'),
  ('PHY-0961', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 229 (§10.4)', 'A charged particle moves in a circle in a uniform magnetic field. Its kinetic energy:', '["increases steadily, because the field keeps pushing it", "decreases steadily, because the field slows it down", "becomes zero after one revolution", "stays constant, because the magnetic force is always perpendicular to its velocity"]', 3, 'F = q(v × B) is perpendicular to v, so it does no work: the speed and K.E. stay the same while the direction changes.', '{"0": "A force perpendicular to motion does no work.", "1": "The magnetic force changes direction, not speed.", "2": "The particle keeps moving at the same speed."}'),
  ('PHY-0962', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 228 (§10.4)', 'A proton and an electron enter the same magnetic field with the same velocity at right angles to it. The magnetic forces on them:', '["are in opposite directions", "are in the same direction", "are both zero", "act along their velocities"]', 0, 'F = q(v × B); reversing the sign of q reverses the force, so they curve in opposite directions.', '{"1": "The sign of the charge reverses the force.", "2": "Charges moving across a field feel a force.", "3": "The force is perpendicular to the velocity."}'),
  ('PHY-0963', 'Electromagnetism', 'Velocity selector', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 230 (§10.5)', 'In a velocity selector the electric field is 6000 V m⁻¹ and the magnetic field is 0.3 T. Particles pass through undeflected if their speed is:', '["1.8 × 10³ m s⁻¹", "2.0 × 10⁴ m s⁻¹", "5.0 × 10⁻⁵ m s⁻¹", "6.0 × 10³ m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'For no deflection qE = qvB, so v = E/B = 6000/0.3 = 2.0 × 10⁴ m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "This multiplies E by B.", "2": "This divides B by E.", "3": "This ignores B."}'),
  ('PHY-0964', 'Electromagnetism', 'Velocity selector', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 229 (§10.5)', 'In a velocity selector, a positive particle moving faster than E/B will be:', '["deflected in the direction of the electric force", "undeflected", "deflected in the direction of the magnetic force", "brought to rest"]', 2, 'The magnetic force qvB grows with speed while the electric force qE does not; for v > E/B the magnetic force wins.', '{"0": "For v > E/B, qvB is larger than qE.", "1": "Only particles with v = E/B pass straight through.", "3": "Neither force slows it down to rest."}'),
  ('PHY-0965', 'Electromagnetism', 'Velocity selector', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 230 (§10.5)', 'The speed selected by a velocity selector:', '["depends on the particle''s charge", "depends on the particle''s mass", "depends on the length of the tube only", "depends only on E and B, not on the particle''s charge or mass"]', 3, 'qE = qvB gives v = E/B: charge and mass cancel, so any particle with this speed passes.', '{"0": "q cancels from qE = qvB.", "1": "Mass does not appear in v = E/B.", "2": "v = E/B; the length is not involved."}'),
  ('PHY-0966', 'Electromagnetism', 'Velocity selector', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 230 (§10.5)', 'A particle passes straight through a velocity selector with E = 4000 V m⁻¹ and B = 0.2 T. When E is switched off, it moves in a circle of radius 5.0 cm. Its charge-to-mass ratio is:', '["1.0 × 10⁸ C kg⁻¹", "2.0 × 10⁶ C kg⁻¹", "4.0 × 10⁵ C kg⁻¹", "8.0 × 10⁶ C kg⁻¹"]', 1, 'v = E/B and r = mv/(qB), so q/m = E/(B²r) = 4000/(0.04 × 0.05) = 2.0 × 10⁶ C kg⁻¹.', '{"0": "Check the arithmetic.", "2": "This uses E/(Br); B must be squared.", "3": "This uses E/(Br²)."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0967', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Motional emf', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 232 (§10.6)', 'A rod 0.4 m long moves at 5 m s⁻¹ at right angles to a uniform field of 0.3 T. The emf induced across its ends is:', '["0.024 V", "0.6 V", "1.5 V", "6.0 V"]', 1, 'ε = vBL = 5 × 0.3 × 0.4 = 0.6 V.', '{"0": "This multiplies B and L but divides by v.", "2": "This leaves out L.", "3": "Check the arithmetic: 5 × 0.3 × 0.4 = 0.6."}'),
  ('PHY-0968', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Motional emf', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 232 (§10.6)', 'A rod moves through a uniform magnetic field at an angle of 30° to the field, instead of at 90°, with the same speed. The motional emf is now:', '["half as large", "twice as large", "unchanged", "zero"]', 0, 'ε = vBL sin θ; sin 30° = 0.5, so the emf is halved.', '{"1": "sin 30° < sin 90°, so the emf is smaller.", "2": "The emf depends on the angle through sin θ.", "3": "The emf is zero only when the rod moves along the field."}'),
  ('PHY-0969', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Faraday''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 233 (§10.6)', 'The flux through each turn of a 100-turn coil falls from 0.020 Wb to 0.005 Wb in 0.1 s. The average induced emf is:', '["0.15 V", "1.5 V", "15 V", "25 V"]', 2, 'ε = NΔΦ/Δt = 100 × (0.020 − 0.005)/0.1 = 100 × 0.15 = 15 V.', '{"0": "This leaves out the 100 turns.", "1": "Check the arithmetic: 0.015/0.1 = 0.15 per turn.", "3": "This uses the final flux, not the change."}'),
  ('PHY-0970', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Faraday''s law', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 233 (§10.6)', 'A coil of 50 turns and area 0.02 m² is perpendicular to a magnetic field that rises steadily from 0 to 0.6 T in 0.2 s. The induced emf is:', '["0.06 V", "0.3 V", "3 V", "6 V"]', 2, 'ε = NAΔB/Δt = 50 × 0.02 × 0.6/0.2 = 3 V.', '{"0": "This leaves out the 50 turns.", "1": "Check the arithmetic: ΔΦ per turn = 0.012 Wb.", "3": "This doubles the rate of change."}'),
  ('PHY-0971', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Faraday''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 236 (§10.7)', 'A single loop is perpendicular to a field of 0.4 T, and its area shrinks steadily at 0.5 m² s⁻¹. The induced emf is:', '["0.08 V", "0.2 V", "0.8 V", "1.25 V"]', 1, 'ε = B(ΔA/Δt) = 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2 V.', '{"0": "Check the arithmetic: 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2.", "2": "This divides B by the rate.", "3": "This divides the rate by B."}'),
  ('PHY-0972', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Faraday''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 233 (§10.6)', 'The number of turns of a coil is doubled, and the flux through each turn now changes by the same amount in half the time. The induced emf becomes:', '["four times as large", "twice as large", "unchanged", "half as large"]', 0, 'ε = NΔΦ/Δt: doubling N doubles ε, and halving Δt doubles it again, so ε × 4.', '{"1": "Both changes double the emf, giving ×4.", "2": "Both changes increase the emf.", "3": "Both changes increase the emf."}'),
  ('PHY-0973', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Faraday''s law', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 233 (§10.6)', 'In Faraday''s law, ε = −N ΔΦ/Δt, the minus sign shows that:', '["the induced emf is always negative", "the flux always decreases", "the induced emf opposes the change in flux that produces it", "energy is created in the coil"]', 2, 'The minus sign expresses Lenz''s law: the induced emf acts to oppose the change in flux.', '{"0": "The sign gives direction, relative to the change.", "1": "The law applies to both increasing and decreasing flux.", "3": "The minus sign reflects energy conservation, not creation."}'),
  ('PHY-0974', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Lenz''s law', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 234 (§10.7)', 'The north pole of a bar magnet is pushed towards one end of a coil. The face of the coil nearest the magnet becomes:', '["a south pole, which attracts the magnet", "neither pole, because no current flows", "a north pole, which attracts the magnet", "a north pole, which repels the approaching magnet"]', 3, 'By Lenz''s law the induced current opposes the push, so the near face becomes a north pole and repels the magnet.', '{"0": "That would help the motion, against Lenz''s law.", "1": "A changing flux induces a current.", "2": "Like poles repel."}'),
  ('PHY-0975', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Lenz''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 234 (§10.7)', 'The north pole of a bar magnet is pulled away from the end of a coil. The induced current makes the near face of the coil:', '["a north pole, which repels the magnet", "a north pole, which attracts the magnet", "no pole, because the flux is decreasing", "a south pole, which attracts the magnet and opposes its removal"]', 3, 'Lenz''s law: the induced current opposes the "pull", so the near face becomes a south pole attracting the receding north pole.', '{"0": "That would help the pull, against Lenz''s law.", "1": "Like poles repel.", "2": "A decreasing flux also induces a current."}'),
  ('PHY-0976', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Lenz''s law', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 234 (§10.7)', 'Lenz''s law is a consequence of the law of conservation of:', '["energy", "charge", "momentum", "mass"]', 0, 'If the induced current helped the change, kinetic energy would grow from nothing; opposing the change keeps energy conserved.', '{"1": "That underlies Kirchhoff''s first rule.", "2": "Lenz''s law is about energy, not momentum.", "3": "Mass does not come into it."}'),
  ('PHY-0977', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Lenz''s law', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 235 (§10.7)', 'A conducting rod is pulled at constant speed along rails in a magnetic field, and an induced current flows. A steady external force is needed because:', '["friction is the only force on the rod", "the induced current speeds the rod up", "the induced current in the rod produces a magnetic force that opposes its motion", "the rod gains mass as it moves"]', 2, 'The current-carrying rod feels F = ILB opposite to v. The external force does work against it, supplying the electrical energy.', '{"0": "The magnetic force on the induced current also opposes the motion.", "1": "That would break energy conservation.", "3": "The mass does not change."}'),
  ('PHY-0978', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Factors affecting emf', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 236 (§10.8)', 'Which change would NOT increase the emf induced in a coil?', '["Increasing the number of turns", "Changing the flux more quickly", "Using a coil of wire with a higher resistance", "Moving the magnet faster relative to the coil"]', 2, 'The induced emf depends on the rate of change of flux, the number of turns and the relative speed, not on the resistance of the coil.', '{"0": "The emf is proportional to N.", "1": "The emf is proportional to the rate of change of flux.", "3": "Faster motion changes the flux more quickly."}'),
  ('PHY-0979', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 232 (§10.6)', 'On an induction cooker, the water in a metal pot boils while the glass top stays cool enough to touch. This is because:', '["the glass top conducts heat to the pot very quickly", "microwaves from the coil heat the water directly", "the pot is heated by a flame hidden under the glass", "a changing magnetic flux from the coil below induces currents in the metal pot, which heat it"]', 3, 'An alternating current in the coil makes a changing flux that links the pot; currents induced in the pot heat it.', '{"0": "The glass stays cool, so it is not the heat source.", "1": "An induction cooker uses changing magnetic flux, not microwaves.", "2": "There is no flame."}'),
  ('PHY-0980', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Ferrofluids', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 236 (§10.9)', 'A ferrofluid is:', '["a colloidal suspension of magnetic nanoparticles in a carrier liquid", "a liquid metal such as mercury", "a solution of iron salt in water", "a permanent magnet that melts when heated"]', 0, 'A ferrofluid is a colloid of iron oxide particles about 10 nm across, in oil or water.', '{"1": "Mercury is not magnetic and is not a suspension.", "2": "The particles are suspended, not dissolved.", "3": "A ferrofluid is a liquid with suspended particles."}'),
  ('PHY-0981', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Ferrofluids', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 236 (§10.9)', 'The magnetic particles in a ferrofluid are coated with a surfactant in order to:', '["make the particles permanently magnetic", "stop the particles from clumping together", "increase the surface tension of the fluid", "dissolve the particles in the liquid"]', 1, 'The surfactant coating keeps the nanoparticles apart, so they stay evenly dispersed in the fluid.', '{"0": "The particles are only temporarily magnetised.", "2": "A surfactant reduces surface tension.", "3": "The particles stay suspended, not dissolved."}'),
  ('PHY-0982', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Ferrofluids', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 237 (§10.9)', 'The spikes seen on a ferrofluid near a strong magnet result from competition between:', '["electric forces and friction only", "buoyancy and viscosity only", "magnetic forces, surface tension and gravity", "nuclear forces and gravity"]', 2, 'Magnetic forces pull the fluid up into chains along the field lines, while gravity and surface tension pull it back; spikes form where magnetic forces win.', '{"0": "The book names magnetic forces, surface tension and gravity.", "1": "These are not the forces the book names.", "3": "Nuclear forces play no part."}'),
  ('PHY-0983', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Ferrofluids', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 238 (§10.9)', 'In a loudspeaker, ferrofluid held around the voice coil is used to:', '["make the coil a permanent magnet", "increase the electrical resistance of the coil", "store electrical energy", "cool the coil and damp unwanted vibrations"]', 3, 'The magnetic field holds the ferrofluid round the voice coil, where it absorbs and dissipates heat and damps vibrations.', '{"0": "Ferrofluid particles are only temporarily magnetised.", "1": "That is not its purpose.", "2": "Ferrofluid is not a storage device."}'),
  ('PHY-0984', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Ferrofluids', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 238 (§10.9)', 'A medical use of ferrofluids given in the textbook is:', '["targeted drug delivery guided by external magnets", "measuring blood pressure", "recording the heart''s electrical activity", "killing bacteria by heat sterilisation"]', 0, 'Magnets can steer ferrofluid particles carrying drugs to a tumour; ferrofluids can also serve as MRI contrast agents.', '{"1": "That uses a sphygmomanometer.", "2": "That is an ECG.", "3": "That is not a ferrofluid application."}'),
  ('PHY-0985', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Seismometer', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 238 (§10.10)', 'In an electromagnetic seismometer, an emf is induced during an earthquake because:', '["the earthquake produces electric charges in the rock", "the suspended magnet tends to stay still by inertia while the coil moves with the ground, so the flux through the coil changes", "the spring generates a current when stretched", "the coil heats up and produces a voltage"]', 1, 'Ground motion gives relative motion between magnet and coil; the changing flux induces an emf (Faraday''s law).', '{"0": "The emf comes from relative motion of magnet and coil.", "2": "The spring only supports the mass.", "3": "The effect is electromagnetic induction, not heating."}'),
  ('PHY-0986', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Seismometer', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 238 (§10.10)', 'In an electromagnetic seismometer, the induced emf is proportional to:', '["the mass of the suspended weight only", "the depth at which the seismometer is buried", "the speed of the relative motion between the magnet and the coil", "the total distance the ground moves, however slowly"]', 2, 'By Faraday''s law the emf depends on the rate of change of flux, i.e. on the velocity of the relative motion, as the book states on p. 238.', '{"0": "The mass sets the inertia; the emf depends on the rate of flux change.", "1": "Depth does not set the emf.", "3": "A slow movement gives a small rate of flux change and a small emf."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-0987', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 232 (§10.6)', 'Wireless charging of a mobile phone works on the principle of:', '["electrostatic induction", "the photoelectric effect", "thermionic emission", "electromagnetic induction"]', 3, 'A changing current in the charging pad''s coil makes a changing flux that induces an emf in the phone''s coil.', '{"0": "That separates charges; it does not transfer power from a coil.", "1": "No light is involved.", "2": "No heating of a filament is involved."}'),
  ('PHY-0988', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 10 Electromagnetism, p. 231 (§10.6)', 'An emf is induced in a closed coil when:', '["the magnetic flux through it changes", "a strong magnet rests motionless inside it", "a steady current flows in a nearby wire with nothing moving", "the coil is heated"]', 0, 'Faraday''s law: an emf is induced only while the flux linking the coil changes.', '{"1": "Constant flux induces no emf.", "2": "Steady flux induces no emf.", "3": "Heating does not change the flux."}'),
  ('PHY-0989', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Special relativity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 244 (Introduction)', 'Einstein put forward the special theory of relativity in:', '["1687", "1905", "1915", "1919"]', 1, 'Einstein introduced the special theory of relativity in 1905.', '{"0": "That is when Newton''s Principia appeared.", "2": "That is the general theory of relativity.", "3": "That is the eclipse that confirmed the bending of starlight."}'),
  ('PHY-0990', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Relative motion', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 245 (§11.1)', 'A passenger drops a ball inside a train moving with uniform velocity. The ball is seen to:', '["fall straight down by the passenger, but follow a projectile (parabolic) path by an observer on the ground", "fall straight down for both the passenger and the ground observer", "fall behind the point of release, as seen by the passenger", "follow a parabola for the passenger and fall straight down for the ground observer"]', 0, 'The ball shares the train''s velocity, so the passenger sees it fall vertically, while the ground observer sees a horizontal velocity plus free fall: a projectile path.', '{"1": "The ground observer sees the ball''s horizontal velocity too.", "2": "Ball and train share the same horizontal velocity.", "3": "This is the wrong way round."}'),
  ('PHY-0991', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Frames of reference', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 246 (§11.2)', 'An inertial frame of reference is one in which:', '["the law of inertia holds", "objects always remain at rest", "the frame is accelerating", "there is no gravity"]', 0, 'In an inertial frame a body stays at rest or in uniform motion unless an unbalanced force acts: the law of inertia is valid.', '{"1": "Objects can move with uniform velocity in an inertial frame.", "2": "An accelerating frame is non-inertial.", "3": "An inertial frame is defined by the law of inertia, not by the absence of gravity."}'),
  ('PHY-0992', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Frames of reference', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 246 (§11.2)', 'Which of these is a non-inertial frame of reference?', '["A car moving at constant velocity on a straight road", "A train moving at constant velocity", "A laboratory at rest on the ground (ignoring Earth''s small acceleration)", "A car that is braking suddenly"]', 3, 'An accelerating (or decelerating) frame is non-inertial: objects inside start moving without any applied force.', '{"0": "Uniform velocity relative to an inertial frame gives an inertial frame.", "1": "It is inertial, since it is not accelerating.", "2": "This is treated as inertial."}'),
  ('PHY-0993', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Frames of reference', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 246 (§11.2)', 'Strictly, the Earth is a non-inertial frame, yet it is usually treated as inertial because:', '["it does not move at all", "it moves with exactly constant velocity", "the special theory of relativity applies only on Earth", "its acceleration due to rotation and revolution is very small"]', 3, 'The Earth rotates and revolves (so it accelerates), but the acceleration is so small that it can be treated as inertial.', '{"0": "The Earth rotates and revolves.", "1": "Its rotation and revolution involve acceleration.", "2": "Relativity applies everywhere."}'),
  ('PHY-0994', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Frames of reference', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 246 (§11.2)', 'A frame of reference moves with uniform velocity relative to an inertial frame. This frame is:', '["also inertial", "non-inertial, because it is moving", "inertial only if its speed is small", "non-inertial, unless it is at rest"]', 0, 'Any frame moving with uniform velocity relative to an inertial frame is itself inertial.', '{"1": "Uniform motion does not make a frame non-inertial.", "2": "Any uniform velocity keeps it inertial.", "3": "Being at rest is not required."}'),
  ('PHY-0995', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Special relativity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 246 (§11.3)', 'The special theory of relativity deals with:', '["inertial (non-accelerating) frames of reference", "accelerating frames of reference", "only objects at rest", "only sound waves"]', 0, 'Special relativity covers inertial frames; general relativity deals with frames accelerating relative to one another.', '{"1": "Those are the subject of the general theory.", "2": "It deals with relative motion.", "3": "It deals with all physical laws."}'),
  ('PHY-0996', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Postulates of relativity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 246 (§11.3)', 'Which of these is a postulate of the special theory of relativity?', '["Time passes at the same rate for all observers.", "The mass of a body is the same at all speeds.", "The speed of light depends on the speed of its source.", "The speed of light in free space is the same for all observers, whatever the motion of the source or observer."]', 3, 'The two postulates: (1) the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames; (2) c is the same for all observers.', '{"0": "Relativity shows time is not absolute.", "1": "Relativity predicts mass increase.", "2": "That contradicts the second postulate."}'),
  ('PHY-0997', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Postulates of relativity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 246 (§11.3)', 'A consequence of the first postulate (the principle of relativity) is that:', '["the speed of light changes from one frame to another", "some inertial frames are absolutely at rest", "no experiment can detect absolute uniform motion", "the laws of physics differ in moving frames"]', 2, 'If the laws are the same in all inertial frames, no observer can tell which frame is "really" moving.', '{"0": "That contradicts the second postulate.", "1": "No frame can be shown to be absolutely at rest.", "3": "The postulate says they are the same."}'),
  ('PHY-0998', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Relativity of simultaneity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 247 (§11.3)', 'A lamp in the middle of a fast-moving train is switched on, and the light opens the front and back doors. To an observer outside on the ground:', '["the front door opens before the back door", "both doors open at the same time", "neither door opens", "the back door opens before the front door"]', 3, 'The back door moves towards the light and the front door away from it, so to the outside observer the back door opens first. Simultaneity depends on the frame.', '{"0": "The back door moves towards the light, so the light reaches it first.", "1": "That is what the traveller inside sees.", "2": "The light still reaches both doors."}'),
  ('PHY-0999', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Time dilation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 247 (§11.3)', 'The proper time between two events is the time measured by an observer who is:', '["at rest relative to the frame in which the events occur", "moving at the speed of light", "moving fastest relative to the events", "at the centre of the Earth"]', 0, 'Proper time t₀ is measured in the frame of the events; any observer moving relative to it measures a longer time t.', '{"1": "No material observer can move at c.", "2": "A moving observer measures a dilated (longer) time.", "3": "Proper time is not defined by location."}'),
  ('PHY-1000', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Time dilation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 247 (§11.3)', 'A process lasts 4.0 s in its own frame. For an observer moving at 0.6c relative to it, the process lasts:', '["2.4 s", "3.2 s", "5.0 s", "6.7 s"]', 2, 't = t₀/√(1 − v²/c²) = 4.0/√(1 − 0.36) = 4.0/0.8 = 5.0 s.', '{"0": "This multiplies by v/c.", "1": "This multiplies by √(1 − v²/c²); time is dilated, not contracted.", "3": "This divides by v/c instead of √(1 − v²/c²)."}'),
  ('PHY-1001', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Time dilation', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 248 (§11.3)', 'A clock on a spaceship ticks off 6.0 s. The ship moves at 0.8c relative to an observer on Earth. The time measured by the Earth observer is:', '["3.6 s", "4.8 s", "7.5 s", "10 s"]', 3, '√(1 − 0.64) = 0.6, so t = 6.0/0.6 = 10 s.', '{"0": "This multiplies by √(1 − v²/c²) = 0.6.", "1": "This multiplies by v/c.", "2": "This divides by v/c instead of by 0.6."}'),
  ('PHY-1002', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Time dilation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 248 (§11.3)', 'According to special relativity, time dilation at very high speeds applies to:', '["only mechanical clocks", "all timing processes: physical, chemical and biological, including ageing", "only light clocks", "nothing real, because it is only a mathematical trick"]', 1, 'The book stresses that the result applies to all processes; even ageing is slowed at relativistic speeds.', '{"0": "It applies to every timing process.", "2": "It applies to every timing process.", "3": "Time dilation has been measured experimentally."}'),
  ('PHY-1003', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Length contraction', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 248 (§11.3)', 'A rocket is 50 m long when at rest. Its length, as measured by an observer it passes at 0.6c, is:', '["30 m", "40 m", "50 m", "62.5 m"]', 1, 'ℓ = ℓ₀√(1 − v²/c²) = 50 × 0.8 = 40 m.', '{"0": "This multiplies by v/c.", "2": "A moving object is contracted along its motion.", "3": "This divides by 0.8; lengths contract, not expand."}'),
  ('PHY-1004', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Length contraction', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 248 (§11.3)', 'A spaceship moves past an observer at a very high speed. Its mast stands at right angles to the direction of motion. Compared with its rest length, the mast as measured by the observer is:', '["shorter", "longer", "the same length", "zero"]', 2, 'Length contraction occurs only along the direction of motion; perpendicular lengths are unchanged.', '{"0": "Contraction happens only along the direction of motion.", "1": "Lengths do not expand with motion.", "3": "The mast does not vanish."}'),
  ('PHY-1005', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Length contraction', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 248 (§11.3)', 'The proper length of an object is its length measured by an observer who is:', '["moving relative to the object at high speed", "moving at the speed of light", "far away from the object", "at rest relative to the object"]', 3, 'Proper length ℓ₀ is measured by an observer at rest relative to the object; moving observers measure ℓ < ℓ₀.', '{"0": "That observer measures a contracted length.", "1": "No material observer can do this.", "2": "Distance does not define proper length."}'),
  ('PHY-1006', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Mass variation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 248 (§11.3)', 'An object has a rest mass of 2.0 kg. When it moves at 0.6c, its mass is:', '["1.6 kg", "2.0 kg", "2.5 kg", "3.3 kg"]', 2, 'm = m₀/√(1 − v²/c²) = 2.0/0.8 = 2.5 kg.', '{"0": "Mass increases with speed; it does not decrease.", "1": "Relativity predicts an increase at high speed.", "3": "This divides by 0.6 instead of 0.8."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1007', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Mass variation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 248 (§11.3)', 'A material object cannot be accelerated to the speed of light because, as v approaches c:', '["its mass tends to infinity, so an infinite force would be needed", "its mass tends to zero", "it turns into light", "its length becomes infinite"]', 0, 'm = m₀/√(1 − v²/c²) → ∞ as v → c. An infinite mass would need an infinite force.', '{"1": "The mass increases without limit.", "2": "Accelerating does not convert it to light.", "3": "Its length contracts."}'),
  ('PHY-1008', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Mass variation', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 248 (§11.3)', 'At what speed is the mass of a particle double its rest mass?', '["0.5c", "0.707c", "0.866c", "0.943c"]', 2, 'm = 2m₀ means √(1 − v²/c²) = 1/2, so v²/c² = 3/4 and v = 0.866c.', '{"0": "At 0.5c, γ is only about 1.15.", "1": "At 0.707c, γ is √2 ≈ 1.41.", "3": "That speed triples the mass."}'),
  ('PHY-1009', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Relativity and everyday life', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 249 (§11.3)', 'Newton''s laws work well for everyday motion because:', '["relativity applies only to light", "everyday speeds are tiny compared with the speed of light", "everyday objects have no mass", "relativistic effects are exactly zero below the speed of sound"]', 1, 'Even Earth''s orbital speed (30 km s⁻¹) is about 10⁻⁴ of c (300,000 km s⁻¹), so relativistic effects are negligible in daily life.', '{"0": "Relativity applies to all motion.", "2": "They do have mass.", "3": "They are not zero, just far too small to notice."}'),
  ('PHY-1010', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Mass–energy equivalence', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 249 (§11.4)', 'The rest-mass energy of 1.0 g of matter is (c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹):', '["3 × 10⁵ J", "9 × 10¹³ J", "9 × 10¹⁰ J", "9 × 10¹⁶ J"]', 1, 'E₀ = m₀c² = 10⁻³ × (3 × 10⁸)² = 10⁻³ × 9 × 10¹⁶ = 9 × 10¹³ J.', '{"0": "This uses c instead of c².", "2": "Check the conversion: 1.0 g = 10⁻³ kg.", "3": "That is for 1 kg."}'),
  ('PHY-1011', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Mass–energy equivalence', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 249 (§11.4)', 'We do not notice changes in mass in everyday energy changes (such as heating water) because:', '["mass and energy are not related in everyday life", "energy changes only happen in nuclear reactions", "c² is so large that ordinary energy changes correspond to tiny changes in mass", "the mass change is exactly zero"]', 2, 'Δm = ΔE/c², and c² ≈ 9 × 10¹⁶ m² s⁻², so everyday ΔE gives unmeasurably small Δm; only nuclear reactions show it clearly.', '{"0": "E = mc² holds everywhere.", "1": "Energy changes are everyday events.", "3": "It is not zero, just immeasurably small."}'),
  ('PHY-1012', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Space-time', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 249 (§11.5)', 'In the theory of relativity, space-time is a model in which:', '["space and time are completely separate and absolute", "time stops for all moving objects", "there are four dimensions of space and no time", "time is treated as a fourth dimension along with the three dimensions of space"]', 3, 'Space-time unifies the three space dimensions and time into a single continuum.', '{"0": "Relativity unites them.", "1": "Time is dilated, not stopped.", "2": "Time is the fourth dimension."}'),
  ('PHY-1013', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Space-time', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 250 (§11.5)', 'In Einstein''s description of gravity, a planet orbits the Sun because:', '["it follows a geodesic in space-time curved by the Sun''s mass", "the Sun pulls it with an instantaneous force at a distance", "space-time is flat near massive bodies", "the planet carries electric charge"]', 0, 'Massive bodies curve space-time; bodies and light move along geodesics in this curved space-time, which we see as gravity.', '{"1": "In Einstein''s picture the motion comes from the curvature of space-time.", "2": "Massive bodies curve space-time.", "3": "Gravity is not electrical."}'),
  ('PHY-1014', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Space-time', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 250 (§11.5)', 'The bending of starlight by the Sun''s gravity, which supported Einstein''s theory over Newton''s, was measured during a solar eclipse in:', '["1687", "1905", "1919", "2015"]', 2, 'Measurements during the 1919 solar eclipse matched Einstein''s prediction of the deflection of starlight.', '{"0": "That is Newton''s Principia.", "1": "That is when special relativity appeared.", "3": "That is when gravitational waves were first detected."}'),
  ('PHY-1015', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Time dilation', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 251 (§11.5)', 'A spaceship travels to a star at half the speed of light. Earth observers say the trip takes 8.0 years. The time recorded on the ship''s clocks is about:', '["4.0 years", "6.9 years", "8.0 years", "9.2 years"]', 1, 'The ship''s time is the proper time: t₀ = t√(1 − v²/c²) = 8.0 × √0.75 ≈ 8.0 × 0.866 ≈ 6.9 years.', '{"0": "This multiplies by v/c.", "2": "Less time passes on the moving ship.", "3": "This multiplies by γ instead of dividing."}'),
  ('PHY-1016', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Postulates of relativity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 11 Special Theory of Relativity, p. 247 (§11.3)', 'You are in a closed cabin moving at constant velocity, with no view outside. By doing experiments inside, you can:', '["measure the cabin''s speed using a pendulum", "not tell whether the cabin is moving or at rest", "detect the motion because light travels more slowly in a moving cabin", "detect the motion because objects drift backwards"]', 1, 'By the principle of relativity, all laws are the same in every inertial frame, so uniform motion cannot be detected from inside.', '{"0": "A pendulum behaves the same in any inertial frame.", "2": "c is the same in every inertial frame.", "3": "Objects drift only if the cabin accelerates."}'),
  ('PHY-1017', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Structure of the nucleus', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 255 (§12.1)', 'How many neutrons are in the nucleus of uranium-238 (²³⁸₉₂U)?', '["92", "146", "238", "330"]', 1, 'N = A − Z = 238 − 92 = 146.', '{"0": "That is the number of protons (atomic number).", "2": "That is the mass number, the total number of nucleons.", "3": "This adds Z to A instead of subtracting."}'),
  ('PHY-1018', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Structure of the nucleus', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 255 (§12.1)', 'Carbon-12, carbon-13 and carbon-14 are isotopes. Their nuclei have:', '["the same number of neutrons but different numbers of protons", "the same mass number but different atomic numbers", "the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons", "the same number of protons and neutrons"]', 2, 'Isotopes have the same Z (6 for carbon) but different neutron numbers, so different mass numbers.', '{"0": "Then they would be different elements.", "1": "Those are isobars, not isotopes.", "3": "Then they would be the same nuclide."}'),
  ('PHY-1019', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Structure of the nucleus', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 255 (§12.1)', 'One unified atomic mass unit (1 u) is defined as:', '["the mass of one proton", "the mass of one hydrogen atom", "one-twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom", "one-sixteenth of the mass of an oxygen-16 atom"]', 2, 'By definition, 1 u is exactly 1/12 of the mass of a ¹²C atom (≈ 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg).', '{"0": "A proton''s mass is 1.007276 u.", "1": "A hydrogen atom is slightly more than 1 u.", "3": "The modern standard is carbon-12."}'),
  ('PHY-1020', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Structure of the nucleus', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 255 (§12.1)', 'The energy equivalent of 1 u of mass is about:', '["0.511 MeV", "1.02 MeV", "9.31 MeV", "931 MeV"]', 3, 'Using E = mc², 1 u corresponds to about 931 MeV.', '{"0": "That is the rest-mass energy of an electron.", "1": "That is the threshold energy for pair production.", "2": "Check the powers of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-1021', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Structure of the nucleus', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 255 (§12.1)', 'The neutron, an uncharged nucleon with almost the same mass as the proton, was discovered in 1932 by:', '["Ernest Rutherford", "Paul Dirac", "Carl Anderson", "James Chadwick"]', 3, 'James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932.', '{"0": "Rutherford discovered the nucleus and proposed the neutron''s existence, but Chadwick discovered it.", "1": "Dirac predicted antiparticles.", "2": "Anderson discovered the positron."}'),
  ('PHY-1022', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactivity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 259 (§12.4)', 'Natural radioactivity is shown by nuclei that are naturally unstable. According to the textbook, these are nuclei with:', '["atomic numbers less than 20", "equal numbers of protons and neutrons only", "no neutrons", "atomic numbers greater than 82"]', 3, 'Nuclei with Z greater than 82 are found to be naturally unstable and emit α, β and γ radiation spontaneously.', '{"0": "Most light nuclei are stable.", "1": "Many such light nuclei are stable.", "2": "Only ordinary hydrogen has no neutrons, and it is stable."}'),
  ('PHY-1023', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactivity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 259 (§12.4)', 'Making a stable element radioactive by bombarding it with high-energy neutrons, protons or alpha particles is called:', '["artificial radioactivity", "natural radioactivity", "pair production", "annihilation"]', 0, 'Bombardment makes stable nuclei unstable; the radioisotopes produced show artificial radioactivity.', '{"1": "That occurs spontaneously in unstable nuclei.", "2": "That is a gamma photon creating an electron–positron pair.", "3": "That is a particle meeting its antiparticle."}'),
  ('PHY-1024', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactivity', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 259 (§12.4)', 'When α, β and γ radiations pass between two oppositely charged plates:', '["α bends towards the positive plate, β towards the negative plate, γ goes straight", "α bends towards the negative plate, β bends more strongly towards the positive plate, γ goes straight", "all three bend towards the negative plate", "α bends more than β because it carries more charge"]', 1, 'α (positive) goes towards the negative plate; β⁻ (negative, much lighter) bends more, towards the positive plate; γ (no charge) is undeflected.', '{"0": "α is positive and β⁻ is negative; this is reversed.", "2": "γ is uncharged and β⁻ is negative.", "3": "β particles are far lighter, so they bend more."}'),
  ('PHY-1025', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Alpha decay', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 260 (§12.4)', 'Uranium-238 (²³⁸₉₂U) emits an alpha particle. The daughter nucleus is:', '["²³⁴₉₂U", "²³⁸₉₀Th", "²³⁴₉₀Th", "²³⁴₉₁Pa"]', 2, 'α-decay lowers Z by 2 and A by 4: Z = 90 (thorium), A = 234.', '{"0": "Losing two protons changes the element.", "1": "The mass number falls by 4.", "3": "Z falls by 2, not 1."}'),
  ('PHY-1026', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Alpha decay', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 260 (§12.4)', 'An alpha particle is identical to:', '["a helium atom, with two electrons", "a hydrogen nucleus", "a fast electron", "a helium nucleus, with two protons and two neutrons"]', 3, 'An α particle is a ⁴₂He nucleus: 2 protons and 2 neutrons, charge +2e.', '{"0": "The α particle has no electrons.", "1": "That is a single proton.", "2": "That is a β⁻ particle."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1027', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive decay', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 260 (§12.4)', 'A nucleus emits one alpha particle and then two β⁻ particles. Compared with the original nucleus, the final nucleus has:', '["the same atomic number and a mass number 4 less", "an atomic number 2 less and the same mass number", "an atomic number 4 less and a mass number 2 less", "an atomic number 2 more and a mass number 4 less"]', 0, 'α: Z − 2, A − 4. Each β⁻: Z + 1, A unchanged. Net: Z unchanged, A − 4 (an isotope of the original element).', '{"1": "The α decay lowers A by 4.", "2": "These changes are mixed up.", "3": "The two β⁻ decays only cancel the α''s fall in Z."}'),
  ('PHY-1028', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Alpha decay', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 260 (§12.4)', 'The disintegration energy (Q-value) of an alpha decay comes from the fact that:', '["the α particle gains mass as it moves", "the mass of the parent nucleus is greater than the total mass of the daughter nucleus and the α particle", "the daughter nucleus is heavier than the parent", "energy is created from nothing"]', 1, 'The products have less total mass than the parent; the missing mass-energy is released as the Q-value.', '{"0": "Q comes from a mass difference, not from motion.", "2": "The products have less mass.", "3": "The energy comes from the mass difference (E = mc²)."}'),
  ('PHY-1029', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Beta decay', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 260 (§12.4)', 'In β⁻ decay, the emitted electron:', '["is an orbital electron knocked out of the atom", "was stored inside the nucleus before the decay", "is created in the nucleus when a neutron changes into a proton", "comes from a proton changing into a neutron"]', 2, 'In β⁻ decay a neutron becomes a proton, and an electron and an antineutrino are created and emitted.', '{"0": "β particles are not orbital electrons.", "1": "The nucleus contains no electrons; it is created at the moment of emission.", "3": "That is β⁺ decay, which emits a positron."}'),
  ('PHY-1030', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Beta decay', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 261 (§12.4)', 'Sodium-22 (²²₁₁Na) decays by β⁺ (positron) emission. The daughter nucleus is:', '["²²₁₂Mg", "²¹₁₀Ne", "¹⁸₉F", "²²₁₀Ne"]', 3, 'In β⁺ decay a proton becomes a neutron: Z falls by 1 and A stays the same, so ²²₁₁Na → ²²₁₀Ne + e⁺ + ν.', '{"0": "That would result from β⁻ decay.", "1": "The mass number does not change in β decay.", "2": "That would be the result of α decay, which removes 4 nucleons."}'),
  ('PHY-1031', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Beta decay', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 261 (§12.4)', 'The particle emitted together with the positron in β⁺ decay is:', '["a neutrino", "an antineutrino", "a gamma photon only", "a proton"]', 0, 'β⁺: p → n + e⁺ + ν (neutrino). β⁻: n → p + e⁻ + ν̄ (antineutrino).', '{"1": "The antineutrino goes with β⁻ decay.", "2": "A neutrino must be emitted to conserve energy and momentum.", "3": "In β⁺ decay a proton becomes a neutron; no proton is emitted."}'),
  ('PHY-1032', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Beta decay', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 260 (§12.4)', 'A nuclide with too large a neutron-to-proton (N/Z) ratio for stability is most likely to decay by:', '["β⁺ emission", "β⁻ emission", "emitting a neutrino only", "absorbing an electron from outside"]', 1, 'Too many neutrons: a neutron turns into a proton (β⁻), lowering N/Z. Too few neutrons: β⁺ decay.', '{"0": "That happens when N/Z is too small.", "2": "A neutrino is not emitted alone in decay.", "3": "The textbook does not describe this as the result of a high N/Z ratio."}'),
  ('PHY-1033', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Energy of alpha and beta particles', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 262 (§12.4)', 'Alpha particles from a given radionuclide have one sharp energy, but beta particles have a continuous spread of energies from zero to a maximum. This is because:', '["β particles lose energy by collisions inside the nucleus", "β decay does not conserve energy", "in β decay the released energy is shared in varying proportions between the β particle and a (anti)neutrino", "α particles are heavier, so they always have more energy"]', 2, 'Each α carries the same energy, giving a line spectrum. In β decay the electron (or positron) and the (anti)neutrino share the energy, giving a continuous spectrum.', '{"0": "The spread is due to sharing with the neutrino.", "1": "Energy is conserved; the neutrino carries the missing part.", "3": "Mass alone does not explain the continuous β spectrum."}'),
  ('PHY-1034', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Beta decay', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 261 (§12.4)', 'The neutrino was first proposed in order to:', '["explain the deflection of alpha particles", "explain why gamma rays have no charge", "explain the stability of the nucleus", "preserve the conservation of energy in beta decay"]', 3, 'The continuous β spectrum seemed to break energy conservation; the neutrino carries off the missing energy. It was detected later in a nuclear reactor.', '{"0": "That is explained by their charge.", "1": "That needs no new particle.", "2": "That is the role of the strong nuclear force."}'),
  ('PHY-1035', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Gamma radiation', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 262 (§12.4)', 'When a nucleus emits a gamma ray:', '["its atomic number and mass number stay the same as it drops from an excited state to a lower energy state", "its atomic number falls by 2", "its mass number falls by 4", "it changes into a different element"]', 0, 'ᴬ_ZX* → ᴬ_ZX + γ: the excited nucleus loses energy as an EM photon, with Z and A unchanged.', '{"1": "γ rays carry no charge.", "2": "γ rays carry no nucleons.", "3": "The element is unchanged."}'),
  ('PHY-1036', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactivity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 262 (§12.4)', 'In Table 12.2 of the textbook, which isotope is given as a typical source of gamma rays?', '["Radon-222", "Cobalt-60", "Uranium-238", "Carbon-12"]', 1, 'The table lists radon-222 as an α source, a strontium isotope as a β source, and cobalt-60 as a γ source.', '{"0": "The table gives it as an α source.", "2": "The table does not list it as the γ source.", "3": "Carbon-12 is stable."}'),
  ('PHY-1037', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactivity', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 260 (§12.4)', 'The change of one element into another during radioactive decay, such as radium becoming radon, is called:', '["annihilation", "pair production", "transmutation", "ionization"]', 2, 'When the daughter nucleus is a different element from the parent, the process is called transmutation of elements.', '{"0": "That is a particle meeting its antiparticle.", "1": "That creates a particle–antiparticle pair from a photon.", "3": "That is the removal of electrons from atoms."}'),
  ('PHY-1038', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Fundamental forces', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 256 (§12.2)', 'Which fundamental force is the weakest, yet has an infinite (long) range?', '["The strong nuclear force", "The weak nuclear force", "The electromagnetic force", "Gravity"]', 3, 'Gravity is the weakest of the four forces but acts over unlimited distances.', '{"0": "It is the strongest and short-ranged.", "1": "It is short-ranged.", "2": "It is far stronger than gravity."}'),
  ('PHY-1039', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Fundamental forces', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 257 (§12.2)', 'The fundamental force that is responsible for beta decay and can change the identity of particles is:', '["the weak nuclear force", "the strong nuclear force", "the electromagnetic force", "gravity"]', 0, 'The weak nuclear force drives β decay and neutrino interactions, changing, for example, a down quark into an up quark.', '{"1": "It binds nucleons and quarks but does not cause β decay.", "2": "It acts between charges and cannot change a neutron into a proton.", "3": "Gravity is negligible at nuclear scales."}'),
  ('PHY-1040', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Fundamental forces', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 256 (§12.2)', 'The scientist whose four equations unified electricity and magnetism into electromagnetism was:', '["Isaac Newton", "James Clerk Maxwell", "Albert Einstein", "Michael Faraday"]', 1, 'Maxwell''s equations show that electric and magnetic fields are two aspects of one electromagnetic force.', '{"0": "Newton gave the law of gravitation.", "2": "Einstein developed relativity.", "3": "Faraday discovered induction; Maxwell wrote the unifying equations."}'),
  ('PHY-1041', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Pair production', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 258 (§12.3)', 'The minimum energy a gamma-ray photon needs to produce an electron–positron pair is:', '["0.511 MeV", "1.02 MeV", "2.04 MeV", "931 MeV"]', 1, 'The photon must supply the rest-mass energy of both particles: 2mₑc² = 2 × 0.511 = 1.02 MeV.', '{"0": "That is enough for only one of the two particles.", "2": "This counts each rest-mass energy twice.", "3": "That is the energy equivalent of 1 u."}'),
  ('PHY-1042', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Pair production', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 258 (§12.3)', 'Pair production can only happen near an atomic nucleus. The nucleus is needed to:', '["supply the charge of the positron", "supply the mass of the electron", "conserve linear momentum, since it takes up some recoil", "slow the photon down"]', 2, 'A photon alone cannot create a pair while conserving both energy and momentum; the nearby nucleus recoils to balance the momentum.', '{"0": "Charge is conserved because the pair has zero net charge.", "1": "The mass comes from the photon''s energy.", "3": "A photon always travels at c."}'),
  ('PHY-1043', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Pair production', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 258 (§12.3)', 'A gamma photon of energy 1.50 MeV produces an electron–positron pair near a nucleus. The total kinetic energy of the pair is about:', '["0.48 MeV", "0.99 MeV", "1.02 MeV", "1.50 MeV"]', 0, 'KE = 1.50 − 1.02 = 0.48 MeV (ignoring the small recoil energy of the nucleus).', '{"1": "This subtracts only one rest-mass energy.", "2": "That is the energy used to make the rest masses.", "3": "Some of the energy becomes rest mass."}'),
  ('PHY-1044', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Annihilation', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 258 (§12.3)', 'An electron and a positron, both almost at rest, annihilate. They produce:', '["one gamma photon of 1.02 MeV", "two gamma photons, each of about 1.02 MeV", "a neutron and a proton", "two gamma photons, each of about 0.51 MeV"]', 3, 'e⁺ + e⁻ → γ + γ; each photon carries mₑc² ≈ 0.51 MeV, and opposite directions conserve momentum.', '{"0": "A single photon could not conserve momentum.", "1": "The total available is only 1.02 MeV.", "2": "Annihilation of e⁺e⁻ gives photons."}'),
  ('PHY-1045', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Matter and anti-matter', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 257 (§12.3)', 'Compared with its particle, an antiparticle has:', '["the same rest mass but opposite charge", "the opposite mass and the same charge", "a larger mass and the same charge", "no mass and no charge"]', 0, 'Antiparticles have the same rest mass as their particles but opposite charge (and magnetic moment).', '{"1": "Mass is never negative.", "2": "The masses are equal.", "3": "An antiparticle has the same mass as its particle."}'),
  ('PHY-1046', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Fundamental particles', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 263 (§12.5)', 'Particles with half-integer spin (such as electrons, protons and neutrons), which obey Pauli''s exclusion principle, are called:', '["bosons", "fermions", "mesons", "photons"]', 1, 'Half-spin particles obey Pauli''s exclusion principle and are fermions; zero- or integer-spin particles are bosons.', '{"0": "Bosons have zero or whole-number spin.", "2": "Mesons are quark–antiquark hadrons with integer spin.", "3": "A photon has spin 1 and is a boson."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-1047', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Hadrons and leptons', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 264 (§12.5)', 'Which of these particles is a lepton?', '["Pion", "Proton", "Muon", "Kaon"]', 2, 'Leptons (electron, muon, tau and their neutrinos) do not feel the strong force.', '{"0": "A pion is a meson, a hadron.", "1": "A proton is a baryon, a hadron.", "3": "A kaon is a meson, a hadron."}'),
  ('PHY-1048', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Hadrons and leptons', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 264 (§12.5)', 'A meson is made of:', '["three quarks", "three antiquarks", "two leptons", "one quark and one antiquark"]', 3, 'Mesons (such as pions) are quark–antiquark pairs; baryons (such as protons) are three quarks.', '{"0": "That is a baryon.", "1": "That is an antibaryon.", "2": "Mesons are hadrons, made of quarks."}'),
  ('PHY-1049', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Quarks', 'hard', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 265 (§12.6)', 'The up quark has charge +2/3 e, and the down and strange quarks each have −1/3 e. A baryon made of the quarks u, d and s has a charge of:', '["0", "+1 e", "−1 e", "+2/3 e"]', 0, 'q = +2/3 − 1/3 − 1/3 = 0 (this is the neutral lambda particle).', '{"1": "That is the charge of uud (a proton).", "2": "That would be dds or similar.", "3": "This counts only the up quark."}'),
  ('PHY-1050', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Quarks', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 265 (§12.6)', 'The charge of an anti-up quark is:', '["+2/3 e", "−2/3 e", "−1/3 e", "+1/3 e"]', 1, 'An antiquark has the opposite charge of its quark: ū has −2/3 e.', '{"0": "That is the up quark.", "2": "That is the down quark.", "3": "That is the anti-down quark."}'),
  ('PHY-1051', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Quarks', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 264 (§12.6)', 'The quark model was proposed in 1964 by:', '["Paul Dirac and Carl Anderson", "Peter Higgs and Albert Einstein", "Murray Gell-Mann and George Zweig", "James Chadwick and Ernest Rutherford"]', 2, 'In 1964 Gell-Mann and Zweig proposed that hadrons are made of quarks.', '{"0": "They are linked with antiparticles.", "1": "Higgs proposed the Higgs field; Einstein gave relativity.", "3": "They are linked with the neutron and the nucleus."}'),
  ('PHY-1052', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Quarks', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 264 (§12.6)', 'Which of these is NOT one of the six quark flavours?', '["Charm", "Bottom", "Strange", "Muon"]', 3, 'The six flavours are up, down, strange, charm, bottom and top. The muon is a lepton.', '{"0": "Charm is a quark flavour.", "1": "Bottom is a quark flavour.", "2": "Strange is a quark flavour."}'),
  ('PHY-1053', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Quarks', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 266 (§12.6)', 'In terms of quarks, β⁻ decay of a neutron happens because:', '["a down quark changes into an up quark, emitting an electron and an antineutrino", "an up quark changes into a down quark, emitting a positron", "a quark is knocked out of the neutron", "the neutron gains a quark"]', 0, 'udd → uud: d → u + e⁻ + ν̄, which turns the neutron into a proton.', '{"1": "That is β⁺ decay.", "2": "Quarks are never seen on their own.", "3": "The number of quarks stays three."}'),
  ('PHY-1054', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Quarks', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 266 (§12.6)', 'The theory of the strong (colour) force between quarks is called:', '["quantum electrodynamics", "quantum chromodynamics (QCD)", "string theory", "the general theory of relativity"]', 1, 'Quarks carry colour charge (red, green, blue). The force between colour charges is described by quantum chromodynamics.', '{"0": "That is the theory of the electromagnetic force.", "2": "String theory is a proposed theory of everything, not the theory of the colour force.", "3": "That is the theory of gravity."}'),
  ('PHY-1055', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Quarks', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 265 (§12.6)', 'A proton is "colourless" because:', '["all its quarks are red", "quarks have no colour charge", "its three quarks carry one each of the three colours, red, green and blue", "it contains a quark and its antiquark"]', 2, 'A baryon has one quark of each colour, which together make "white" (colourless).', '{"0": "Three quarks of one colour would not be colourless.", "1": "Quarks do carry colour charge.", "3": "That is how mesons are colourless; a proton has three quarks."}'),
  ('PHY-1056', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Higgs boson', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 266 (§12.7)', 'The Higgs boson was discovered in 2012 at the Large Hadron Collider at:', '["LIGO", "NASA", "Virgo", "CERN"]', 3, 'The Higgs boson, with a mass of about 125 GeV/c², was found at CERN''s Large Hadron Collider in 2012.', '{"0": "LIGO detects gravitational waves.", "1": "NASA is the US space agency.", "2": "Virgo is a gravitational-wave observatory."}'),
  ('PHY-1057', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Exchange particles', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 266 (§12.7)', 'The exchange particles (force carriers) of the weak nuclear force are:', '["the W⁺, W⁻ and Z bosons", "gluons", "photons", "gravitons"]', 0, 'W⁺, W⁻ and Z bosons carry the weak force; they get large masses through the Higgs field.', '{"1": "Gluons carry the strong force.", "2": "Photons carry the electromagnetic force.", "3": "Gravitons are the proposed carriers of gravity."}'),
  ('PHY-1058', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Higgs boson', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 266 (§12.7)', 'Photons have zero rest mass. According to the textbook, this is because:', '["they interact strongly with the Higgs field", "they do not interact with the Higgs field", "they travel more slowly than light", "they are made of quarks"]', 1, 'Particles get mass by interacting with the Higgs field; photons do not, so their rest mass is zero.', '{"0": "Strong interaction with the Higgs field gives a large mass, as for W and Z bosons.", "2": "Photons travel at c.", "3": "Photons are not made of quarks."}'),
  ('PHY-1059', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Fundamental particles', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 263 (§12.5)', 'When a pion collides with a proton, "strange particles" such as a kaon and a lambda are produced. Such strange particles are always produced:', '["singly", "only in beta decay", "in pairs", "only from gamma rays"]', 2, 'π⁻ + p → K⁰ + Λ⁰: strange particles are always created in pairs.', '{"0": "The book stresses that they always appear in pairs.", "1": "They are produced in high-energy collisions.", "3": "The example given is a pion–proton collision."}'),
  ('PHY-1060', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Universe', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 267 (§12.10)', 'According to the textbook, the share of the universe made of ordinary matter (atoms) is only about:', '["5%", "27%", "68%", "95%"]', 0, 'Ordinary matter ≈ 5%, dark matter ≈ 27% and dark energy ≈ 68%.', '{"1": "That is dark matter.", "2": "That is dark energy.", "3": "That is dark matter and dark energy together."}'),
  ('PHY-1061', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Universe', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 268 (§12.10)', 'Most of the ordinary matter in the observable universe exists as:', '["solid", "liquid", "neutral gas at room temperature", "plasma"]', 3, 'Most known matter is hydrogen and helium in stars, which is almost entirely plasma.', '{"0": "Solids are rare on a cosmic scale.", "1": "Liquids are rare on a cosmic scale.", "2": "Hydrogen and helium in stars are largely ionized."}'),
  ('PHY-1062', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Theories of forces', 'easy', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 268 (§12.11)', 'String theory proposes that fundamental particles are:', '["tiny vibrating strings, whose vibrations give the particles their properties", "point particles with no size", "made of quarks and leptons", "waves in an electric field only"]', 0, 'In string theory, open or closed vibrating strings replace point particles. It is still unproved experimentally.', '{"1": "String theory replaces point particles with strings.", "2": "Quarks and leptons are themselves the fundamental particles.", "3": "That is not string theory."}'),
  ('PHY-1063', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Standard model', 'moderate', 'Physics 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 12 Nuclear and Particle Physics, p. 269 (§12.12)', 'Which of these can the Standard Model of particle physics NOT yet explain?', '["The electromagnetic force", "Gravity", "The strong nuclear force", "How particles get mass"]', 1, 'The Standard Model covers the electromagnetic, strong and weak forces and the Higgs boson, but not gravity, dark matter or dark energy.', '{"0": "It is part of the Standard Model.", "2": "It is part of the Standard Model.", "3": "The Higgs mechanism in the Standard Model explains this."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

